        <p>BATE'S SALVE. For Inetantly Relltving Pain and CURING ABS. ESSES, BAD BREASTS, BOILS. BURN". SCALDS, and all SORES., NOTHING TOUCHES BATE'S SALVE, Md. and 13id . Poet Free from BATES. 1, REGENT SQUARE, LONDON, W.C. Please mention paper,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Y 0 U CDO U GALLS PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR GROCER FOR</head>
        <p>Y 0 U CDO U GALLS PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR GROCER FOR</p>
        <p>" CAt'ITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>Inspector Quinn is a Louden individuality, very active, and much in evidence just now. He is Mr. Melville's right hand man. He has been in the mak- ing of some history, for it was he who was sent after Pigott. when that hapless personage fled to Spain after the famous scenes in the Parnell Com- mission Court. Inspector .Quinn tracked Pigott to his hotel,and was just in time to hear the shot that ended his chequered existence.</p>
        <p>One of the first things which the County Coun- cil will do when it resumes its sittings next month will be to choose a successor to Mr. Simonds as chief of the Fire Brigade. The candidates are of a somewhat similar class to those who competed for the clerkship of the Council. Beyond four or five candidates who are at the head of provincial fire brigades, most of the applicants are impossible. Many of them are retired military men, whose ex- perience of "fire" has not been of the kind required for the director of the Metropolitan Fire Brigade. Others have only been members of volunteer fire brigades in small towns. Whatever the difficulty of making a selection from the sixty applications received, it will be easy enough to weed out the bulk of the candidates.</p>
        <p>The Bishops are cycling, and the Deans, and the Minor Canons. At Ripon, at all events. Bishop, Dean,and Chapter disport themselves on the wheel, without any misgivings about their dignity. We understand that the warning of the " Spectator" to Bishops who may be tempted to pat their feet up when "coasting,' that this is inconsistent with their sacred mission.bas made no impression what- ever on his Grace of Ripon.</p>
        <p>The difficulties in the way of Tynan's extradition are not believed by the authorities to be insur- mountable, and it is thought that the reputed citi- zenship in America will not '-we satisfactorily estab- lished. According to French law, however, a crim- inal is not amenable to justice if ten years have ex- pired since the last ark de pursuits,. Owing to the lapse of time since the Phoenix Park murders the questien as to what amounts to an axle de pursuite will have to be determined, and it remains to be seen whether the taking out of a fresh warrant may not be sufficient to meet the requirements of the law in this respect. That the police received the information of the plot from someone associated with the conspirators is confidently affirmed, and it is not improbable that we may yet have information as to who played the part of what is commonly called a " policeman's nose."</p>
        <p>The proceedings against Mr. Jerome Y. Jerome have failed, as they fieserved to fail. Mr. Jerome has been singularly outspoken in regard to the sultan; but he voiced what most of us have felt. It must be a source of satisfaction to him to know that he has pricked Abdul Hamid in a tender place. His language was undeniably strong. The inter- pretation placed upon it at the Turkish Embassy was that Mr. Jerome advocated assassination. 'rho Foreign Office was promptly requested to procure a prosecution. I understand that the only step taken by the officials was to inform Mr. Jerome of this re- quest. There they left the matter. The lurkish himbassy made a fresh effort before it was realised that the co-operation of the Foreign Office was hopeless. Then the project was dropped.</p>
        <p>Portsmouth is already all agog in anticipation of the embarkation of the Emperor of Ru.isia. The inhabitants are piously hoping for unsettled weather in the Channel, so that their Imperial Majesties may be induced to accept the hospitality of the town for a few hours. The naval display will not be large, though it will be imposing. It was said in town that Mr. Alfred Austin had been put on his mettle by the news that the French poets were hare at work on odes in honour of the Emperor. It is fervently to be wished that the report is untrue.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladstone has. I understand, been inundated with lettars of a congratulatory character as the result of his Liverpool speech. So far his health has not suffered appreciably from the effort. A friend tells me that he is full of literary projects, but is already looking forward to his winter holi- day abroad. And no wonder! The weather round Hawardon has been as atrocious and trying as elsewhere.</p>
        <p>Cardinal Vaughan will not relish Mr. Purcell's plain-spoken vindication of his "Life of Manning." Referring to his emient critics, Mr. Purcell inci- dentally remarks that "many for a time lost their heads, or their tempers, or both." And, again, he playfully warns the Cardinal against posing as an " introverted Athanasius." Mr. Purcell has very astutely assured himself of the friendly attitude of the Pope before replying, and in Catholic opinion he has made his case wellnigh impregnable. " The Life of Cardinal Manning„' by the way,has attained a circulation in England, America, and the colonies of two hundred thousand. For a high-priced two- volume biography this may he said to be phe- nomenal. Froude's " Carlyle" did not do better.</p>
        <p>"Bell," or" Ivory," seeme to be regarded as an impertant catch by Scotland Yard, if one can judge from the special efforts made to prevent the pos- sibility of his escape from custody. He is lodged in a special cell at Holloway. He is kept under obvoreation day and night When the next journey to Bow-street takes place he will not only have the usual escort in uniform, but he will also be watched by detectives scattered along the route.</p>
        <p>A friend who landed in London from Cairo a day or two ago. tells me of an exceedingly improbable piece of news which was going the rounds before he left. It is to the effect that the Anglo-Egyptian authorities have endeavoured to negotiate a loan of £l,OOO 000 from the chief banking establishments tor the purpose of helping to defray the cost cl the Dongola expedition,and also to pay part of the cost of an advance on Khartoum. This is, of course, wildly improbable. There is no security to offer for the money. And banking establistments are not benevolent enough to lot Lord Salisbury finger their hard cash without even an I 0 U in exchange. From the same informant I. learn that Cairo is by no means a safe pine just now. Sedition and dis- content are so rife that those who know about it have got the unpleasant impression that. they are sitting on a powder magszine My friend thirike it extremely likely that this it he true reason for the mysterious return of the Staffordshire Regiment to Cairo.</p>
        <p>It is noteworthy that the Ministerial organs are extremely anxious at the present time to explain that t.':e capture of Dnngola practically ends the Soudan expedition for the present, and that there is no reason for hurry in carrying out, the remain- ing objects of the expedition the dervishes are demoralised and in flight, and therefore Sir Herbert Kitchener can afford to rest on his laurels. It is hardly necessary to say that if only military reasons were concerned this argument would be positively absurd. If the enemy are dispirited and without stores and ammunition, now would he the time to follow them up—instead of eivinq them time to rally, as they certainly will do,—and thus to secure the real objects of the expedition with the mini- mum of bloodshed and risk. But it is an open secret that military reasons are not uppermost.</p>
        <p>It is just possible that the stoppage of the ex- pedition may have some international bearing, but the chief reasons. are undoubtedly financial. Mr. Chamberlain's principle of conducting the cam- paign on the system of "limited liability" has, in fact, been put into force. In .spite of the extraor- dinary economy which has been practised, a Rood deal more money has been spent already than bad been provided for, and sooner or later the British taxpayer will find this out to his cost. Sir .11.1 lick- s is not the kind of Chancellor of the Exche- quer to look on a prospect of this sort with indiffer- ence,and it is pretty safe to say that the "rest and be thankful" view of the situation which is taken at the present time has less to do with the opinions of the military advisers of the Khedive than with the scruples of the British Treasury. The manner in which the North Staffordshire Regiment is being hurried back to Cairo is pretty eloquent as to the real state of thins. That the Government will have to come to Parliament for money next session ap- pears to be beyond all question.</p>
      </div>
