      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>trim great objection to most of the fine writing of the present day is that it isn't fine reading. Crincsx may be as good fish in the sea as were ever c aught, but that is no satisfaction to the man 'whose bait is gone. "SOME are born groat ; " and in nearly every case it was the only chance they had. CLARA : "Mr. Nicefello said my face was classic. What is classic !" Dora: "Oh' most anything old."</p>
        <p>PARENT AND OFFSPRING.—Mamma "What are you playing with, Essie ?" Essie : "A caterpillar an' two little kittenpillars." A MEAN QUESTION: Dobzin : "Are you aware, old man, that I'm about to join a dramatic com- pany?" Doozey : " Yes ; but for what pur- pose?" UNDER CONSIDERATION. Mrs. Cobwigger : "Now, Freddy, if you're not a good boy, I'll send you to bed without any dinner." Freddy: "Say, ma, what are we going to have for dinner?" "You probably think you're a fine piece of furniture," observed the chair-mender, removing his coat and getting to work, "but I am going to take the cane seat, out of you." "IF I had known," sobbed young Mrs- Fits "that you would be such a brute to poor Fido, I would never have married you." "My dear" re- plied Mr. Fits, "the anticipation of kicking that miserabie little beast was one of my chief reasons</p>
        <p>for proposing to you." "MAMMA, do you like to hear stories?" she began. "Yes, my child." "Shall I tell you -one?" "Yes." "Will you be much interested?" "Certainly." " nut it is not a long story." "Never mind. Tell it to me." " Well, there was once upon a time an—eau-de-cologne bottle— and I broke it!" Mr. FERRY: "Greatness is all comparative. For example, an elephant four feet high would be called a little thing, while a rat that size"— Mrs. Ferry: "Yes, and twenty-five shillings for a bonnet is an enormous expense, but it isn't any- thing at all when you lose it at cards." His EXACT POSITION. —First Citizen: "Is it __ _</p>
        <p>true that you said you didn't believe in the Monroe doctrine?" Second Citizen : "Nonsense ! Of course I believe in it. I only said that I don't know just what it is." His FAVOURITE. Weary Raggles : "Ever read any of Howells' stories?" Wandering Willie: "Naw. Never heard tell of him afore." Weary Raggles : "Why, he's the great novelist of all time. His hero is always a feller what never does nuthin'." FIRST MESSENGER BOY (excitedly) : Here, come into this show and see the nigger dance on a box an' turn summersets widout losin' step." Second Messenger Boy (solemnly) : "I've got a message for a doctor—case of life and death—and can't stop more'n a half-hour or so." "THE trouble with this tooth," said the dentist, "is</p>
        <p>probing it with a long, slender instrument, that the nerve is dying." "It seems to me, doctor," groaned the victim, "you ought to treat the dying with little more respect." A POSSIBLE OPPORTITNITY.—WiIIie : "I know sister would be glad to go skating with you." Rinway : "What makes you think so?" She says she has been dying all winter to have you break the ice." _ _</p>
        <p>EXCHANGE Or CONFIDENCE. —Mr. Jinks : "I don't know how you will feel about it, sir, but the fact is that my wife, your daughter, is a dread- fully hard woman to live with." Mr. Blinks; "I can sympathise with you, sir. I married her mother."</p>
        <p>Too Mcrcm—Banns : "You didn't know that I was once engaged to marry your wife, did you ? " Danns : "No ; who broke the engagement?" Banns: "I did." Danns : " Did—eh ? " (Thrashes him within an inch of his life). "There, now; if •cu ever play a trick on me like that again, I'll kill you the next time!" No REPLIES PERMITTED.—Wife: "You saw Mrs. Browner last evening?" Husband: "Yes, but not to speak to." Wife: "What a story! They tell me you were sitting with her for more than two hours." Husband: "True; but it was she who did the talking." NOT TO FORGET.—Jones : "Why have you that string tied about your finger?" Smith: "So I wouldn't forget an errand of my wife's." "And what's the handkerchief tied round your arm for ? " "So I wouldn't forget the string." "And what did she want you to do ?" "I've been trying to think of it for the last hour." DIAMOND CUT DIAMOND.—" I've had proof that a diamond is the hardest of all subitances." "How's that ? " "You know Marguerite Wilkes?" "The girl with the heart of porphyry?" "Yes." " Well ? " "I took a diamond round to her house last night, and her heart simply softened before it." CITIZEN: "Why are you in this condition ? " Tramp: "It is not long to tell, sir. I can't live ith my wife." Citizen (tilled with sympathy, because he had just had a quarrel with his own) : "Poor man ! I deeply feel for you. Here's 84- pence. Tell me why you can't live with your wife?" "Because I have'nt got one. Ta !ta !" A REFORMED METHOD.—" Ha, ha !" laughed the murderer, as he raised his gleaming dagger aloft—" ha, ha !" It did not escape him that his victim shuddered. "Ha, ha ! " he proceeded; "you will observe that lam merry. Most people take life too seriously." A DANGER AVERTED.—A tramp was arrested, taken before an Australian judge, and sentenced to three months. The judge, in explaining the sen- tence, remarked that while there was no evidence that the prisoner had been guilty of any crime, he thought it prudent to commit him, as he had the wild, haggard look of a man about to start a news- paper. MRS. QUERREL sat looking into the fire. Pre- sently she chuckled a little bit. "What are you giggling at?" inquired Mr. Querrel. "I was thinking of the time when you proposed. You told rue to say the one little word that would make tou happy for life." "Yes, I remember. And you said the wrong word." No ALLIGATORS.—A naval officer wishing to bathe in a Ceylon river, asked a native to show him a place where there were no alligators. The native took him to a pool close to the estuary. The officer enjoyed his dip. While drying himself, he asked his guide why there were never any alliga- tors in that pool. "Because, sah," the Cingalese replied, "they plenty 'f raid of shark !" THE AGE OF NEW VANITIES.—" Prisoner at the bar," demanded the judge, sternly, "have you anything to say why the sentence of the law should not be passed upon you?" "No, indeed, your honour," replied the prisoner, bowing and smiling pleasantly; "I've had a delightful trial, and the newspapers have very kindly given roe seventeen columns more than any murderer has got this year. Don't let me detain von.</p>
