      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>For years past it has been gravely asserted that capes have had their day, and would soon cease to be, as far as fashion was concerned; but despite the prophecy, these cosy and often very becoming garments come gaily to the front again as soon ag chill autumn winds begin to blow.</p>
        <p>A very fa.'hionnble espe is in grass lawn, with embroidery in st:quins and heads, and full frills of ckJarse Wad; lthe, with kilt d satin bows. A hat g,4) wlm thl capo ipt in Panama straw, with a Tamim o' Shanier crown, trimmed with gauze ribb,n, and with ceriso bows of black quills placed Si t he side.</p>
        <p>All the nicest autumn capes are of a sensible eic h, reaeloi,g well bekw toe waist, and thereby nth,' din.; p:, o y of pro, e,..q ion to the arms. A nice cape Is of fawn beaver cloth, finished all wi!ii five rows of machine stitching, and v. a .voe. .pnis, and rituhiately high Medici of ,•iol h, r.rtilt d wish tine black and ur I.ra.d. The clout is the thick beaver that r•fpnres iii iining, and that is being used for all the newest, esp., of the kind just now ; and the bui tons, shich are rather a special feature on coat,, and gowns this season, are of filigree work in oxidised silver. Fur lined cape will be very generally worn during the next few months. Some or the prettiest are of a nice medium length, and wade in thin beaver cloth ; sometimes plain, hut :wire often finely braided ; the lining being of ~.plifrel in the high:tr priced, and of rabbit fur in the cheaper ones. Fawn, light tan, bronze, and nip tie-green are the favourite colours, and nearly all have the inedified Medici collar, which makes one so snug round the throat.</p>
        <p>Of course, women who are endowed with straight, slender figures never look better than when attired in a well-cut coat; but the coats must be beauti- fully made, with that indefinable air about them that we now term smart. A successful coat requires to be built by an experienced tailor, whether it is bought ready-made or built to order; whereas a stylish cape is far less difficult of achievement, and can be made at home with the aid of a good pattern. A useful coat and costume for ordinary winter wear is of navy cheviot serge, adorned by elegant revers faced by white velvet moirii, edged by a double cord of silver and cornflower blue, and flanked down the fronts by cut steel buttons. The coat is daintily lined with white glace, and really constitutes a very charming model, though I should like to add, from an utilitarian point of view, that this costume is very serviceable and looks very nice with dark linings.</p>
        <p>A good cycling hat has a coarse crown and a brim of fine chip, with trimmings of black satin ribbon and quills. An exceedingly becoming and stylish hat for a young girl is of fine white chip straw. It is trimmed with a wreath of lovely pink roses and a smart bow of sedge grass. Two rosettes—one of green, the other of white tulle—are placed under the brim at the back. Several prettily trimmed hats in the ever-popular sailor shape are also on view. One, with dark green fancy straw brim, and white straw crown, has puffs of green over white tulle round the crown, interspersed with rosettes of critn.ion silk poppies. It is too early for winter costumes, and the little ones require warm wraps to cover their frocks. The sacque paletot is very adaptable, since later on it may be trimmed with fur, and will be quite cosy over the winter garments. For present wear, the trimming consists of string-coloured lawn and appliqué, which is not in the least expensive, and has a very pretty effect. The material is Dutch frieze, fifty inches wide, and this is thick, and requires no lining whateveL. Your loving JENN-Da.</p>
        <p>Adepts in palmistry assert that the length of the fourth or little finger, ii the most important sign in the hand. Without a straight, long little finger, no man or woman ever rises to importance in any line of life 1 With the long spatulat€ little finger, success in business will re- sult, while the long pointed one denotes tact and the minagement of people.</p>
