      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Till 6 EXAMINER, SATURDAY OCTOBER 3. 1896</head>
        <p>Till 6 EXAMINER, SATURDAY OCTOBER 3. 1896</p>
        <p>" WOME3'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The Czarina up to the time of her marriage being used to the German Lutheran service, which is closely akin, at least in eutward seeming, to that of the Scottish Established Church, probably felt herself quite at home in the parish church of Crathie on Sunday last. Ile Czar, on the other hand, could not but ho struck with the simplicity of the service, as compared!with the gorgeous Eastern ritual to which he has been accustomed. It is almost certain that this is the first occasion on which a Russian sovereign has attended a Presbyterian service, and there is seraething touching in the idea of the Queen and all the leading members of Britain's Ro. al Family worshipping God side by side with the rulers of that other mighty empire in the little wayside church of Crathie. A gathering under such circum- stances and amid such scenes, could be witnessed perhaps in no other country but this, and here only once in a lifetime. The little item of news that has lately been going the round of the papers,to the effect that Her Mijesty never uses any other walking stick save one which was origin- ally made for and presented to Charles II by a loyal citizen of Worcester, who secured it from a famous oak tree, is scmewhat incorrect. The Queen is in the ha bit of using a black bamboo, with a crutch handle, which was a gift to Her Majesty from Princess Henry of Battenberg. Despite the cold damp weather, the Queen has not once entered a close vehicle. Thie is partly because Her Majesty prefers a low temperature and partly because her health would suffer if she. veers not able to i•Jend some part of each day in a smart rusk of cold air. It is for this reason that the Royal Coachman drives so fast. The Queen's owls personal apartments are always five degrees cooler than those used by the Court at large. Before her marriage with the Marquis de Caux, Madame Patti never received more than £l2O for each performance ; now when she sings in London else gets £BOO. It has been estimated that Mr. Abbey alone paid Madame Patti £200,000. During the last tea years bee average earnings have been £40,000 per annum. Per- haps one of the best paid artists in France was the late Mr. Duprez who in his prime, received ova: £35,000 a year. Two hundred and seventy-four years ago there was only one newspaper, and that was issued under the title if " The Certain News if the Present Week." It seems almost impossible in these slays, when the difficulty is to make a choice of the newspapers offered to Us, to imagine a time when there was only one newspaper to be had, for love or money. The first " Sunday " news- paper was not published until 1780, when the fist num- ber of the" British Gazette and Sunday Monitor," printed by a man named Johnson,proved such a success that In a short time he had a number of rivals compet- ing with him on the same lines. It appears however, that as far back as the year 1476, there were nulls en• gaged in the printing trade. The sisters of the Convent of St. James at Mount Rijioli who had for more than two centuries excelled in the art of copying and illumi- nating manuscripts,learnt the new art,and so industrious were they that in eight years more than 100 works were issued from the conventual press. The gross amount of paper manufactured every year is estimated at 70,904,000,000 quires. (he beolc pub- lishers absorb half the total, while the newspapers con- sume about 600,000,000. Great Britain is at the head of the list as far as paper consuming is concerned. We dispose on an average of 114 quires per bead. America comes next with 104,then Germany and then France. By the way,all the paper known before the thirteenth century was apparently made from cotton, not linen. Speaking of the manufacture if paper reminds nee of a new preparation of paper pulp made in Germany and called " papvrolith." It is in the shape of a dry powder which is mixed with water. This dries in a short time and may be spread and planed shown upon a foundation of either atone, cement, or w od. It is said to be solid, without a crevice,deadening to all noise,fireproof,and to wear especially well. At length we are forced to recognise the fact that our pretty thin blouses must be cast aside and something warmer and more seasonable procured to take their place. Some of the new flannelettes would make up into fascinating little blouses for morning, or even after- noon wear at home, and moreover possess the decided merit if costing but a few pence. Most girls .possess a black serge skirt and ttose possessed also of nimble fin- gers and three yards of a bright red and black checked flannelette, could turn out an inexpeneive costume and be quite up to date too. Care must be :taken however that the colour of the skirt is in some way repeated in the blottse,and where possihlo the waist belt should blend the two into " one harmonious whole." L favourite combination of color just now is red and brown, and the ordinary brown coat and skirt may be easily rendered attractive by a smart red silk shirt. I have nctice several women wearing bright red hats to match these shirts, but the effect is not quite all one could desire. Red is distinctly the favoured calor for accessory. There is tomato red, geranium red,poppy or peony red. This revival has caught on immensely, but it is poseihle that it may become somewhat " too popu• lar " later en for a minority.</p>
        <p>t- Flannelette lingerie is appearing and will be an un- speakable comfort to chilly mortals during the cold weather. Some of the designs are remarkably pretty. pink rosebuds on a cream ground being one of the most effective. ritick torchon lace is the best trimming for lannelette, as it washes and wears excellently. There is no doubt that serge and tweed knicker- bockers, built with a removable lining of flannel, are most comfortable for walking, especially in cold,winclY, weather, but a short skirt should be worn over them, other wise the gown does net hang well, and clings un- comfortably round the legs. Those of my readers who have hitherto indulged in the old-fashioned flannel pet- ticoat will only need to try the knickerbockers I have described (lace, ere they make up their minds to wear them always. From time to time we are reminded by a summons against some hapless individual who has let his dog go out without a muz-Lle, that the regulation which has up to the present, resulted in the extermination of some 40,000 dogs, is still in force. London of course is not Constantinople. but a good many people will be sur- prised to learn how many dogs we can ice e by the opera- tion of the lethal chamber, without thlticing the differ- ence. One would think the destructien of from 100 to 150 a day must perceptibly thin the numbers towards the point of exhaustion, nevertheless the holocaust still goes on, and it is expected that at the present rate of progress the reamber will reach 60,000 before the end of the year. Last year the total number of dogs destroyed was about 24.000, and that was 3,000 or 4,000 above the year preceding. Those of us who prefer lavender bags to place amongst our linen and clathes generally, should remetn- ber that the lavender must be thoroughly dried before it is put into bags. Moreover it requires frequent shakings, or a thorough stirring up to keep the perfume strong and in good tone, is it is a quality that is liable to grow faint if left quiet with never any rousings. Blackberry Jam.—Pick the blackberries and remove any leaves or stalks. Have ready, pare], cored, and sliced, ahnet half the weight of good cooking apples,that there is of blackberries. Rinse an enamel stewpan and put in the apples, let them cook to a pulp, then add the blackberries, and cook till soft, letting all boil together. Warm the preserving sugar,and stir it in,allowing three quarters of a pound of sugar to every pound of fruit, blackberries and apples together. When the suear has melted let the fruit boil for twenty minutes, removing the scum as it rises.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>TIIE SUNDEUED HEARTS:</head>
        <p>TIIE SUNDEUED HEARTS:</p>
        <p>A WAR OF THE HOUSEHOLD.</p>
        <p>By the Author of "The Double Life " "The Scheme," &amp;e., &amp;e.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XII.</p>
        <p>The compirtments in which Dora and Mrs. Narr had taken their seats in the express train had no tenants but themselves, the latter having bestowed a m 11 gratnity upon the guard with a request that he should put no other passenger in with them.</p>
        <p>Dora sank into a corner seat, turned her face away from her ci mpanion, sho was regarding her too closely, and gave hersclf up to her grief.. . .</p>
        <p>For a time thee was silence-between—the two.</p>
        <p>But at length Mrs. Narr began to move about uneasily, and finally broke the silence by exclaim- _</p>
        <p>"Aren't you ever going to say anything, Dora ? It 's dull travelling this way, with no one to speak to, no more'n if one was a carved wooden image " "I don't feel like talking," said Dora, .gently.</p>
        <p>"And, of course, if you don't feel lice it,you won't 1" exclaimed Mrs. Narr, testily. .</p>
        <p>Dora sighed and turned around, showing a face so white and woeful that a heart of stone might have been touched by it. But Mrs. Narr took umbrage, instead of being touched.</p>
        <p>"You seem to take it awful hard that you have to go away with your own mother!" she sniffed. "Of course, I hint as good as them Chessoms. I aint got no fine house, no troop of servants, no carriages, and heaps of money. But, inferior as I am, I wouldn't turn out a girl as had been made so much of in the family, just to save paying her mother a little annuity ! These fine folks are the meanest—that's so!"</p>
        <p>Dora looked pained, but made no answer. "This is a pretty how d ye do altogether !" con- tinued Mrs. Narr, discontentedly. " What sent the old squire off so suddenly, I can't see. And he as healthy to look at as any butcher ! He pre- tended to think so much of you, too, but fine words won't butter no pa'suips, as I've heard. And he never left you even a penny to buy mourning, but just left you to be turned out to earn your own living—you that he made so much fuss over! If them's the ways of great folks, I've had enough of 'em !"</p>
        <p>"Papa meant to provide for me," replied Dora, in a pained voice. " Please say nothing about him. You can never know as I know how good and generous he was." " 'Papa !' I should think that name had played out, since he left you so poor !" cried Mrs. Nam "Ho showed his affection, didn't he? Good and generous !' Yes, to his o n. When I heard that he was dead, I supposed of course he'd left you an heiress. But it's the way of the world. I told young Chessom yesterday that you were free to stay at the Grange, if he'd give me a hundred a year, but he didn't want you to stay. He said he was going to be married one of these days, and he thought you had better go away with your own mother. That's what your fine 'brother' thinks of you!" "You do not seem pleased to have me with you. " Well, I'm not," confessed Mrs. Narr. "I'd rather have had the annuity, that's a fact. You've got your fine-lady ways, and I've got my own notions and I'm afraid we aint going to get along together first-rate. What are you going to do?"</p>
        <p>"I shall become a daily governess. Mr. Chessom has given me an address where we can find lodgings, and also the address of the lady who will find me pupils." "He is generous. He told me he gave you a hundred and fifty pounds too. Where is it ?" "In my pocket-book." "Give it to me. I'm your mother, and the pro- per person to take charge of it." "No," said Dora, firmly. "I shall retain the money in my own hands. The duty of providing for us both falls upon me, and it is necessary that I should be purse-bearer." Mrs. Narr flushed with anger at this declaration, and burst forth with a torrent of vehement words; to all of which Dora remained insensible.</p>
        <p>"Whatever wants you have shall be gratified," said the young girl, quietly, "but you are not used to the possession of so large a sum of money, and I think it will be better if I keep it." "That's the respect of a daughter I" snivelled Mrs. Narr, rubbing her watery eyelids energetically and viciously. " Sharper'n a serpent's tooth is a thankless child, as somebody says, and he told the truth. That I should have lived to see this day! My own child afraid to let me take charge of her puree 1" -</p>
        <p>She snivelled yet louder, but as Dora took no notice of her display, her anger deepened into a spiteful rage. "You are under age, Miss !" she ejaculated. "I can take you before a magistrate and compel you to give up that money to me." "What do you want of it ? " asked Dora. "Why, to use. To pay our way and so on." " Well, I'll do all that. " We'll see what the magistrate has to say on the subject," cried Mrs. Narr. " You'll be taught your place, NI iss." Dora faced the woman squarely, her pale little face growing detei ruined and resolute. " Let us settle this matter between ourselves," she said, calmly. "it will not be necessary for us to go before a magistrate, I think, slus. No.rr." "Mrs Na/r ! Why don't you call me mother ?"</p>
        <p>"Because I do not feel that you are my own mother," answered Dora. "I shall never feel a daughter's idiection for you. I think it just as likely that I am your nurse-child, of whom you told inn last week, as that I urn your own child. Papa said the same. Indeed, it may be that your own child died, and that I 's-s kept by you for sonic re.i,on which as yet I cannot guess. If you go before is magi,trate, I will mention these sus- picions of mine to him." Mrs. Nair gave a quick gasp. Her face changed colour, and a wild look glared out of her eyes.</p>
        <p>" What an idea 1" she muttered. "It aint so. I enn prove it. 111. lordship buried his own baby in his vault." " His JordAip ! Who was he</p>
        <p>The woman leered cunningly. "lie was the father of my nurse-child," she an. owered. "Never mind his name. About this money, Dora. You can keep it, since you're set on doing so. I've no wish to anger you. We will be fi ienos ; but don't you think you can call me mother ? " " No ; but it isn't necessary." "But you'll have to call yourself Dora Itiarr." "No ; I shall keep the name of Chessum. It was the gift to me of dear papa, and I shall not give it up." Mrs. Narr growled discontentedly, but did not venture a remonstrance. Dora was too spirited for her to manage, and Dora's remarks concerning the nurse-child had thoroughly cowed her. Dora returned to her outlook from the window, and Mrs. Narr lapsed into silence, and finally drowsiness. Who shall speak the unutterable sadness of Dora's thoughts during the weary hours of that me- morable journey. But the journey at last drew to its end. Mrs. Narr awakened as the train steamed into the Lon- don Bridge station, and was the first to step out upon the platform when the guard unlocked, the door. A cab was selected, the cabman dispatched in quest of Dora's luggage, and Mrs. Narr and Dora entered the vehicle.</p>
        <p>"Where are we going!" asked the woman, anxiously, as the cabman came marching up with the trunks. "I hope you haven't lost the address." "No ; here it is," said Dora, taking the card from her pocket-book. "Number Six Lowater Crescent, Notting Hill." She repeated the address to to the cabman, who mounted, and they were soon bowling out of the station and moving slowly in the direction of the Strand.</p>
        <p>It seemed a long ride through the hot sun to Notting Hill, but that precinct was gained at last, and Lo water Crescent sought out and dip covered.</p>
        <p>The Crescent consisted of a dreary block of dingy brick houses, facing a small, fenced-in and pad- locked quadrangle of brown-looking grass. The quadrangle, called by the neighbouring people, out of courtesy, "a park," seemed never to be used, and houses fronting upon it were respectable and unspeakably dismal. To the country-bred Dora, fond of sunshine, the open-air and flowers, the place looked like a prison. The cab stopped before the number desig- nated, and the cabman ascended the high steps and sounded the knocker.</p>
        <p>Dora and Mrs. Narr then alighted, just as a servant came to the door.</p>
        <p>Dismissing the cabman, after he had brought in the trunks, the two went into the dwelliniz, and</p>
        <p>were ushered into a dreary little parlour, where they were joined by the landlady. To this personage, Mrs. Gaston, short, plump, red-faced little woman, with a hustling manner, Dora delivered one of the two letters which Ed- mund Chessom had sent up to her room just previous to her departure from the Grange. "Ali, from .Mr. Chessom ! " said the woman, glancing at the signature. "He asks me to find lodgings for the bearer and her companion. How very fortunate ! My second floor, the same Mr. Chessom had last year when he was here, happens to be vacant. How many rooms would you require?" " One ; a good bedroom," said Mrs. Narr. The landlady glanced with surprise at Dora. "We shall need three," said the young girl, quietly; "a sitting-rcom and two bedrooms." "Just my second floor. The rooms are in com- plete order, being half promised to a family, but I can't abide children, and you can have them. May I ask your name, Miss?"</p>
        <p>"ram Miss Chessom, having been the adopted daughter of Squire Chessom, on whose account I am in mourning," said Dora. "And this—this person?" "Is my mother, or foster-mother." Mrs. Narr glaxed at Dora as if she longed to hurt her.</p>
        <p>"Will you look at the rooms now ? " asked Mrs. Gaston.</p>
        <p>Dora assented; and the landlady led the way up stairs.</p>
        <p>As Mrs. Gaston had said, the three rooms com- prised her entire second floor. The front room, looking out upon the tiny "park," was of course the largest and was the sitting-room of the suite.</p>
        <p>_ It was furnished in true lodging-house style, with the inevitable chiffonier, rickety chair, ambitious easy-chair and pretentious sofa, and with a couple of oil paintings, each over a yard square.. . _ _</p>
        <p>-Adjoining this room, and opening from it, was a neat little bedroom, of which Dora took possession at once.</p>
        <p>At the further end of the wall, and not connect- ing with the other rooms was a third be iroom as good as Dora's and with this Mrs. Narr professed herself well pleased. "Mrs. Narr will do our cooking," said Dora, "and we shall, therefore, want the privilege of your kitchen. What will be your terms ?" Mrs. Gaston named them, and Dora paid a week's lodging in advance. _</p>
        <p>Mrs:. Narr then offered to assist the stout serving-woman to bring up the trunks from the narrow entry below, and this operation was soon effected, the trunks being carried into Dora's private room.</p>
        <p>"I'll send you up a cup of tea, Miss Chessom," said the landlady, "for you do look beat out. Your cheeks are as white as a rag. And I'll show Mrs. Narr the best shops in the neighbourhood, so that you can commence living at once." Dora thanked Mrs. Gaston in a way that won the plump little woman's heart, and she hurried away intent on preparing a little meal for her new lodger. Dora untied her hat and sat down on the sofa. "How very warm it is !" she sighed. "I wonder If there are no breezes in London." "Not many, I'll be bound !" said Mrs. Narr. "But one hardship more or less won't make any matter to me. This has been a disagreeable day to me. Indeed, this past week's been disagreeable. First, the squire dying without any reason, as you may say, and not leaving yon a farthing, and then all these things happening. If the squire had done as he'd ought to, I should to-day have been the guardian of an heiress." " We must not forget that we are poor, Mrs. Narr," said Dora, paying no heed to these com- plaints. "Two pounds a week ought to suffice for our household expenses, after the lodgings are paid for. We must try and make it do." She placed the sum mentioned in Mrs. Narr's hands, the woman seizing it greedily. "I'll make it answer, Dora," she exclaimed. "I never had so much to keep house with. We can live like a pair of queens on the sum. I think I'll go out and do our marketing at once, before I take off my bonnet." Without waiting for an answer, she hurried away, meeting the landlady on the stairs, and obtaining from her the necessary addresses and directions.</p>
        <p>Dora was, therefore, alone when Mrs. Gaston came up with a little pot of tea and a plate of toast.</p>
        <p>"I don't think I feel hungry," she said, lifting her heavy eyes and trying to smile. "You look downright sick, Miss Chessom," ex- claimed the landlady, setting down her tray. "Do try and drink a little of the tea—that's a dear." Thus urged, Dora sipped the tea, but the weary headache and heartache were beyond such simple prescriptions, and Mrs. Gaston at length retired fully persuaded that her young lodger was •' in a decline."</p>
        <p>About an hour later, as Dora was nestling on the sofa nearly distracted with the strange street noises, Mrs. Narr came swaggering in, her blitek bonnet bent all awry, her arms filled with pur- chases, and her face flushed almost to a purple colour.</p>
        <p>"Well, I 'm back," she pante&amp; thickly. dropping into the nearest chair, and tumbling her parcels upon the table. "I spent the mouey to good advantage, and its all gone. There's butter, and cheese, and bacon, and so on, and I left an order 1: the ale-house around the corner, and, not feeling very well, I got a little spirits, whioli they say is good at such times." She exhibited a black bottle, drawing it from under her shawl, with a maudlin smile of tliumph. Dora was at once disgusted and terrified. " Do you drink ?" she asked, sitting up. "Don't I?" demanded Alm Narr, with a leer, putting the bottle to her lips. " I aint a habitual drinker, my dear. I scorn such wiees. But I'm subject to them turns as require to warm up the stomach—spazzums, and sich.' Even in her disgust Dora remarked that Mrs. Nitres language had suffered the same deteriora- tion as her appearance, under the influence of liquor. She arose and approached the woman, trembling &amp;sq.-very limb. rive me that bottle !" she said, sternly. 011 won't ?" declared Mrs. Narr, doggedly. "It's 111 Ili tie. I don't feel well. Lem me 'lone !" "Give it to me I" repeated Dora, holding out her band.</p>
        <p>But Mrs. Narr was by no means intoxicated. She had taken a liberal draught of liquor—enough to make her obstinate and ugly—and she would not yield to the young girl's demand. " Stand back! ' she said flourishing the bottle as if it had been a weapon. "(Jr 'way Pretty way to treat your own mother I Lein me 'lone, or I'll hurt you ; see 'f I don't." There was a menace in her eyes which Dora understood, and before which she deemed it beat to retreat.</p>
        <p>She went back to her sofa, at a loss what to do, while Mrs. Nair took a comforting Full out of the black bottle.</p>
        <p>" Wish he was here," said the- woman medita- tively, after a pause. " Who?"</p>
        <p>" Jack, of course—Jack Narr, your pa." "Is he dead ?" said Dora. " You said he died In America." _</p>
        <p>" Yes, so I did," exclaimed Mrs. Narr, with a. cunning wink. " But I only said that to gain help and sympathy. Bemides, Jack was alw ger, and somebody might be looking for him, even after all these years. Well to be on the sble side."</p>
        <p>Dora thrilled with a sudden fear. " Isn't he dead ?" she demanded. "No more'n lam cried Mrs. Narr, with a hoarse laugh. " He's down in Cheshire, a waiting to hear from me. In a week we'll have him living here with us, my dear, as right as a trivet 1 He knows the squire's dead, and he's waiting to hear the next move." The news seemed incredible to Dora. " Alive !" she murmured, " Coming here I How can I ever bear it ?" Mrs. Narr chuckled.</p>
        <p>" The old man and I will have easy times now," she said, flourishing her bottle triumphantly. "We've got somebody to work for us. You ;Lint of age, you know, Dora. Your pa used to think a sight of you, and he'll think more of you than ever, now that you've got a hundred and fifty pounds! I've got a good mind to telegra.ft do‘ ‘n to Cheshire. He ought to be on hand. You might give us the slip !" she add-d, with sudden BUS picion. Poor Dora ! It had seemed before as if her bur- den were greater than she could bear, but the pros- pect of having to live wild' such a couple as this woman and the drunkard and forger Jack IN arr, was almost too much for her.</p>
        <p>The fumes of the gin began to make the air in- intolerable. The insolence of 'Mrs. Narr, who was growing quarrelsome, bec me almost unbearable. The girl walked the floor, wild with grief and Shame.</p>
        <p>"Oh, papa, papa 1" she moaned. "To what a heritage you have left me 9' Mrs. Narr heard the faint murmer, and, although she could not distinguish the words, she chose to interpret them as derogatory to herself. "The old squire might at least have learnt you manners I" she cried, roughly. "Them Chessoros are a worthless lot, for all their money. You'll have to get down off your stilts, my doll-faced Dora, I can tell you, when Jack Narr comes. ge won't stand no nonsense I"</p>
        <p>At this moment the knocker sounded loudly 00 the outer door.</p>
        <p>Dora scarcely heard the sound, in the excitement of her great grief. 4' Jack 'll take the whole of your money," car tinned the woman, garrulously. "And I don t care if he does. He's the proper one to take charge of it. What are you standing there for like a hor pole, Dora? Sit down 1" "Put up your bottle," said Dora, in a conciliat- ing tone. "You are drinking too much." "Say that again 1" cried Mrs. Narr, in a manse' ing tone she had before used, raising the bottle above her head, as if about to throw it. At this juncture a low knock was heard on the door. "Wish 'twas Jack 1" muttered the woman. Dora stood as if turned to stone, her face cold and white like marble.</p>
        <p>"Come in," hiccoughed Mrs. Narr. The door opened and a young man entered. One look at the noble face and tender eyes wile enough for Dora. The new comer was the young Squire Weir. "Oh, Noel, Noel !" cried the girl, from the depth of her despairing anguish. "Oh, Noel, help ne ! Save me</p>
        <p>She flew toward him as if for rescue, while the young squire closed the door, and surveyed the scene with a glance that took in thoroughly the state of affairs.</p>
        <p>(To be continued).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>GREAT WESTERN ENGINE DRIVER IN DANGER.</head>
        <p>GREAT WESTERN ENGINE DRIVER IN DANGER.</p>
        <p>MANLY CONDUCT OF A DOCTOR.</p>
        <p>Engine drivers have many narrow escapes, of whiell the public never hears : aor are their dangers confined to the active risks of their calling. The " South Wsll6 Daily Star " newspaper brings to light an interest'og case, which illustrates this. It appears that a Mr. Job° South, ef 30, St. Micheal-street, Newport, Monsloutht was for many years an engine driver on the Great war tern Railway, but he retired from active duty on reas°' log his aixtietis year, in good health. About a year ago, however, he waa taken suddesli</p>
        <p>" I always felt thoroughly tired out," said be, and I had severe paiu at the small of my back, as well as pa° in the back of my head and side of my face." Nis sufferings were very severe, and after two or thrte weeks he went to a very able doctor for them, and 610 for a violent cold which he had oontracted. The doctor gave him Medicine for the cold, but informed him thst, the pains in his head were " rumbling riteuenaticg, and that he could really do nothing at all to cure them.</p>
        <p>In consequence of this candid and manly admissioti, the doctor only paid him a couple of visits. The seemed to get worse, and though not actul Ily cosfincv te his bed,lae was unable to get about or do work of sill description. He kept a look-out in the papers for aEll' thing which might bring relief,and shortly after read ill account of the public attention excited by the Uri 6,,, effected by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pate peoplo cases very similar to his own. He procsred a box, 1$ followed religiously the instructions. When half the box, or perhaps a little more, had be.° taken,the pains began to get easier, the spasms irl thi° face being neither so frequent nor so severe. Thill rrt sulteneouraged him to continuo, and the improvernel was rapid and eontinuous,until by the time he had tait_iell about two and a half boxes he was completely cur r:' and felt as well as he ever did in his life notwithstanu" ing his 80 years. Since that time he has felt oil/ slight twinges of rheuniatics,and a single pill has proved sufficient te subdue them. Mr. south said he taught the Pills a wonderfai assdi" cine. He always kept a box in the house that th"; might be at hand,in case a tonic should be needed, and would not be without them for anything. He was nos' able to get about and work, and ;as surprisin? active.</p>
        <p>le I</p>
        <p>Mr. South showed a prudent precaution in kelp the pills at hand in:care :of need, for though 04' Williams' Pink Pills are not like other medicines, 8111 their effects are permanent, nothing else is so promPtilr pulling up the system when, from some temporary us pressien or otherwise, [strength is needed. They he cured more than five thousand Case., besides Mr. Sae° r ef nervous exhaustion, rhounsatisna, sciatica, esuiscul% weakness, depression, los: of appetite, palnitation of heart, headache, early decay, paralysis, -and lononOt.i ataxy ; al4o all diseases arising from impc veriihad $l/0 vitiated blood, such as scrofula, rickets, St. Vitus'dase; and chronic erysipelas, pale and sallow complesl°il. anaemia. wasting diseases like consumption, aid the manta of ladies. These Pills are not a purgative 0.1 cine. They contain nothing that would injure ths Psr,v-r, delicate system. They are sold by chemists and 11 Williams' Medicine Company, 44, Flolborn-viaduct. don, E.C., at 2s. 9d. a box, or six for 13s. 9d, Gellufry only with the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for fit Pimple. Pink Pills sold out of glass jars aro P</p>
