        <p>THE GREAT ASSASSIN.</p>
        <p>Vr RKLY CHAT:—Mr.Gladltone's apt deserip- the tion of the bloodthirsty monster who, unluckily for Armenians. reigns supreme in Constantinople, has trnlsed British indignation throughout the world. A. repeti- On of the horrible scenes recently enacted would overheat Isiltish blood to boiling point. Lord Salisbury is, however pdicionsiv steering the Ship of State through the sh,als of loreign jealousies. The intervention of this country with PQnstontinople would simply let loose the dogs o war. and ,Ind this Government inte vrious complications ; our Foreign linister is, therefore, calculating the cost of the probable ;LW'. This should actuate the minds of everyone when ;rat to engage in any new venture. There is no venture hiPh requires more care or consPeration than house fur- thing and our remarks to-day will be directed towards gly- Luit a few valuable hints upon house furnishing. Everyone I" II right to rurchse in the cheapest market, providing they have the knowledge and power to discriminate as Which is really the cheanest market. The lowest in price Is not always the cheapest ; many a man has to regret a cheat, De„noyworth. The cheapest article is the one which is actu- :PY fhe best value. Now, in furnishing, the first considers- Is. of course, the length of the purse; the long purse is eorrernecl with the cost; the man with a big income need *et trouble his head five minutes with the troubles of ersonotby ; but. the bulk of people "to not possess long purses, litid therefore economy is one of the most valuable and neces- considerations. There are a vast number of people in Is country who are possessed of tolerably good means, and others who might be termed rich, who delight In a bargain. Ito all those who are specially pleased In obtaining rare bar- "he in furnishing, and to those to whom the practice of ee°,lor4V is an absolute necessity, we cannot do better than to tlYise them to pay a visit to wORSLEY'S. the Great House 'ornishing Pantheon, Gaythorn. Manchester. They are the "lortest people in the trade, and they manage the only fur- !lolling emporium in England to-day that possesses the un- 'lsOnted largest stock of high-class second-hand furniture, in rlditlon to an immense stoc', of new furnittve, partly menu- etured by themselves, and otherwise drawn from the finest Vgnet-making firms in the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WHY ARE WORSLEY'S SO POPULAR?</head>
        <p>WHY ARE WORSLEY'S SO POPULAR?</p>
        <p>The reason ef WOR :LErs peonlarity, evidence of which tt given in the enormous patronage enjoyed by them Zu'ollghnut Great arises. in the first instance, from "tuft straightforward system of business which they have itloirlted. The whole of the stock is marked in plain figures. 111• perhaps. does not snem to he a great advantage at fl-st 'litht, but we venture to assert. for the instruction of those lbent furnishing, that it in an immense advant.ge ; it ore- we imposition, as you can walk through the establishment LIM see the price for yourself: you cannot then have arty- put on as an additirmal profit. It is a well-known fact hat house furnishers and furnishing companies generally _Mills, go in for getting whatever profit they can. One Ostomer may pay 25 par cent profit, and another may nay Lo Per cent nrnfit ; another may nay 25 per cent profit. WORSLRY'S have one unive•sal profit of 10 per cent ; they teeePt no abatement; everyone is charged the same price. ~.ut the most powerful reason of WORSLEY'S ponniaritv iles in the fact that that they have the largest turnover, in rnrniture alone, of any boil in Manchester. Their turnover for last year was over £5O 601 Their enormous turnover onables them to Inky in large quantities ; their capitol enables them to take every possib'e advantage of the markets. It is tensible that the,e arty Intages alone enable WORSLEY'S to bnrchase at 25 per cent less than the ordinary house tllrnisher. Their pr,,fit being 10 per cent the public then ibtaln what they require at 15 ner cent less than the crdinary bflse fu-nishen con purchase. To show how gigantic .WORSLRY'S operations are, we have just made a contract iar 30) bedroom suites all one pattern.</p>
