      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>TURKISH MASSACRES.</head>
        <p>THE TURKISH MASSACRES.</p>
        <p>MEETING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>SPEECHES BY LOCAL CLERGY, Mr. R. PIER- POINT, M.P., AND OTHERS.</p>
        <p>There was a crowded attendance at the Co-opera- tive Hall, Sankey-street, Warrington, last Friday night, when a public meeting was held to express abhorrence of the recent doings in Armenia and Constantinople. Canon Willis (rector of Warring- ton) occupied the chair and there were also on the platform :—Mr. R. Pierpnint, M.P., Rev. W. H. Drummond, Mr. R. Garnett, J.P., Rev. John Yonge, Mr. T. Kiernan, Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, Mr. W.Long, J.P., Rev. J. J. Langham, Mr. T. Birchall, J.P., Revs. Fathers Ryan. Firth, and Duggan, Rev. E. T. Reed, Rev. W. Potts, Mr. Stringer, Mr. E. J. Guest, Dr. Fox, Councillor Henry Roberts, J.P., Councillor Bennett and Mr. H.Broadbent. Amongst those in the body of the hall we notir:ed :—Rev. H. V. Pigot, Rev. B. Percival, Rev. L. J. T. Darwall, Rev. J. Gunnell, Rev. C. Andrew, and Rev. A. A. Markham, Councillor Lee and others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>LETTARS OF APOLOGY</head>
        <p>LETTARS OF APOLOGY</p>
        <p>Mr. Ziba Armitage,J.P..wrote "I beg to express to the chairman of this evening's meeting, my sincere regret that the dampness of the atmosphere and the probable heat of the Hall force me to decide that I cannot attend to manifest my deep sympathy with the persecuted and outraged Armenians and to protest against the apparent helplessness of the great powers of Europe to prevent these massacres of innocent people. Trusting that the present outcry may exteni to he people of continental states until the Governments are forced to exercise their undoubted power to put an end to the Turkish misrule. —I am yours truly, ZINL ARMITAOI. Mr. Parr wrote from Staunton Park, Staunton-on Arrow that he regretted to say that he had engagements there which prevented his being in Warri,gton yester- day. He sympathised most deeply, in common with them alhwith the object of their meeting and if such meetings would strengthen the Government in dealing with this sad state of things it would have his cordial support. At the same time they must take care that the agitation which it helped to keep going did not rather embarass their executive, and by creating distrust and apprehension among the other European powers, tend to keep them in a state of isolation, and so render them as a nation powerless to grapple with the gigantic evil. He could not suppose that Lord Salisbury was indiffer- ent to the terrible position of their co-religionists in Turkey and that he was not straining every nerve to come to their rescue, but had they sufficiently grasped the momentous issue involved ? Were we alone, and not only alone, but in the face and in the teeth of the opposition of united Europe to commence a bloody and horrible war for the deposition of the Sultan ? No action on their part could bring back to life the thousands who had been murdered, and were we, for the sake of revenging them and pun- ishing a poor bloodthirsty imbecile (for such the Sultan appeared to be), to run the risk of bringing misery and death to other thousands by kindling the flame of a European war? They ought not to suppose that Eng- land had a monopoly of the humanity and compassion of the world and that other nations were not looking on with horror and dismay at what had occurred —only their neighbours moved more slowly, and the fact of our agitation and proposed inde- pendent action, was causing jealousy and hesitation in their part and an defeating the very object we bad in view. The wisest course was to express their confid- ence in the Government and their satisfaction at the pressure they had attempted to put on the Sultan,and to express their fervent hope that in conjunction with the other powers and through the concerted action of Europe means might be devised for the hotter administration the Turkish Empire and the protection of the Chris- tian subjects of the Porte. Me hoped they would still recognise the neceuity of prudence and caution iu these very difficult and delicate circumstances and use cc language that would influence passions and ferment agitations, which instead of helping, would substantially injure the cause we had at heart and by accentuating our isolated position, render us more and more power- less to intervene to exercise any useful and effectual influence.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN also announced that he had received a letter from the Mime Rimer, Wilson Patton-street, expressing sympathy with the movement and enclosing 10s. 6d. for the relief fund. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>HANDSOMS CONTRIBUTION.</head>
        <p>A HANDSOMS CONTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. YONOB on rising to make an announce- ment was received with prolonged applause. He said that he thanked the audience for their hearty welcome to an invalid back to work. (Applause.) He then men- tioned that in the course of the afternoon he had had a visit from their townsman, Mr. Henry Thornton, who had entrusted him with a donation of £.50 for the Duke of Westminster's fund. (Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>TER CHAIRMAN'S ADD 3S.</p>
        <p>The Rieman said he wished to address himself to two points. First as to the good of such a meeting as that, fer it has been questioned. Two or three had aaked him why they should held such a meeting, and he had tried to think the matter over during the p...st few days. It struck him that there were two or three strong reasons for such a meeting as that. The first was that it was an opportunity for the expression of right. (Hear. hear.) If there was a responsibility there was also a responsi- bility attached to silence. He thought the time had come when it would really be wrong te sit still and be silent. When they read that within the past year 100.000 Armenians had been massacred and when they read that almost in a day 4,1100 Armenians perished in Con- stantieople one felt it impossible to be silent. (Hear, hear). His 'emend reason was that that meeting was in no sense a party gathering; and thirdly, because he be- lieved that such meetings would enable the Prime Minister to show to ether nations and especially to Turkey the real motive of any action which he may be disposed to take, so that it may net be thought he is actuated by any selfish diplomatic reason but that he is urged on to act by the united and wise expres- sion of feeling of the whole nation. (Ap- plause.) His second point was that this was a golden opportunity, for they had in the country one who had perhaps a greater power than any other man living to stay those horrible atrocities. Me alluded ta the Czar of Russia. (Hear, hear.) If the nation spoke wisely they had every reason to believe that their words would reach the Czar and that be would be in- fluenced by them. (Hear, Year.)</p>
        <p>TRU RESOLUTIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. WM. Long, J.P., moved" That this meeting de- sires to express in the strongest possible terms its deep- est sympathy with the Armenians end its abhorrence of the atrocities committed by the Turkish Government." He said he should like his first words to be those of con- gratulation and compliment to Canon Willis for taking the responsible place at their head in that meeting. (Hear, hear.) He had had a splendid lead from other members of his church and as they were all Englishmen they liked to see their national church at the head of suck a Christian movement. Another thing was the pleasure of standing upon the platform with men of all parties and creeds. (Hear, hear.) He thought they had not suffici- ent of that kind of thing. (Hear, hear.) That move- ment.he pointed out, was not against the Mohammetans and not against to Sultan because he was a Moham- metan hut because he was a bad man. (Hear, hear and applause.) Mr. R. PIERFOINT seconded the resolution. He argued that that was a question which concerned not the Armenians only nor England only but the very welfare of their world-wide empire, and in fact concerned the world at large. He desired to associate himself corn- pieta's, with the feeling ef sympathy by the Chairman, and Mr. Long, and he desired that the Powers should be able to rectify the misrule of the Porte and to cure it for ever. He hoped that meeting would strengthen the hands of the Government, but he should e.,,tremel7 regret if that meeting were to urge the country to plunge into war all by itself. He shovild like to remind them that although they were apt to talk of Turkey as the "Sick Man "it yet remained for fighting purpesee a very strong power. It bad a standing army of 160,100 capable of being raised to 800,000 and containing some of the finest fighting material in the world. The Dardanelles bristled with new forts and guns placed there, he was told, for the express purpose of keeping the English fleet out. Be also pointed out that if Con- stantinople were bombarded the Christians of that city who numbered 230,000 in all would be in the power of the fanatical mob. He maintained that the Armenians had not been persecuted because of their religion but because of their race,and he expressed the hope that some arrangement might be made whereby Armenia might have a government similar to that which was establiened in the Lebanon a few years ago. (Hear, hear.) The Rev. W. H. DRIIIMOND in supporting the resolu- tion said he had taken a deep personal interest in the Eastern question and in the meet develepment in Constantinople. The resolution was one about which there could be no differenee of opinion. It was simply an expression of their deep and heartfelt sympathy with men,women, and children who had endured things such that it was impossible for them to understand. Be pointed out some of the peculiar circumstances which made it aeem imperative for us to express our sympathy with the Armenians. He asked them not to consider the resolution as ordinary sympathy but sym- pathy which was kindled into moral indignation. He believed that the expression of that sympathy could not fail to be a- souree of great strength to the Government which was in power. They had to leave all setts of executive measures to men who were experts of their several departments of Government,and it was not for them to decide the precise ways in which intervention was to take place in that or any other cause. But an executive government was</p>
        <p>To MoTITERs.—Mrs. Wine low's Soothing Syrup has been used over fifty years by millions of mothers fu theirehildren while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the pool sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste : it produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain. and the litth cherub awakes 'as bright as a button." Of all Chemists, Is. lid. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>FXAMINER., SATURDAY, OCTOBER 3; 1896.</head>
        <p>THE FXAMINER., SATURDAY, OCTOBER 3; 1896.</p>
        <p>powerless to take a great and momentous decision unless it felt that it had the heart and the conscience of the nation behind it. (Applause.) We judged very largely of tne attitude of other great powers to ourselves by the utterances of their semi- official organs and their diplomatists. Be was not sure, with all deference to Mr. Pierpoint, that we always understood the real attitude of a nation best from those who spoke from an official position. (Laughtet end ap- plause.) He did not believe either that the great mass of the peoples of Europe were going to stand passive to any course we might take in order to support the iniqui- tous system of Government in Turkey. (Applause.) He asked them to accept the resolution of sympathy with tbe Armenians as an expression of the conscience, which should be the harbinger of tardy rescue to the people who were suffering such grievious and unspeak- able wrongs at the hands of a wicked Government. (Applause.) The resolution was unanimously earned. .</p>
        <p>_ Father Ryan proposed that the following resolution be adopted : "Resolved, that this meeting convey to Her Majesty's Government its strong desire to support it in any measures which it may find necessary te take in order to terminate the Sultan's iniquitous misrule." In referring to the remark of Mr. Pierpoint that it was not the persecution of Christians that ceused the Sultan of Turkey to act as he did,he said if they looked at the object of the policy of the Turkish Government for very many years past they almost invariably found that it had been the persecution of the Christians. (Hear, hear.) He could go back to the times when Turkey persecuted the Christians in Spain,when they persecuted Italy and also when they persecuted the Christian nation of Greece to such an extent that all the powers of Europe rose up and delivered Greece from Turkey's grasp. (Applause.) Their own monitories would also carry them back to the Bulgarian atroci- ties ; and there were very few that would state that thatlwas not en account of their being Christian. (Hear, hear, and loud applause.) Councillor ROBIRTS seconded the resolution, and said they were anxious and desirous to give the fullest pos- sible consideration to the sentiments expressed in the letter from Mr. Parr, and the speech from the mem- ber for the borough, but they must not forget that they could net afford to let their heads and pockets in this matter altogether rule their hearts. (Hear, hear and applause.) It would perhaps be a difficult thing for him to agree altogether with the posi- tion which tito member for the borough took up. (Laughter.) Their Member had called their special attention to the difficulties to be met with in a war with Turkey. He (Mr. Roberts) hoped and believed thatthere was not a man or a woman who would not do every- thing possible and reasonable to avoid that dread calam- ity. (Applause.) But there were things worse than war and one was the loss for ever of national conscience and national righteousness. (Loud applause.) There had not been a single occasion upon which when the conscience ef the coun- try had been aroused it had not gone forward in de- fiance of everything and everybody. (Applause.) He did not think they need to consider altogether the fact that Turkey had been spending a good deal of British money in fortifying the Dardanelles. (Applause.) The righteousness ef a cause was half way to victory. (Loud applause.) ;There were a number ef mongrel bred men in London and he meant what he new said—who were neither fish, flesh, nor good red herring (Loud laughter, and applause.) All they eared about was their pockets, and they must remember that fact when they saw some of the newspapers from that city. He did not believe war was in the slightest dee.ree probable. It was a bogey to deceive them as te the real issues of the question, and to blind them to their re- sponsibilities under the Cyprus convention. (Loud ap- plause.) The Rev. E. C. E. CARLETOm supported the resolution. The resolution was carried with acclamation.</p>
        <p>TELBORAM 'FROM MR. SCOTT.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAW, at this point, read a telegram frem Mr. P. B. Scott as follows:—To the Oksirman if the meeting.—Dear Sir,—Not being one ef your fellow townsmen keeps me from being with you this main. As my presence might he looked upon by same in a different light to that which I trust all wish to aveil, namely, making your meeting gamble of party. lam with you in all but person and hope you may have a grand meeting, carrying your resolutions unanimously thereby swelling the number of those who wish to see the most heinous Mephistopheles if modern times pre- vented for ever from adding further to his already atrocieus crimes and that at once. rind regards, Percival B. Seett.—Fallowfield.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. J. Guest, S.F., moved "That the Chairman be requested to forward the above resolution te the Prime Minister." He said they were desirons to do no harm to the Powers that be but it was their linty to stir them up te do that which they believed to be. right. It was the duty of England now to have no diplomatic dealings with Turkey and for the Turkish ambassador to leave London and the Enzlish ambassador to return from Constantinople. He did not think we should involve Europe in a general war if we went to the relief of the Armenians in genuine earnest. (Applause.) The Rev. J. J. LANGWAV seconded. . -</p>
        <p>The Rev. W.—Po—Trs— in supporting said many a time 'Alen the chariot wheels of our diplomacy dragged slowly alone he wished for an hour of Oliver Cromwell. (Lauehter and applause.) The third resolution was carried unanimously. On the motion of the Rev. .1. YONOI. seconded by Councillor BEIMITT, a vote of thanks was accorded the Chairman. A collection was taken for the Duke of Westminster's fund for distressed Armenians, after deducting the ex- penses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>EXHIBITION OF ART STUDENTS' WORK AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>EXHIBITION OF ART STUDENTS' WORK AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The annual exhibition of students' work con- nected with the School of Art is now being held in two of the second storey rooms of the Museum, and particulars respecting the time etc., will be found in our advertising columns. The numbers of ex- hibits is perhaps a little below that of last year, but what is shown is exceptionally meritorious work. There is a notable advance in the quality, and there are evidences of concentrated study and careful tuition. Mr. Aylward, the headmaster of the school is to be congratulated on the success which his students attain year by year. The first room entered contains some very good specimens of model and freehand drawing which have gained the csrd of merit, and also some draw ings from life and some paintings. Miss Slater ex- hibits two splendid drawings from life—one the bust of a man—a local nensioner—and the other that of a woman. The facial expression on both is accur- ate and highly creditable to the student. Miss Gertrude Pickavance who won a £lO scholarship at the last examination also displays a drawing from life, t work which gives promise of a clever artist. The paintings shown in this room are four-hour time studies, and although here and there may he detected blemishes, the neleuring en the whelp is rood and shows that the students have been well !ramified in thin imperil...at matter. An antique figure of Hercules by B Daintith ism strikin.; exhibit in this room and is . work of unusual merit. Fel. this, Mr. Paintith gained the national bronze medal, a very high distinction. Another fins niece of work ley the same student is a pedestal which he has designed and carried out. It will be found in an edjoinine' room whore the remainder of the exhibits are shown. Miss Lees eXhihitS a drawing from life—which is easily recognisable as the face ef Mr. Taylor. of Mnsieum-street. and also a skilfully carved corner cup- board. Roth exhihite are excellent. Stanley Pierpoint, holder of a Lancashire County Csunnil scholarship, at present in Paris, has four painting.* on view of what may he termed night effects. Oswald Gareide, whe has spent three rears in Paris Salon, sends ten naint. inv.; to the exhibitinn, and they give evidence of the nroerens he has made, especially in the painting of nnrtraita. The "lieu..s of a eirl with a bundle of 'ticks in her lap is perhaps the hest if the groon. Hollins Allen, fornserl'r a student at the sehenl. hot new art master at Canterbury, has three Pictures on view, one a striking portrait, another a Csiea for a stained siege window, and the third, a vase finvc.ra , We !late before commented on the excel- lence of Mr. Alien's work, and the three niptnres. now in the Mun,nrn may ha taker, as very gnarl enemmona. Miss RrundrPtt and 14112 K‘thi.Onrilqn,l also show form, executed naintines. Mr. Woodier, holder of a (1-ointr Council sehnlarshin, sends en fewer than fifteen black and white skatelles to the exhibition. They are rne,tiv of locli blrs of scenery, and some of his pie'",, seem admirably ariapte4 for bnek work illustrations. nrawiega nf classic ornamonts are shown by Mr. S. W. Rail: and Miss Parker, an rld student of the .scheol,sends a large number nf doliehtfol sketches in California, which cannot fail to interesting.</p>
        <p>The finme Serretary, ineakine at Preston nn Monday. advised surspntters nf vnhintaro Rchnnls not tn make nnreagnnahle demands nn the Government. If the de- mandg were r.aannahle he had not the slicrhtest heeita- tinn in sayinff that the Gnvernment wnold tint be nn- willincy to do what they could with the, object ef assist- in Yohintarr erhnnls.</p>
        <p>EPPS'S CADDA.—GRITEFUL AND COMFORTING.—" Br ft thorough knowleg, of the natural laws which govern the operations of digastmn and nutritin.-1. and by a isreful ap- plication of the fine pronerties of well-selected CO-OA. Mr. iTnp3 has provided for our breakfast end slipper a delicately fl,voured beyrrage which may save us many heavy doc- tors bit's. It is by the indicions use of such Articles of diet that a emistitution may be gradually built sin until strong enough to resist every tewiency to disease. We may esca'ne many a fat d shaft by keeping ourselves well fortified with mire blood and a n-olerly nourisned frame."—Ci”il Service Gnzette. —Maae simply with boiling water or milk.-- sold on'y in rackets and pound tins. by Grocers. la%elled " VPP.S &amp; co , Ltd.. Homo•onathic Chemists tendon." Also makers of Epps's Cocotine or Cocoa-Nib Extract ; A thin beverage of full flAvnitr, now with many beneficially taking the place of tea. Its active principle b.ing, .a gentle 1 nerve stimulant, snoolies the needed energy without unduly exciting the system.—(No. 2.)</p>
