      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DOG, THE MAN, AND THE MEAT.</head>
        <p>THE DOG, THE MAN, AND THE MEAT.</p>
        <p>A friend of mine and I were walking together the other day ; a dog dashed past us after something he saw on the pavement. It was a big piece of meat. He pounced on it and swallowed it in two seconds. My companion looked at the deg with envious admiration. "My humble friend," he said, " I'll give you £5,000 for your appetitite and your digestion. You are not afraid to eat ; I am." But the dog knew what happiness is made of. He declined the offer and trotted away.</p>
        <p>It is astonishing how many different people use this expression. "I am "or "I was" afraid to eat. As the writer pens these lines five letters lie on the table before .him, every one of them containing it. Yet the persons who wrote the letters are not known to one another. There was, therefore, no agreement among them. Why should there be even if they were acquaintel ?1 /%11, there is nothing in it to wonder at. They went through the same experience,and express it in the most natural way, that's all. But what does it mean ? Are people suspicious of poisoned food? No, no; that is not so. The food is not poisoned before it is eaten, but afterwards. An ex- ample will show what really occurs, and why so many are afraid to eat.</p>
        <p>We quete from one of the letters "One night, early in 1892," says the writer, "1 was seized with dreadfu pains in the pit of the stomach,and a choking sensation in the throat. I feared I was going to die. My wife called in a neighbour. They applied hot flannels and turpentine,but I got no relief. Then a doctor came and gave me medicine. He said he never saw anyone's tongue in such a condition. It was of a yellow colour, and covered with a slimy phlegm, so thick I could have scraped it with a knife. I had a foul,bitter taste in the mouth,and my eyes were so dull I could scarcely see. I gad a heavy pain in the side, and felt E o dejected and miserable I didn't know what to do with myself. What little food I took gave me so much pain I was afraid to eat. The doctor put me on to starvation diet, and in- jected morphine to ease the pain. " Getting no real benefit from the first doctor I saw another, who said I had enlargement of the liver. He gave me medicines, but I got no better. In August I went to Exmouth to see what my native air would do for me,but came back worse than ever. I had lost over three stone in weight,and being too weak to move about I used to lie on the couch most of the time. I never ex- pected to get well, and didn't care much what became of me.</p>
        <p>"One day in October my wife said, 'it appears the doctors can do nothing for gou, so lam going to doctor yon myself.' She wont to the Southern Drug Stores, in Camberwell-road, and got a bottle of Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. After taking this medicine for a few days the pain in my stomach left me, my appetite im- proved. and I gained some strength. Soon after wards I was back at my work. The people in the office, see- ing how well I looked, asked what had cured me, and I answered Mother Sei4el's Syrup. I shall be glad to reply to any inquiries about my case. (Signed) Charles Harris, 74, Beresford-street, Camberwell,London, Decem- ber Ist, 1892." Mr. Harris' statement goes straight to the point. Why was he afraid to eat ? Because his food gave him pain without giving him strength. This was dead wrong. It was exactly the reverse of what it should have been. When a man is the proper form he gets vigour and power from his meals, and eats them with enjoyment and relish. If he doesn't there is something the matter with him. What is it ?</p>
        <p>Now let your thoughts expand a bit, so as to take in a broad principle. One man's meat is another man's poison, they say. That's 80, but it's only half the truth. Any man's meat is any man's poison, under certain con- ditions. If grain never got any further than the mill hopper we should never have bread, and if bread (or other food) never got further than the stomach we should never have strength. See ? Well, when the sto-nach is torpid, inflamed, and "ON STRIKE," what happens ? Why, your food lies in it and rots. The fer- mentations produce poison which gets into the blood and kicks up the worst sort of mischief all over the body. This is indigestion and dyspepsia, though the doctors call each and overy trick of it by a separate name. Yet they don't cure it, which is the main thing after all. But Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup does, as Mr. Harris says, and as thousand of others say.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>SHIP CANAL. LIVERPOOL AND THE SHIP CANAL COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL. LIVERPOOL AND THE SHIP CANAL COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>It is, says the " Manchester Guardian," understood that the agents of the principal railway companies run- ning into Liverpool are at present making investigations with a view to ascertaining the exact effeat which the competition of the Manchester Ship Canal is having upon their goods traffic to and from Liverpool. Lists are be- ing prepared showing the shipments by each route and the volume of trade diverted. If it is found that the present condition of things involves less loss to the companies than would result from all-round reductions in their rates no actien will be taken. In the same con- nection, it may be mentioned that Messrs. Elder, Demp- ster, and Co., whose recent action in regard to the im- portation of cotton at Liverpool has already been referred to in these columns, have followed it up by a letter asking the railway companies for their assistance in reducing the charges and increasing the facilities for getting cargo to and from the Liverpool docks. They claim that the rates ef carriage to and from places within 100 miles are too high, and that high railway rates and port charges are responsible for the scistence of the Ship Canal. The letter suggests that the heavy terminal expenses on goods from Manchester are un- necessary, and that goods should be brought by rail directly alongside the steamer, the great terminal ware- houses tieing useless.</p>
        <p>The efforts which are now being made at Liverpool to enlist the help of the railway companies to crush the canal are in striking contrast with the attitude of in- difference and contempt adopted during the early career of the port sof Manchester. But so far the railway com- panies have displayed no intention of fosi-ering Liver- pool at the expense of Manchester and of their own in- terests more than they do already. It appears not to be generally recognised that the cases of Liverpool and the railways are by no means on all fours in regard to the canal. There is no reason in the world why Manchester dock traffic should not be quite as profitable to the latter as Liverpool traffic;indeed,the canal has already brought a very large volume of absolutely new trade t 6 the West coast,from which the railways have drawn their share of profit, and the railway traffic between the Manchester decks and inland towns in all parts of the country is in- creasing by leaps and bounds. Manchester has certainly no reason to be very grateful to the railwar companies for assistance in develonin., threueh traffic via the canal,but there are already indications that the growing importance of the great waterway an a feeder to the rail- ways is being recognised. It must not be forgotten that the London and North-Western Company has, at con- siderable expense, made a new branch line connecting their system with the docks at Manchester ; that the Lancashire and Yorkshire Company is at present driv- ing a costly tunnel under the streets of Salford to ob- tain direct access to the quays, and that the Man- chester, Sheffield, and Lincolnshire Company has a scheme pigeon-holed for connecting their line with the dock railways at Trafford Wharf. Railway companies do not spend large sums of money in this way without expecting some return, and it may safely he assumed that they will not at the behest of Liverpool render such property valueless by helping to stifle the trade of Man- chester, even were it in their power to do so, which is at least doubtful. They can, of coarse, reduce their rates between the two cities, but so long as it is possible to charter steamers to Liverpool and Manchester indif- ferently on the same terms such a step would simply add another to the many substantial benefits already conferred by the canal on the traders of this district without injuring the waterway in the slightest.</p>
        <p>It is highly probable, therefore, that Liverpool will be left to face the competition of the Ship Canal by it- self, and that the railwAy companies, which will ulti- mately benefit- by any increase of trade whether at Liverpool or Nianchester. will maintain a neutral atti- tude. One thln2 at least is certain, that none of the ridiculous schemes for plateways, motor road-waf.mons, and competitive railways will be taken seriously either by the railway companies or by Liverpool capitalists themselves. The Ship Canal has abundantly justified its existence in various ways already, but nr more slequent testimony to its industrial utility c4uld be con- ceived than that which is at present coming from Liver- pool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
