      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>BROKEN HEADS IN BANK-STREET.</head>
        <p>BROKEN HEADS IN BANK-STREET.</p>
        <p>A LIVELY MELEE.</p>
        <p>THE POLICE GET ASSAULTED.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor and other magistrates at the War- rington Police Court on Monday,John M'Coy and Edward M'Grath, both of 17, Bank-street, were charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bank-street on Saturday night; they were further charged with assaulting P C. Alfred Newsy at the same time and place.—Mr. Braw ne appeared for the defendants and pleaded not guilty.— The Chief Constable (Mr. L. Talbot) prosecuted. P.C. Newey said that at 11.20 on Saturday night be and P.C. Delahay, both in plain clothes, were c,l:ed down Bank-street by a Mrs. Wooton who complained about a disturbance. They proceeded to a lodging-house walking on the footpath. They saw a number of People including M'Grath standing opposite a door. Witness and Delahay stepped off the footpath to pass them while Mrs. Wooton pushed through. Some of the men swore at her. Witness said nothing at the time but went to the lodging-house where they found the disturbance all over. Both of them returned and Delahav told the people on the path to go into the house and not insult people. M'Cey then struck witness in the mouth. They closed and witness was kicked several times by women and men. Delahay took M'Coy into custody, and the second defendant then cline up and struck and kicked witness. He then ran into the house and came out with a poker. With this he struck at witness who warded off the blow with his stick. Another blow caught witness on the wrist and another on the back. He (Newey) then hit M'Grath across the head and shoulders with his stick. Sergeant Monaghan and P.C. Corbett came up and went into the house with them. Cross-examined by Mr. Browne :—The men were drunk. Dr. Fox came 20 minutes after the row. He dressed M'Grath's head but did not say in witness's hearing that the men were not drunk. Mr. Browne ; Is this the first time you have been accused of assaulting men ? _</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: I object. That is an improper question. Mr. Browne : Were you implicated when Sergeant- Major Stone was injured? Newey: Yes. Were you in that case in Winwick-road ? Newey : I was a witness in that case. Mr. Browne: Was Mrs. Wooton sober? Newey : She appeared perfectly sober to me. Mr. Browne: Did you hit Mrs. Glavin in the eye? Newey : No. Mr. Browne: Do you know M'Coy was asleep all the time in the house?</p>
        <p>Newey : We was not asleep when he hit me in the mouth. (Laughter.) Witness admitted hitting right and left with the stick.</p>
        <p>By the Chief Constable: Bank-street was a vary noto- rious neighbourhood and the police had great difficult), in getting witnesses there. He knew that Mrs. Glavin had said that whatever injury she got was not done on purpose. P.C. Delahay gays similar evidence to P.C. Newey. He witnessed the assault on the latter by M'Coy and also saw M'Grath kick him. He saw M'Grath fetch the poker and strike him with it. Witness then let M'Coy go and got possessien of the poker. Newsy struck M'Grath with his stick. Dr. Fox was sent for and washed M'Grath's head. Both defendants were drunk and no more violence was used than arm necessary. Witness also struck out hut he had no stick.</p>
        <p>In answer to Mr. Browse, Delahay said neither himself or Newey had hld any drink and were quite sober. They were paraded by Inspector Cox at 10 p.m. He never saw Newey use the stick before the poker was used.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Rowbottom deposed that he saw Delahay and Newey at 10.35 in Buttermarket-st., and they were quite sober. They were steady officers and had no report against them. Witness took the charge at 12.30 when the defendants were drunk. Newey's lips were burst and his face bloody. His thumb was also knocked up. Detective-Sergeant Monaghan stated that he and P.C. Corbett went to the assistance of the ot er two officers. Both nf them went into Glayin'e house and witness fol- lowed Newey complained of being assaulted and M'Grath was bleeding. They waited for Dr. Fox as he had been sent for. Defendants were drunk but more dangerous than if they had been worse drunk. Mr. Monks : Is this one of the districts where it is almost dangerous for a witness to come from and give evidence in favour of the police ?</p>
        <p>Sergeant Monaghan : It is, sir. Inspector Spinks stated that he had heard the prisoners tell the Chief Constable what drink they had had, M'Coy said he had had several pints and M'Grath said he had had two. Mrs. Glavin had said that Newev hit her eye with his ;:tick but she did nct say it was wil- ful. _ _</p>
        <p>For the defence Mr. Browne submitted that the officer u were a little excessive in their duty. If they had never interfered with the men in the street, who were doing no harm, the case would not have occurred. With regari to the first charge, the police always added the charge of drunk and disorderly if there was an assault on the police, to make it safer. Delendants denied they were drunk.. M'Coy wag asleep in the house wheu the police began the row. Newey must have lost his Lead, because he was hitting right and left. Mrs. Offends resident in Bank-street,said on Saturday night she heard the row. She went out and sass two nen using sticks but did not know they were policemen until she was told. The sticks were used more than they cught to be. By the Chief Constable : She could not tell how many cracks she heard. She saw two sticks. One was not a poker. She was not sufficiently excited to see double. Michael Varley gave similar evidence to Mrs. Owen. He also saw two sticks which the officers were using in a fearful manner without provocation. The Chief Constable pointed out that it was admitted that there was only one stick and he had evidence te prove it. He had made the most searching investiga- tions and was perfeetly satisfied that the officers were right in acting as they did. M'Coy had been up before but M'Grath had not. He did not consider the assault one ef a serions nature in its results.</p>
        <p>The magistrates retired for a few minutes and the Mayor on their return sentenced each defendant to one month's hard labour, and said that but for Mr. Talbot's remarks they would have got more. They could not allow the use of a poker to the police. The Chief Constable asked for an expression of opinion as to the action of the constables.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: The police were only doing their duty. Mr. Monks: The unanimous feeling of the bench is that they did not exceed their duty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>COMMON DISEASES.</head>
        <p>COMMON DISEASES.</p>
        <p>2.-THE LUNGS.</p>
        <p>Lung troubles in the British Isles are more common than any other diseases. Simple catarrhs or colds lead to bronchitis and inflammation of the lungs. In addi- tioa.to these minor troubles the lungs are subject to dis- eases dee to germs, such as consumption. When at a mean sea level the oxygen is plentiful all the breathing capacity of the lungs is not used, but ascend, say, a mile above sea level, and all the lung substance is called into play. That is how consumptives are sent to places a mile and more above sea level, where they are bene- fited and sometimes cured. Pneumonia is another dis- ease due to germs. More care is required in cold, damp weather to keep them free from trouble than any other organs of our body. The question of pure air is , a vital one, and exercise in all weathers in the open air is of the utmostlimportance. But over and above all is tbs ab- solute necessity for keeping the body in robust health. See how quickly a weakly, antetnic person catches cold, and hew soon it flies to the lungs. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, with its pare Caracas Cocoa, Kola, Extract of Malt and Extract of Hops, is not a medicine, but :imparts n• urishment, and canoes to the rescue by building up strength and vigour. Mothers who would keep their children in good health should give them morning and evening Dr. Tibbles' Vi- Cocoa made with hot milk. Delicate men and women who have weak lungs to be hale, robust, and healthy should use Dr. Tibbles Vi-Cocoa morning and evening, and all men who have to be expeaed to the bleak uncer- tainty of our trying climate should fortify themselve be- fore they face their daily toil with Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, and they can then brave the fury of the elements with equanimity. The writer speaks from personal experi- ence and from observation of beneficial effects on others. Tsa opens the pores and temporarily excites, coffee stimulates the action of the heart, whilst Dr. Tibbles' Vi- Cocoa gives strength, stamina, and builds :uri and strengthens the lune tissues. It is indeed a wonderful ' food beverage. Nothing has ever been discovered that can approach it in giving lightnese of heart, joy of lite, fleetness of foot, and that general feeling of comfort which only comes from a full capacity to enjoy every pleasure, moral, intellectual and physical. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is made up in 61. packets, and 9d. and is. 6d. tins. It can be obtained from all chem- ists, arocers, and stores, or from Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, Limited, Saffolk House, Cannon Street, London, E.C. Merit, and merit alone, is what we claim for Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cecoa, and we are prepared to seed to any reader will do) who names The Warringtnn Ea nin.,r a dainty sample tin of Dr. Tibbles' ter Sa Vi- Cocoa.</p>
        <p>The New York " Mail and Express " states that, al- though their names did not appear on the passenger list, Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Chamberlain sailed on the Germanic for England. The journal states that four dotectiyes followed Mr. Chamberlain to the pier, and re- mained nearly until the ship left. Subsequently the de- tectineA said they had shadowed Mr. Chamberlain for three weeks, as he had recerred a letter from Fenians stating that they intended t&amp; make an attack on his life. I member of the Endicott family whom Mr. Chamber- lain visited at Salem, Massachusetts, says that the de- tectives who were recently engaged in watoning the Ihouse were men supplied by the United States Secret Service Departuient,and were sent owing to information which had been received at headquarters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>NOTES.AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES.AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>The writer of " Anecdotal Europe" in the " Illus- trated London News" says that with regard to the simple lite led at Balmoral, it is altogether in accord- ance with that of the Czar's great-grandfather, grand- father, and father. To be fully convinced of this, one should read in Moltke's Letter a description of the bed- room in which Nicholas I. died: and it must be borne in mind that the great German was by no means fastidi- ous. F.r the last sixty years, in fact, the private exist- ence of the Russian sovereigns has ben deliberately shorn of all pomp; though the Grand Dukes did not always follow tkeir example. The Czars during that period were all, more or less, addicted to reading, but their main amusement, with the exception of Alexander 11., was music.</p>
        <p>The appearance of a railway company as the publisher of educational literature is a decided novelty. That, however, is the character in which the Great Eastern has just appeared. It app,Ars that the cheap rates of carriage for small parcels of agricultural produce have beea unable to meet the demand for fowls and eggs to be sent direct to town. The Great Eastern directors have decided to help in the matter by the issue of pamphlets giving instruction as to the best methods of production, and have made a beginning by publishing "Hints on Poultry-keeping," an illustrated pamphlet, which is being extensively circulated in the rural dis- tricts served by the company.</p>
        <p>A sister of Lord Russell of Killowen has been for many years the Superioress of the Sisters of Mercy Convent in San Francisco, where she is known as "Mother Mary Baptist." She has been in California since she was twenty-five, and is five years younger than the Lord Chief Justice. She is one of the leading women in her own sphere.</p>
        <p>The contrast which has been noted as existing be- tween Mrs. Bryan and Mrs. McKinley is not less marked than that between the two ladies whose interests are 'diametrically opposed to them—Mrs. John M. Palmer and Mrs. Simon Bolivar Buckner, the wives of the Sound Money Democratic candidate for the Presidency and Vice-Presidency of the United States. Although neither of them is young, they have not been long married—as length of years is counted—Mrs. Buckner having been a wife only eleven years, while Mrs. Palmer has been married only seven years to her present husband. Mrs. Palmer is very much the senior of the two, and is, in fact, in her sixtieth year, General Palmer being in his eightieth. Mrs. Palmer is the daughter of one of the early settlers in Springfield, Illinois, who made a large fortune in trade. She herself is a highly-educated woman, who married a well-known railwayman, Mr. Leigh Kimball. He, however, did not live very long, and at the age of twenty-seven Mrs. Palmer was left a widow. Hor studious temperament led her to apply for the post of city librarian at Spring- field, and for ten years before her second marriage she held the office. Mrs. Palmer not only accompanies her husband on all his tours,but has for a long time acted as his private secretary, helping him to prepare his auto- biography on which he has been engaged for some time. Unlike Mrs. Buckner, who is tall. Mrs. Palmer is a little woman. She is popular among her acquaintances, but does not care much for society. Mrs. Buckner, on the other hand, was made to entertain, and her reputation as a hostess is great among the Southerners, who are notoriously celebrated for their hospitality no less than for the charm with which they disoense it. She is an exceedingly pretty woman, with clear-cut fe4tures and black hair and eyes. She is descended from a well- known Virginia family,one of her ancestors having been a near relative of George Washington.</p>
        <p>A novel kind of pilgrimage, which will assuredly pro- voke some sympathy in this country, has just been or- ganised by the beerdrinkers of Berlin. Under the patron- age of St. Gambrinus a band of pious votaries will set forth this month to visit all the great brewing centres of Qermany from Munich to Pilsen. As it is intended in course of a week to halt at Nuremberg,Dresden,Leipzig, Culmbach, and a dozen other towns where the cult of malt and hops flourishes in all its pride,the work of the pilgrims is cut out for them, and not a few may be ex- pected to fall by the way. The promoters of the enter- prise themselves appear to be rather nerveus lest the zeal of their followers should carry:them away. They append to the programme a warning that the eyes of the empire will be upon them, and that it is hoped they will bear themselves in a manner worthy of citizens who hail from the imperial capital. What a pity it is that age and failing health prevent Prince Bismarck from heading the processien,as he certainly weuld have loved to do in the day of his prime.</p>
        <p>An incident in the House of Commons of the Dominion Parliament of Canada has not (says the " Daily News") been sufficiently noticed. The members, during a divi- sion, displayed their feelings of rejoicing at the reign of the Queen having exceeded in length all those which have preceded by singing the National Anthem in the division lobbies. Yet in the year of the Queen's acces- sion to the throne, when "To Deums " were sung in Canadian churches to celebrate the event, the congrega- tions left their seats and walked out. Responsible govern- ment was conceded to Canada in 1840, after years of violent disaffection and refusals of Home Rule, which risked the loss of the colony to the Empire. The grant- ing however, to the Canadians of a Constitution, to quote the words of the statute, " similar in prineiple to that of the United Kingiom." transformed the disloyal and revolted Canadians:into the most loyal of British sub- jects. The concession of Home Rule in Canada has been well described as the emancipation of the Empire and the principal event in our modern colonial history.</p>
        <p>The great question of dress for the Czar's visit to Paris has (says the " Daily News" correspondent) been settled in favour of the civil suit. There will be one ex- ception, M. Hanotaux, who, apart from his Ministerial office, holds rank as a diplomatist. He will therefore have the distinction of his diplomatic uniform. The President will wear the Grand Cross of St. Andrew. He will ride in the same carriage as the Imperial gnests,and will sit with his back to the horses. Royalists think this is shocking. After all, they say, M. Faure represents France. The Emperor of the French, by the way, sat with his back to the horses in condi cting Queen Victoria and her husband from the Western Railway terminus to St. Cloud in 1855.</p>
        <p>Perhaps the most charming article in the review tbis month is Lord Welby's description in the " Nineteenth Century "of Lord Randolph Churchill's career at the Treasury. SLord Welby's praise of poor Lord Randolph's faculty for work, his insight, his passion for economy, his genuine reforming spirit, his impulsive and com- manding political genius, is unstinted. Many pleasant stories are told of his 'admiration of Mr. Gladstone. A portrait of the Grand Old Man stood on his table. " How could you have left such a man ?" he asked a Unionist at a dinner table which Mr. Gladenne had just quitted. He was always prophesying that Mr. Gladstone would outlive him,and would write a beautiful letter about his death. The prophecy was fulfilled. Mr. Gladstone wrote a most touching letter to the Duchess of Marl- borough.</p>
        <p>The Duke of Newcastle, who celebrated his thirty- second birthday a few days ago, has, following the example of Dr. Gcrden Stables, a large caravan ex- quisitely and compactly fitted up and furnished to ac- commodate three people. Occasionally,during the sum- mer months,his Grace takes a tour in it with a friend or two. Besides a perfect little cooking range there is a tiny piano, typewriter, fishing rods and guns, and of course,a photographic dark room and apparatus,for the Duke is an ardent photographer,and the taking of "bits of scenery." &amp;c., is one of the main objects of the tours. Only a few years ago the Duke made a long tour of Surrey, Sussex and Kent in a caravan.</p>
        <p>The various authorities in South Africa are getting thoroughly alarmed at the spread of the rinderpest, and even the Transvaal Government is spending something like £40.000 a month in efforts to curb or stamp out the plague. Unfortunately all the precautions adopted hitherto appear to have been well-nigh futile, and in their despair the colonists have been considering the advisability of importing camels for saddle, pack, and draft purposes. The hopes of the Cape authorities,how- ever, have been dashed by ad vices from Russia and In- dia to the effect that camels are particularly liable to rinderpest.</p>
        <p>Several extraordinary scenes werl witnessed at Done- gal Quay, Belfast, on Tuesday erenir,g, in connection with the departure of a large detachment of soldiers for foreiE:n service. The military were accompanied by a crowd of about 2,000 persons, who raised a tumult, and stones and other missiles were freely thrown. During the melee two soldiers either jumped or were thrown into the river, but were quickly rescued. A large force of police quelled the disturbance, but a constabulary sergeant was seriously injured.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Farah-/lname and Ilyaoun, two unhappy Armenians who were among the earlier refugees who escaped from Constantinople to Marseilles. have (says the "Chronicle") been seeing a good deal of the world— not in comfort, perhaps, but at trifling cost. As soon as they set foot on French soil it was politely but firmly intimated to them that they had better move on. The destination was left to their own choice, and they vaguely suggested North America. Raving been put on hoard a steamer bound for New York, they were asked by the skipper whether they possessed the five-pound note which is a necessary passport for admission to the ; hospitality the United". States. We regret to state that I they replied in the affirmative, whereas their pockets were absolutely empty. To New York, therefore, they found their way, but on discovering their penniless con- dition. Cousin Jonathan promptly sent them back. vessel conveyed them to Boulogne, and thence they got passed on to Paris. The Turkish Consul General bestowed en each the handsome donation of 5F., upon which they lived for a day or two, and then took to begging. A police agent arrested them in the Rue Saint Lazare, and they were haled before a magistrate, who ordered them to be carried to Marseilles and restored to their own ountry Zitgre2</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>"A MAN IN A TRANCE."</head>
        <p>"A MAN IN A TRANCE."</p>
        <p>At the Chester Police Court on Monday" Dr." Vint, a "mesmerist," was summoned for using threats to Harry Bowers Nicholson, a commercial traveller. The pro. ceedings arose out of a midnight scene in the Chester Music Hall, where Viut had been giving a" man in a trance" entertainment. Nicholson and some friends bad gone to the music hall to demonstrate that the affair was a fraud, and with the object of tiring out the trance subject had provided themselves with cards to while away the hours of the watch. According to Nicholson, the subject suddenly, about midnight, simu- lated a fit and struck one of the watchers in the face. Ho was immediately taken in hand by the attendants, who induced him to resume his sleep in the casket or "coffin" About two o'clock, however, probably find- ing the proceedings monotonous, the sleeper arose, put himself in a fighting attitude, and asked Nicholson and friends what they wanted. Vint was sent for to quell the slistu,bance, and threatened Nicholson with a re- volver. For the defence it was contended that the com- plainant and his friends had cruelly maltreated the trance subject, and it was further asserted that the revolver was a toy weap-m, and used as a cigar cutter. Vint, was however, b3und over to keep the peace for three months.</p>
