      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>ALL MEN ARE LIARS</head>
        <p>ALL MEN ARE LIARS</p>
        <p>S 3 writes a Poplar Author. Not so bad as that I hope, although I must admit some men are wicked liars ; in fact, it's only by accident they ever speak the truth. It is not a lie when Gershon, the Universal Furnishing Contractor, 44, Oldham-street ; 17, 19, and 21, Hilton-street. Manchester, states that he never attempts to put before the public common new London made rubbieh, more like orange boxes, for high-class second-hand; it is not a lie when Gershon states that he never attempts to trespass upon his neighbour's business. Why should he? It only causes a bad feeling. Gershon's motto is live and let live. He sticks to the one thing he understands, namely, furnishing. If you can't furnish with a man that understands his business, you cannot do so with men that don't. Gershon has for years cautioned the public against buying common new London furniture for second hand, and he still does so. However distasteful it may be to others, Gershon still takes his stand upon the rock of " Honesty and Justice," which are the guiding principles of his business, and from which preceuts time nor tide will ause him to recede.</p>
        <p>TERMS OF BUSINESS. RAILWAY FARE PAID TO COUNTRY CUSTOMERS. GOODS STORED FREE TILL REQUIRED. " REMEMBER."</p>
        <p>Any lot advertised may be had on the deferred paymen system withoßt any extra charge. Goods exchanged if not satisfactory. Mr. Gershon will purchase or take in exchange a single article or complete kouses of furniture. COMPARE PRICES.</p>
        <p>REVOLUTION IN THE FURNISHING TRADE. Mr. GERSHON is a great admirer of keen competi- tion, but he strongly objects to others copying his advertise- ments. Its me-an; and to those that have not the brain power to write their own, Mr. Gershon will do so for them. REVOLU E TION IN THE FURNISHING TRADE. No extra charge for credit, and 5 per cent. allowed for prompt cash. Mr. Gershon is anxiously waiting to see what unscrupulous shopkeeper will copy this.</p>
        <p>REVOLUTION IN THE FURNISHING TRADE</p>
        <p>MARVELLOUS BARGAINS. _ _ GRRSHOWS CAUTION.</p>
        <p>When in search of genuine second-Land goods don't be de- ceived and have new common stuff, m re like orange boxes, palmed on to you. When I have second-hand furniture I give he names and addresses, and don't simply say I get them from this place and thyt place. You might as well look for a needle in a truss of hay as look for the lateowner. Don't forget what the popular author wrote, "All men are liars."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>NO EXTRA CHARGE FOR CREDIT.</head>
        <p>NO EXTRA CHARGE FOR CREDIT.</p>
        <p>FIVE PER CENT. ALLOWED FOR PRO lIPt CASH. Out of the number of mansions, hotels, and houses I have second-hand goods from, the following may be mentioned. CALL AND INSPECT THE GOODS FROM— HAYESLKIGH, OLD TRAFFORD PRINCE OF WALES HOTEL. HARROGATE. HOLLY LODUE, ALEXANDRA PARK. FULSHAW BANK, WILMSLOW. CARLTON-STREET, MOsS SIDE. MRS. GOLLOTT, Wll4ll 3LOW. MRS, THORBURN, UPPER BROOK-STREET. AND OTHERS.</p>
        <p>BARGAINS GREAT AND WONDERFUL. Smart complete House of Furniture, consisting of the whole furniture for three rooms. sittinir-room. bedroom, full size bedstead, mattress bed,bolster, pillows, sheets, blankets, bolster ease, pillow slips, and kitchen complete all like new; price 14 guineas. No extra charge on the deferred payment system. Five per cent. for prompt cash. Call and inspect them. No harm done if you don't buy. ATARVEILOUS VALUE. A Charming House of Furniture, really like new. It consists of dining-room suit in leather, extending dining- table, carpet, rug, fender, fire brasses, lace curtains, window pole complete, magnificent bedroom suite (not painted, solid wood), enclosed dressing-table, large bevelled glass attached; enclosed washstand, marble slab tile back, and bevelled shaving mirror, they form two complete chest of drawers ; full-size handsome black and brass bedstead, wire spring mattress, bed, bolster, pillows, sheets, blankets, pillow slips, bolster cases, toilet set, carpet. complete furniture for kitchen, floorcloth for same, ditto for lobby and stairs. Call and inspect the lot. Get a complete list, and you will say they are a marvel of cheapness for 24 guineas. No extra charge on the deferred payment system. Five per cent allowed for prompt cash.</p>
        <p>GERSHON'S UNIVERSAL FURNISHING ARCADE, 44, Oldham-street (next to the Adelphi Bank), Manchester. _</p>
        <p>Charming Drawing-room lot, in perfect condition, con- sisting of handsome Suite, just been re-covered in one of the most fashionable coverings : the charming Cabinet to match, lovely Overrnantel, two Tabies, Carpets and Rug, Fender and Fire Brasses. all complete and ready for fit- ting up. A superior drawing-room well worth £s.,,delivered for 24 guineas.</p>
        <p>GERSHON, THE UNIVERSAL FURNISH- ING CONTRACTOR, 44, Oldham-et., next to the Adelphi Bank, Manchester.</p>
        <p>FROM ONE of the ABOVE ADDRESSES. Remarkably handsome Solid Walnut Bedroom Suite, consisting of full 6ft. wardrobe with two robing bevelled mirrors reaching trom plynth to cornice, fitted with drawers, cupboards, and hanging compartments; magnificent pedes- tal dressing table, fitted with eight drawers in the pedestals and large bevelled glass attached supported by carved brac- kets; large size pedestal washstand, fitted with cupboards in the pedrstals ; bedside pedestal with napkin drawer, towel rail, and three charming uphotstered chairs. The suite is guaranteed solid throughout. Well worth £5O; packed, war- ranted, and delivered for 24 guineas. No extra charge for credit, five per cent, allowed for prompt cash. Gershon, Universal Furnishing Contractor, 44, Oldham-street, next to Adelphi Bank, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>GERSHON ALLOWS FIVE PER CENT DISCOUNT FOR</head>
        <p>GERSHON ALLOWS FIVE PER CENT DISCOUNT FOR</p>
        <p>PROMPr CASH.</p>
        <p>ANUMBER of Bedroom Suites, just like new, from a hotel, in solid American Walnut, Bur Walnnt, Satin Walnut, Olive Wood, Bird's Eye Maple, English Ash, Mahogany, Cyprus, and other woods. Each suite includes a superior wardrobe, well-fitted and plate-glass door, bevelled; dressing table with bevelled glass attached, some enclosed, some open, and some with side reflectors ; double washstand with marble slab, tile back, and bevelled shaving mirror ; bedside cupboard, chairs, and • owel airer ; the prices are 18. 16, 14. 12. 10 and 8 guine is the suite, packed and delivered. Each suite is worth double. Five percent. for prompt cash. No extra for credit. Call ;tad inspect them. GERSHON, Universal Furnishing Contractor, 44, Oldham-stteet, next to the Adelphi Bank, Manchester.</p>
        <p>GRAND BARGAIN IN DINING-ROOM SUITE. —All the wood work is guaranteed solid, sprin stuffed, covered in dark grain !either, all in perfect order; the covering is new; price, packed and delivered, £4 18s 6d. Charming Suite, in saddle bags, all new cover- ing, extra strong solid frames ; grand blrgain for 5 guineas. Several Sideboards, in various woods. at marvellously low prices, with bevelled glass backs: from £4 lOs to £6O. Dining Tables, all sizes and all woods; from 245. Second-hand Carpets, Rugs, Fenders, and every requisite for complete furnishing, at GERSHON'S, the UNIVERSAL FURNISH- ING CONTRACTOR, 44. OLDHAM-STREET, next to the</p>
        <p>Adelphl Bank, MANCHESTER. ALL ARE INVITED TO WALK THROUGH.</p>
        <p>RESULT OF UNHAPPY MARRIAGE, DIVORCE PROCEEDINGS, Bcc.. perhaps the most favourable opportunity ever offered to those about furnishing in good style is the present. The furnishing in question has been removed from a very large house in the vicinity of Ply- mouth Grove, and everything was made to order only 14 months ago by one of the best houses in the city. The prices quoted are exactly one-half the cost. Nothing like a full des- cription can be given. Inspection is invited. Firstly. the hall is furnished with a grand dark mahogany Louis Stand and pair chairs to match; price 9 guineas. The dining-room is in solid black walnut of magnificent an t uncommon design. To attempt anything like a description would be a waste of time ; to say the least, it is the most charming and. I unique, the dining-table extending with extra leaves, sup- ported by square legs, which are beautifully carved; the over- mantel is such that can only be made by workmen of the highest standard ;itis a work of art. Next comes the truly magnificent Suite, which, of course, are also in solid black walnut, and consists of 12 standard Chairs (the price quoted is for six chairs, more can be had if required). with double- padded backs, two extra large lounge chairs, and a grand couch, of large size, sideboard alone is worth £5O Os 9d. the whol furming a noble lot for high-class dining-room. Price 68 guineas. 1 have also the carpets, paintings, and engravings from the same room. The drawing room is furnished in the latest style. and surpasses everything for elegance I have ever seen. Firstly, the Curio display cabinet at once strike n you as being a marvel of beauty and workmanship It possesses all the requirements ot such an artistic article Next comes the handsome music cabinet with face fron and various divisions, lined with plush ; two curious shape tables, with fancy cut and carved legs; the overmanted with diamond-shaped glasses, and recesses for the display of nick-nacks ; lastly, but by no means the least, the truly magnificent suite of double gondola settee, for any part of the room ; two charming easy chairs, four occasional chairs, and two gossipy chairs. All are upholstend in a rich silk tapestry, with raised flowers and sprays, and finished with silk cord and gimp. It may be remarked that these fittings are suitable for only a high-class drawing-room. There are in all nine bedrooms, space prevents all being described. The best room of all. I may here state, is suitable for a lady of title or a lord, it cost £l2, and is offered for 60gs The suite is simply magnificent, the wood is perteztty solid bright- polished walnut of the choicest veiny, the wardrobe is 7ft wide, fitted at either end with hanging compartments which are enclosed with brilliant bevelled Vaxhan plate glass of shield shape, between the doors are drawers, and above are bay-shaped cupboards, with massive carved panel., which at one, isplays great dexterity with the chisel. the whole is crowned with 9. cornice of tower like shape,the dressing table is 4ft 61n. wide. psdestal design, and i ay-shaped between the pedestals, which are fitted with drawers, the glass, which supported by carved brackets, is shield shape and extra large. Next comes the 41t Gin bay-shaped washstand, with marble slab, tile, and glass back, about 4ft high, the pedestal towel rail, and upholstered chairs forms one of the finest suites money can buy. There are eight other bedrooms which contain suites at all prices, some as low as 4 guineas and some at 14, 18. and 25 guineas. . . _</p>
        <p>, . The suite from the breaktast room is in saddle bags, price 8 guineas ; dining table, 355; sideboard, £7 15s; two recesses bookcases, 75s each. Another carver 4 oak bookcase and a host of other articles are on view. Carpets, rugs, tenders, orna- ments, paintings, china, and glass, all removed to 44, Old- 11am-street, Manchester.</p>
        <p>DON'T FORGET. r DON'T MAlif'; ANY EXTRA CHARGE FOR a_ CREDIT, AND ALLOW FIVE PER CENT FOR ROMPT CASH.</p>
        <p>GERSHON, UNIVERSAL FURNISHING CONTRACTOR, 44, OLDHAM-STREET, MANCHESTER. NEXT TO THE ADELPHI BANK, and 17, 19, and 21. HILTON-STRNET. .01 adjoining and corn rounicating with 44, Udharn-street. Manchester.</p>
        <p>THERE is still no subject which el- The Powers and cites so much public attention in the Sultan. this country as that relating to the Armenians. There has, it is true, been a con- siderable abatement of the agitation during the last few days. This is not due to any lessening of interest in the question, but to the fact that mos', ot the meetings which were organised to give voice to the iadignation of the country have noW been held. These meetings have certainly answered the main purpose which their promoters had view. They have shown to our own Government and to foreign Governments that the people of this country are practically unanimous in their desire .to put an end once for all to Turkish atrocities. The agitation seems to have had air other very useful result in convincing Continental authorities that the " do-nothing " policy which was in favour two or three weeks is a failure. There is now no talk of leaving the SULTAN a free hand in dealing with his Christian subjects in or- der to prevent the "re-opening "of the dreaded. Eastern Qoestion. It is now recognised that if there be another outbreak of massacre the Eastern Question will be re-opened in spite of all precair tions. Two or three incidents which have occurred' during the week seem to indicate in which dire' tion the wind is now blowing. In the first pie! there is a perceptible hardening in the attitude 01. at all events some of the Continental Powers to- wards the Porte. In the course of a special inter view with the SULTAN the French An-ibassader, it is said, used language of a decidedly menaciag character to wards that potentate. The Suass was, we are told threatened with serione, consequences if he did not show a decide' improvement upon his past record. This action on the:part of the French ambassador is in thie highest degree important, seeing that France an" Russia are now so closely allied with each other ill regard to all foreign questions. If M. CA311305 used the language which, he is repOrted to have used to the Sultan it may almost be taken f°c granted that he spoke on behalf of Russia as well of France. Another suggestive incident of the week is thereply which the representatives of the • Great Powers at Constantinople have made to the excuses for the recent murders and Pill- age which were made by the Turkish go' ernment. This reply is framed in stall and almost threatening language. The anihae# sadors declare that they are compelled t° abide by their remarks regarding the attitude cif the Turkish authorities during the massacre Constantinople. They also draw attention to th danger to public order arising from the impuilli/ enjoyed up to the pres ent day by all those 41:: took part in or fom en ted the massacres, and conclusion they bluntly remind the " Sublime. Porte of its responsibilities. All this diplomatic activity may of course amount to very little, ti on the other hand it may mean a great dea.• may mean that some useful impressions have beel. made upon the CzAR during his stay at Balineril; and that he is now more inclined than he wee few weeks ago to co-operate with England in tecting the Armenians. The agitation has cL/ tainly not brought us any nearer that Ear°,llat war with which we were threatened—a wavy ther is, with England on one side and all the ° great powers,with Turkey thrown in, on the °btlie ourt. It has indeed had exactly the oppose effect. A y °IOW the:time the agitation commenced this c was threatened by the newspapers of Belli - Vienna and St. Petersburg with direful' conse steP quences if it ventured to move even a silllot 0, xcept in unison with the famous "</p>
