        <p>Llrope." But now, and especially since Mr. %.ADSTONE'S memorable speech, all this has been ellunged. The newspapers of Europe have begun to realize that England may have a will of her Own, and that site may have the power to enforce that will, as far as the SULTAN is concerned at all events. The language of menace has, therefore, to a considerable extent given Place to that of persuasion and cajolery. Russian newspapers have discovered that an agree- tent between England and Russia, the other 4ropean Powers concurring, might lead to the efficient maintenance of order and tranquility in the Turkish empire. It is censidered, too, that the" eminently humanitarian sentiments" of Mr. GIAnsToNE should find an echo in all philanthropic hearts—and, in a word it is now being found that 40111ething may be done where only a week or two Mee it was declared that even the attempt to do awithing would be little short of insanity. The bogey of an European war has been discredited,and Will perhaps not be long before even the most tunid patriot is convinced that this country is 4till powerful enough to do effective work in the Cause of humanity.</p>
        <p>IN a review of the work of the The Brewster Sessions Brewster Sessions for 1896, the . "Manchester Guardian" gives E(lnle interesting information. It appears that 419 conspicuous feature in connection with licens- ing this year has been the almost entire absence of private opposition to the renewal of licenses. this is no doubt due to the following incident Ithich took place last year. A chimney sweep at kver named Mr. BOULTER objected to the re- newal of three licenses on the ground that the houses were not needed in his neighbourhood. he objection will not be considered an unreason- able one when it is stated that there was a public- on each side of Mr. BOULTER'S house and a third only a few doors away. The magistrates took this view and decided not to renew the license of one of the houses, known as the "Duke of Cambridge. But this particular Duke of Cambridge" was not to be so easily disestablished. The owner of the house, a firm of brewers, appealed against the decision and the rtst Kent Quarter Sessions re-granted them the „ense. The County magistrates assembled in rarter Sessions did something even more effective ,hati that in the interests of the brewers, for they utiscided that the audacious chimney sweep who ,itred to object to having a public-house on both ;ties of him should pay the costs of the appeal. his decision of the Quarter Sessions was also 1411 Pealed against but in this case it was affirmed t'Y the higher court, and it was laid down as law 44t Quarter Sessions have tho power to award e°4l, ts against an objector to the renewal of a license. ter such a decision as this it is not surprising at private people have judiciously refrained from t'king upon themselves the burden of objecting 17 a license. At the same time the applicants for %ceases have not had it entirely their own way. tpeaking generally, magistrates all over the coun- bq have displayed a decided unwillingness to grant (111°1'.1 licenses. In Liverpool 130 old licenses were Llseted to and the magistrates decided to refuse . renewals, while fourteen others were allowed lapse. As might be expected reports from bet-y direction show a rapid increase in the num- rof tied houses. By far the greater number Of 4'llblic-houses are now owned by brewers and in ,r4arly instances the occupiers are simply salaried "lagers liable to a very summary removal. This rt.ate of things is prevalent not merely in this part 7_t the country, for it is reported that there are i 129 tied houses in Bedford and only 12 free, and eriiPsawiedliwtihte h noryber of tied houses is 230 as ornpre 35 free. The magistrates in %mile quarters have attempted to discourage the gr. owth of tied houses, but they have not been tlery successful, and it is anticipated that the free %Ilse will soon be a thing of the past.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
