        <p>' L°CAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Wyn ,-,LIFFE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH. 'tto " Bund ridan__ evening there was a most gratlfs ing 14:1q,n" Rt Wycliffe CAngregational Church, the h'inZ a free an I open service lasting an hour. 't1.60 tiltaniZers were present and all listened with inter- anrif* Pastor's sermon on, " Christ's first word to `-'• A wniPen." p.s A. hymns were used and heartily " ht, anthem" Seek ye the Lord " was well rev- Rlitho choir, while Mrs. R. H. Pearson's treatment ii,,„'Llinrtinn of it met with much appreciation. Nltif,, au" a likelihood of these occasional sermons be- t/N:l'llas inetitution.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND wiDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN MIISIC&amp;L SOCIEIT.</p>
        <p>- The members of this society will give a rehearsal of the " Messiah " on Thursday evening next. Further particulars will be found in our advertising columns.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The above club held its -last run, on Wednesday,when a good number turned out for a spin to High Leigh. Tea was served at High Leigh Farm, and after a game of football, the party returned home,all being well-satisfied with their afternoon's holiday. RUNCORN PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>_ The annual harvest thanksgiving services, in connec- tion with the above church, were commenced on Thurs- day evening,when the Rev. W. Best,of Chester,preached to a large congregation. The sacred edifice had been tastefully adorned by the lady members of the congrega- tion. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>MR. W. H. JUDE.</head>
        <p>MR. W. H. JUDE.</p>
        <p>It will be seen from our advertising columns, that Mr. W. H. Jude (Liverpool) is to give a musical evening in the Public Hall on Wednesday next. According to the programme, there is a treat in store for all who are priv- ileged to hear the performance of Mr. Jude on this occasion.</p>
        <p>A SUCCESSFUL VOCALIST.</p>
        <p>Our readers will be pleased to learn of the s iccess that has attended Mr. John Pownall,in his professional career as a vocalist. Mr. Pownall it will be remembered,was a member of the Runcorn Parish Church choir, and the other week he attained a position, as one of the princi- pals. in the can Rosa Opera Company, which is now appearing in Manchester. SAD ACCIDENT TO A RUNCORN MAN.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon William Purnival, age 20 years, son of Henry Furnival, of 12 Rock Mount, Mill-brow, Runcorn, sustained serious injuries whilst following his employment at the residence of Mr. Charles Timmins, Rockfield, Higher Runcorn. It appears that Furnival along with his father was carrying out the extensive alterations at Mr. Timmin's house. Mr. Furaival, senior, was on the root of the house and throw a heavy stone on to the ground. As soon as it had left his hands the son emerged from a doorway and the missle struck him on the bead. Dr. Ormsby was quickly upon the scene, and after examining the injured man, ordered his removal to a Liverpool hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>SUDDEN DEATH OF A RUNCORN WOMAN.</head>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH OF A RUNCORN WOMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Worsley,coroner,held an inquest on Tuesday morning at Runcorn on the body of Susannah Kirkham, wife of Richard Kirkham of Brick Kiln Houses,Runcorn, who died very suddenly in the Navigation Inn on Satur- day. From the evidence it appeared that Mrs. Kirkham along with her husband, had been making purchases in the town,anci on returning home they called at the Navi- gation Inn. Some drink was ordered,and after deceased had supped a little, she fell forward and cxpired imme- diately. Dr. McLennon was sent for and on his arrival he pronounced life extinct. The jury returned a verdict of death from " Natural causes." RUNCORN REVISION COURT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Colt Williams continued the revision of Northwich Parliamentary voters' list on Thursday, when he held a sitting at the Runcorn Police Court. The party agents present were Mr.Hasdley,who represented the Liberals, and Mr. Hughes. who represented the Conservatives. There was no feature of public interest. The business principally consisted of the erasing of duplicates,correct- jug addresses,and putting the numerous lists for the pol- ling districts of Aston-by-Sutton,l3alton, Runcorn North and South, and Weston in perfect order. Although ar- rangements made were for a two days' sitting, tbe lists were completed in less than four hours. The respective assistant oversees a were highly complimented for the efficient way in which they 'had prepared their respec- tive lists. A special compliment was paid to Mr. Sutton, of Runcorn, whose lists,the Barrister renaarked,were the best which came under his notice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RUNCORN WESLEYANS AND THE ARMENIAN QUESTION</head>
        <p>RUNCORN WESLEYANS AND THE ARMENIAN QUESTION</p>
        <p>At the quarterly circuit meeting of the Runcorn Wes- leyans, held on Wednesday, the Rev. T. Brackenbury presiding, the following resolution was submitted : "That this Wesleyan Methodist quarterly meeting, representing seven important congregations, churches and Sunday schools, express its profound sympathy with the Armenian people in this time of severe persecution and its horror and indignatiln at the terrible and con- tinued massacres of Armenians under Turkish rule. This meeting earnestly implores Her MAjesty's Government to take immediate and effective measures for bringing to an end the reign of terror, which has so long dis- graced the civilised world, and securing for the Chris- tians and other subjects of the Turkish Empire,freedom from persecution and safety of life and property." It was pet to the meeting and unanimously passed that a copy of the above resolution be forwarded to Lord Salis- bury and Lord Rosebery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>NOTES ON GALES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GALES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY' FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Still the tide of adversity flows on and the Warrington Rugby Football Club is almost becoming accustomed to its novel position of" tail "in the Lancashire League. Another crushing defeat was experienced on Saturday when Runcorn, last year's cup-holders, put on 14 points to nil against Warrington. Some measures will have to be taken if the team is going to uphold its name in first- class football. Time was when the name of Warrington was almost sufficient to induce a team to go in mourning before its engagement with the wirepullers ; now, alas! the same clubs reckon upon Warrington as one of the teams there is a certainty of taking points out of. Run- corn, practically a native team is better than ever, while Oldham, most emphatically a foreign team is as re- nowned as of yore. The time seems to have come when Warrington will have to strike a happy medium between these two and secure a few really first-class men from outside. All the other clubs which have to be met have done so, much to their advantage, and why not War- rington? At least two new three-quarter backs are needed, one half-back, say, though that is not very ur- gent, and three forwards of rather heavier calibre than the present eight. With five successive defeats these alterations are now imperative.</p>
        <p>Though the team has failed so far there were many who thought that Runcorn would not have the game all their own way on Saturday, and this—notwithstanding the score,—was the case. Up to about ten minutes from the finish the visitors had their share of the match, and the much diaparaged Warrington forwards were far and away the superior eight. The half-backs too held their own, but the three-quarter line lackul combination and was lamentably weak. ~The score does not altogether represent the play.</p>
        <p>In the blek divison, Barber was in his usual good form and it was not his fault that the Warringtonians returned home pointless. Twice be carried the ball to the line but no score followed. In the first instance Rad- ford failed to take his captain's pass while close to the line and in the second Barber was glaringly obstructed. Unfortunately the referee failed to see the act and a great injustice was thus done Warrington. This match saw Radford's first appearance with the premier team, and he did not do so badly, everything considered. Ow- ing to his previous Association play he is not sure f...t taking and receiving passes, but when he gets the ball he can show a clean pair of heels. He is useful also in kicking, wbile Saturday's match saw him tackling very pluckily. When be has had a little more experience be should make a smart back. Carey was not very reliable and Lawless, also, played a worse game than usual. Bate and Houghton played a very strong game at half- back, the former coming out brilliantly at times. Heesom was steady at full-back.</p>
        <p>When Butterworth, Runcorn, scored his fine try in the second half the visitors lost all h pe and it was owing to their half-hearted play following, that the line was crossed three more times. It was rather disheartening for the forwards, after getting possession of the ball, to see the three quarter line unable to use it.</p>
        <p>To-day, Morecombe, new converts to the Northern Union, try conclusions with Warrington here. Theyare by no means a poor team, and possess a wing three- quarter, Holmes, who is not much inferior, if any, to Barber. Still it is fervently hoped that the homesters may triumph and break the spell which, seems to have been thrown over the team.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night tho following teams were chosen to play in the trial game for the selection of the Lan- cashire team on the Salford ground next Wednesday : Probaiales : Copp (Leigh), full back; Holmes (More- cambe), S. Lees (Oldham), Anderton (Leigh),and Valen- tine (Swinton), three-quarter backs ; J. Beiry (Tyldes- ley),and A. Lees (Oldham), half backs; Bossor (Oldliana) Worthington (Tyldesley), Robinson and Cleminson (Broughton Rangers), EdwoAds (Oldham). Brown (Wigan), Simpson (Rochdalc Hornets), and Beves (Sc. Helens), forwards. Possibles : Eagiand (Oldham), full back ; Martin (Oldham), Chapman (Broughton Rangers) Rispan (Widnes), and Barber (Warringtoo),threequarter backs ; Messenger (Broughton Rangers), and Molyneux (St. Helens Recreation),half backs; Taylor (Warrington', Greenhalgh (Swinton),Furness (Oldham), Walsh (Wigan) Cornthwaite (Morecambe), Lancaster (Rochdale Hor- nets), M‘Master (Leigh), and Emery (Widnes),forwards. Reserves : Foulkes (St. Helens), full back ; Jago (Wigan), Smethurst (Cro:npton), Lewis (Swinton), and Spillane (Leigh), three-quarter backs ; Halliwell (Wigan), and W. Berry (Tyldesley), half backs ; Wood- ward (Tyldealey), Winstanley (St. Helens), Waugh (Rochdale St. Clement's), Jones (Salford),and Fildes (St. Helens Recreation), forwards. Kick off at 330. Referee: J. H. Smith (WiJues). Touch judges: R. Walmsley (Salford) and J. H. Houghton (St. Helens).</p>
        <p>For the Pcstibles it will be aeon tleat Barber is chosen at wing-three quarter again. It is also very gratifying to notice that Taylor is given an opportunity of proving that he is worthy of a county jersey.</p>
        <p>The new grand stand will be formally opened by the Mayor, preceding to-dare match.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Council was held at the Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday afternoon. There were present, Mr. J. Mclntyre J.P. (chairman), Admiral Parry, Messrs. J. Brandreth, 11. E. Heywood, S. Wood- ward, T. Baxter, C.C., J. M. Williams, J. B. Chadwick, E. Aarcin,J. Darbyshire,T. Lewis, J. Weir, J. Stokes, W. Bradford, T. Wright, C. Roylance, J. Whitlow,G. Pilling, J. Spruce, C. Mounfield, W. Turner, C. W. Davenport, E. Bretherton, W. Pickering, T. Darlington, Mr. J. Ash- ton (clerk), Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk), Mr. J. Far- rington (nuisance inspector), Messrs. Diggle and Swinton (surveyors).</p>
        <p>CONDITION OF STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>A letter was read by the Clerk, which he had received from a Stockton Reath resident, in reference to the "disgraceful condition" ot Fairfield-road, Stockton, Heath, Warrington. The writer asked that the Council would give their attention to this matter at once.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>TEE WALTON BROOK.</head>
        <p>TEE WALTON BROOK.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Mounfield, clerk of the Walton Parish Council, complained of the state of the brook between the Ship Canal and the river, as being detrimental to the public health. The Parish Council requested that the matter should have immediate attention.—Mr. Weir said in con- sequence of the Ship Canal being mado, there was now no water flowing down the brook, and the drainage in consequence settled there.—lt was ultimately decided to instruct the clerk to fully investigate the matter,and re- port at the next meeting.</p>
        <p>FINANCE.-INCREASE OF SALARIES</p>
        <p>Mr. Heywoed,chairman of Finance Committee, said a committee meeting was held that morning, and after considerable «iscussion, and going into every possible point, and also the qualifications of the different officials of the Council, they had come to the decision that Mr. Farrington's salary should re- main at £l5O per annum. Messrs. Diggle and Swinton should be increased to £l4O each per annum. The de- cision arrived at was tested in every possible way, and finally they resorted to the system of ballet. After the ballot a resolution was proposed and seconded that the sums named be given to the two officials. It was put to the meeting and carried unanimously. He hoped that the motion would be adopted.—Mr. Weir proposed an amendment to the motion. He said the time was very unfavourable for such an increase. If any advance should be given it should in his opiniun be given to the inspector. When they considered the changes that had occurred he thought there was no occasion for any ad- vance.</p>
        <p>The amendment was put to the meeting, but fell through,and the recommendation of the committee was unanimously carried.</p>
        <p>THE CONDITION OF THE RIVER AT WILDERSPOOL.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read letters from Mr. J. Lyon Whittle,town clerk of Warrington, with reference to the above mat- ter, and remarked that several statements in the report were scarcely accurate. A reply was sent to Mr.Whittle's communication, and ultimately an interview took place between himselt (Mr. Ashton) and Mr. Whittle,with the result that practically speaking, the original agreement was approved.—lt was resolved that the Council's seal be affixed thereon. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN CONTINUATION SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CONTINUATION SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the scholars attending the Victoria-road Board Schools, c mtinuation cla,§ses,assem- bled in the schools for the first time this session and the awards gained by the successful students were distri- buted. There was a large attendance. Mr. J. Pritchard (chairman of the Sch.ol Board) presided, and he was supported by Mr. F. J. Norman, J.P., Mr. G. Moss (clerk), and the teachers of the classes. The Chairman, explained that they were assembled to inaugurate the seventh session for the boys, and the fourth session for the girls, of the evening schools. So far they were pleased to find that the schools had been attended with success. The numbers at their last session were larger than on any occasion sinee the classes were established.</p>
        <p>Mr. Moss read the reeort of the Inspector, which stated that the boys' school well maintained its excellent character, and that the girls' school continued to do exceedingly well. The average attendance last session was 67 boys and 50 girls, a larger number than in any preceding year. (Applause.) Mr. Norman in distributing the prizes, said, they all well remembered the gentleman who gave the prizes last year, namely their old friend Dr. McDougall. (Hear, hear.) The doctor, as they latew, had taken the keenest interest in those schools, and in the continuation classes since their inception; and had played his part in making them so successful. As regards educational matters, a charge had been made against this country, that, as a nation, they had been laggards with regard to educa- tion, and unfortunately the statement waz only too true some years ago. At the time of the Fraaco-German war it was a fact that of the two armies, placed a the field, every man could read and write. That showed the ad- vanced state of education in those countries about a quarter of a century ago, when unfortunately, the great bulk of English citizens were unlearned. The people were wrangling as to whether or not it was wise to edu- cate them. It was then decided to spread knowledge. , He (the speaker) was daily brought face to face with a number of hard-headed working man, and he was con- vinced that many of them possessed a natural ability, which if it had been properly trained when they were young, would probably, have raised them to important positions. Everything depended upon an efficient training In youth, and their value In life would greatly depend upon the atten- tion they paid to their studies. (Applause.) If the men of to-day who performed physically labori- ous work had had the advantages of a thorough educa- tion they might have held exalted positions. He asked the boys especially, as they would be the future electors of the country, to consider every Proposal placed beforel the people (no ma.ter by what Government) on its edu- cational merits. Everything possible was being done by the Runcern authorities to avail themselves of the edu- cational facilities provided. Those continuation schools had been worked very ably. and the District Council with the Technical Instruction Committee, had pushed the interests of the new building in Waterloe-road with an audacity—he did not use the word in any disrespect- ful senge—that had been the envy of much larger towns than theirs. (Applause.) The echnols were conducted very economically. Durine• the last eearen sessions theV had only coat the ratepapers directly £ll 175., and flor- in.. the last session they obtained all the advantages offered withotit the cost of a single penny. (Applause.) Mr. Norman then proceeded to distribute the prizes to the following scholars : MAL/IS.—First class for .11MP/0i and attendance : .T. G. Millinetne. H. Oliver, W. J. Melyneaus.. Fred Ward, Jelin Cooke, Sam Watten, William Oliver, John A. Hughes, Charles Glover, Ernest Brerfaton, Leonard Stubbs, and Reisert Rawlineen. Second class Harold Rogers. James E. Hughes, William Ray, Gowen Fryer, Sam Clarke, Walter Jones. George A. Lloyd. Howls Hamilton, W. Sneering. A. Lightfont, Afred Rushton,H. Houghton. and Richard Barley. Thin i clame.fer attend- ance only: George Lee. Fred Seften.Harry Carter.Thoman Paul, and Percy Douglas. The following students were successful at the examinations held under the ans- niees of the Lancashire and Cheshire Union of Inatitutea, and received certificates Arithmetic (elementary). first class James Renner. Robert Rexter, Thonsae Hedeeenek, James Edward Hughes, Walter Jones. John G. Millington, Willians James IVlelvnenx, William Oliver. Alfred Rushten, S. Wetten, and H. Woodier. Sneed (lase : A. E Austin, Albert Ball, Josen% Ball, Semite! Thomas Cartwright, Percy Douglas, R. Fryer. T. W. Gleave, C Glover, John Hind- ley, Jeaenh Inelesfield, A. Liehtfeot. G. R. Lloyd, H. Oliver. G. A. 'Poole, William Rev, R. Rawlin- son, L. Stubbs. and A. Woodier. Arithmetic junior eemmercial grade, second class: John Alfred Hitehes. Third clans : H. Hamilton, Joseph H. Kirkham. Joseph Savage, Fred E. Ward. Hand- writing, commercial grade, first class : William .Tames Melyneity. See,one cleee : John Alfred Hughes, James lionry Kirkham. .T. G. Millineten, H. Oliver, Joseph Sherh,clr.and F E Ward. Third class : 'T. P. Baxter, W,Olivr. Geeeranhy.firet (lase; : W. Oliver. S-cend v'lqs: L. Ball. T. P. Raver. D. Chadwick. Thema. ned7ne.ek.Genrge Lee.J. G. Millinvtnn.R. Oliver. Thos. Pall!, William RaY,Joaeph Sherlock, H. Reepre,and J. L. Wilson. Phrsiovranhc, second class F. E. Ward. W. Oliver Jarrt,ia rr n itTh t o . and J. G. Millington. Third class : W. McQninn and Ti. Oliver. GIRLS —Nursing and arithmetic : Ada IViimily. Arith- metic and needlework : Ada Davies. Domestic econerny and arithmetic : Mary E. Brittand. Nursing Elsie Howitty.va Mort.and Emily Turner. Arithmetic : Hilda taa:hhiing and and Elizaheth Hawthorn. Needlework : Lily si. Maac?ie Bolton. General good work : Clara Pilgrim. Nellie Dyer, Florence Billington, Dora Wilson, Ada. Hill.Lncv Rittter.Netta Waterwerth, Mary J.Bratt, Florence Parr. ciseie Stehha. Ada Bowman. Sarah E. Wilson, Gertrude Barnes. Nellie Minshull, Nellie Comes, and CiagiP Aehten. Certifieatee : Arithmetic, first elase : Elsie Hewil-1-.Ade Munriv,and Martha Sweetmen Second class : Ada Flill.El7zahoth A Mnrt, Dora Wilson, Florence Waterw.rth, Nellie Cornea. Emily Tur- ner. Hilda Rawlinson. and Annie Lyons. Handwriting, first. class : Mabel Staffer-I. Second class: Ada Mundy and Dora Wileen.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. Moulton worn-led by Mr. Moss he,r,v vote of thanks was accorded the Chairman and Mr. Norman.</p>
        <p>The latest terrnr with which we arP threatened is said tn he the musical bicycle. Mnst people will think that bicycle"; suPPlv quite ennugh mucic already. NA orches- tral effect is mnre startling and arresting that the sound of hells just behind one. Elderly ladies and gentlemen who are nnahle to bound at an instant's notice from the middle of the marl on to the pavement suffer acutely from this kind of performance. But if you have 1,, jump, it is surely better to do so to the present sort of cenmpanimPnt, than to he encnmpassed by slow music Proceeding from an arrangement of wires and pipes in the front wheel.</p>
        <p>WEsLEY's TTYmNs, Sankey'; Soncrs, Bibles, Prayer Banks and Rtmtionery in great Variety at JOHN S. GRIM'. 34, Bridge-st. &amp; 23, Buttermorket-st., Warring- ton .--Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>SIR J. T. BRUNNER, M.P., AT</head>
        <p>SIR J. T. BRUNNER, M.P., AT</p>
        <p>RUN CO R N.</p>
        <p>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION TO TECHNICEIL SCHOOL</p>
        <p>STUDENTS.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening, in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom, Greenway-road, Runcorn, the first distribu- tkin of prizes, to the successful students of the Runcorn Technical School took place. There was a large attend- ance of parents and students.—;ser.J.W. Lightburn,J.P., chairman of the Urban District Council, presided, and was supported by Sir J. T. Brunner, M.P., the Rev. W. George, Messrs. F. Wiswall, E. Imison, F. J. Norman, J.P., J. R. Frith.A. R. Norman, F. A. Lake, &amp;Marshall, Speakreas, and J.Wilson. The proceedings were opened by the singing of the National Anthem. The Chairman said they were all pleased to see so much interest taken in the meeting. He had pleasure in congratulating the people of Runcorn on the success- ful work which had been accomplished by the Technical Institute during its first session. The present session, he could assure them. would also be a successful one. Ile added that Sir John Brunner had been very liberal to that institution, and it was therefore very fitting that he should take part in the proceedings of that night, when they had what they might well call, the Runcorn students' harvest festival. Sir John Brunner, M.P., on rising was greeted with loud cheers. Be said he was always happy to come to Runcorn, because he was always satisfied that when he was invited, he was invited to take part in some good work or other. Runcorn was exceptionally high in met- ters of education. He did not forget that some twelve months ago be was surrounded by the most brilliant gathering—intellectually considered—Runcorn had ever seen. Men from all parts of the county came there to encourage and to do them honour at the time of the opening of the Institute ; and he was also very glad to be present that night to join them in celebrating—as their Chairman had called it—their first harvest festival. They had a long list of prize winners there that night, hut there were some who had passed out of the kindly, fostering care of the Technical Instruction Committee. As one he would mention a man who had done honour to Runcorn, namely, the son of the Chairman of the Technical Instruction Committee (Mr. Wiswall.) kAp- plume.) Mr.Wiswall's son had h very long list of certi- ficates, and at the age of 21 years he was its a respon- sible position under the Mancheater Ship Canal Com- pany. He (the speaker) hoped he would have a long and prosperous career. He heard, too, of a young man named Herold Davies, who had four or five times been the recipient of prizes at the hands of the Science and Art Committee, and at the age of 21 years he was an as- sistant gas engineer to the Damen Corporation. A young man named Barker, the son of a Runcorn brick- setter, had been a prize winner in the build- ing construction, and now, marvellous to relate, at the age of 21 years, he was the fore- man to the firm of contractors who built the technical institute in Runcorn. Many of the men whom he was addressing, knew very well how the practice of apprenticeship was dying out ; they knew also how extremely important and how valuable was the training which was to be got at the hands of the Technical In- struction Committee as a substitute for the toilsome work of apprenticeship of seven years. They knew too, hew superior was the training to be got from the new mode. In the old system of apprenticeship the hand and the eye were trained only, bat now the hand, eye and brain were at work. Art in Runcorn had not yet be- come distinguished. The poet says " Art is long and life is short. " Warrington was not far from there, and Runcorn might well emu- elate the work Warrington has done. A young man went to school in Warrington and soon showed his skill in the technical schoel of that town. This young man at- tracted the attention of a number of Warrington's wealthy men and five of them subscribed and decided Ito send him to Rome to continue his art studies. At Rome he became a very able and skilful sculptor, and he pro- dueed year by year some of the very best work. Some of his work was near Lime-street Station, Liver- non), and the Walker Art Gallery. They were noble figures, and were the work of Warrington Wood, a Warrington hov—(hear, hear)—who had risen from the Warrington school. In another branch ofartWa rerwrineg- ton school had sent outlLuke Fildos.whose pictures the admiration of the whole civilised world. His picture of the " Docter " had given delight and had touchee the hearts of thousands of people. There was no reason why Runcorn should not be honoured in that way, in sending students out to do work, as the men of Warring- ton did. (Hear. hear.) Looking at the prize list, he noticed more than one individual, he would pick out From the list a bey named Howard, who had greatly distinguished himself, and who was the winner of no less than five nrizes. (Applause.) Last year there were no less than 508 students registered in the books of the schen'. Not only were the evil associations kept at a distance. hut higher and nobler thoughts took their place. To have had 508 young people subjected to the school ru:es was a matter for congratulation. The good work of that institute had made all the leaders in Run- corn realise, and he hoped it would make everyone realise that the welfare of the young was the highest object at which they could aim. (Applause). Sir John Bruntier then commenced to distribute the prizes. Before concluding he remarked, that England at the present moment stood pre-eminent in the work of nursing, and he thought that the institution of the order 'of trained rmrses was a great reform. He thanked them (the ladies) for the success they had attained. Mr. Wiswall, chairman of the Technical Instruction Committee, said he had very great pleasure in propos- ing a very hearty vote of thanks to Sir John Brunner for distributing the prizes. If it had not been for Sir IJohn they could not have had an institute nor such a gathering as they had that night. A great deal had been said about him (the speaker) with reference to his con- nection with the technical school, but he must not for- get. their secretary Mr. Marshall. Great credit was due to him and the Committee. The institute was capable of doing more than it was yet doing. The classes were not as wt.!l filled as he would like them to be. He looked forward to the time when the institute would be occu- pifel in the day as well ise at night. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Imison seconded the resolution. A ro'o ef th Inks to the Chairman. nropoeed by Sir J. T. Brenner aed seconded by Mr. Speakman, brought a successful meeting to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
