      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>STORY OF A DIAMOND RING AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>STORY OF A DIAMOND RING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A MOTHER TAKES AN ACTION AGAINST HER SON.</p>
        <p>Before Deputy-Judge Crawford at the Warrington County Court 'on Wednesday, Elizabeth Monks, a widow, of 90, Wilderspool Causewa.y, claimed the return of a diamond ring or its value.£3s,lent to her son,John Monks,liceusee of the Packet House Inn' and El damage for detention. Mr. Fowler (Messrs. Robert Davies and Company) appeared on behalf of the plain- tiff,and Mr. Arthur Browne represented the defendant. Mr. Fowler, in opening the case, said it was a family dispute. The plaintiff claimed for the return of a valuable diamond ring which she lent to her son the de- fendant on the 4th July,and also £1 damages for deten- tion. On the 3rd July—which was what was called Walking Day at Warrington—the plaintiff was at the Packet House Inn, the defendant's house, and he asked her to lend him his father's ring. It seemed that it was not the first time he had borrowed it. On the following day the plaintiff took the ring to him and her daughter was present when she took the ring and she told her that she was going to lend it to her son. She took the ring to her son and a week afterwards she went for the return of it. He refused to give it her, and she had been obliged to take those proceedings for its recovery. . . .</p>
        <p>Elizabeth Monks, mother.of the defendant, stated nit she remembered the 3rd July—Walking Day—when she was at the Packet House Inn. Her son said to her "Mother, I want you to lend me my father's diamond ring for the week-end. She replied that she would do so. She did not stay very long but on leaving he said to her "Don't' forget to bring the ring to-day." She told him that she could not promise that, but she would bring it the following day. This she did, and oa Satur- day evening when the defendant was at the end of the bar, she put the ring in his hand and said to him "This is the ring you have asked me for." He put it on his finger, turned round, and went into the bar parlour. On the 11th of the same month she went for the return of the ring, and told him that she was going away on her holidays and she wanted the ring. He refused to give it to her, and said he did not intend to do so at present. She was unable to get the ring, and after she returned from Rhyl she again went for it on the 30th of the month. He told her that he did not intend to part with the ring. When she asked him the reason he said he was the eldest son and had the most right to it. She then asked him whether he thought she was going to let him keep it when she did not know what she might want. She had kept the ring since her husband's death nine years ago.—ln cross-e. -eination she admitted that she had given her second son a " snake "ring, but that was in exchange far £l2 he gave her. She had not given the second son some studs but had lent them to him.</p>
        <p>Sarah Dunbobbin, daughter of the Dlaintiff,stated that she was at her brother's house on Walking Day when she heard him ask for the loan of their father's dia- mond ring. Her mother acceded to the request. Ada Dunbobbin, grand-daughter to the plaintiff, stated that during the absence of her grandmother at Rhyl, the defendant said he supposed there had been a fearful row because he had not given up the ring. She told him there had been, and the defendant replied that he would take care of the ring until plaintiff came back. For the defence, Mr. Browne stated that the ring had been lent to his client once before when he returned it. On Walking Day, although he had no nee lof it, Mr. Monks again asked for the loan of it and to his surprise when she came with it she said "Here Jack I will give it thee." His (Mr. Browne's) client knew that his mother had distributed his father's jewellery and he took it as a gift. In addition to that, when the plaintiff asked him for the ring she only asked him to lend her the ring, and she ;caul I give it him back. John Monks, licensee of the Packet House Inn, gave evidence in corroboration of his solicitor's statement. When she told him that she would give him the ring he said "Thank you." A week after- wards his mother came to him and said " Jack I want you to let me have that ring fcr Fleming's. We are going to Rhyl and I will bring it you back." He told his mother that she o,ight to be ashamed to ask for it for Fleming, his brother-in-law, and he was determined not to give it up.—ln answer to the Judge, witness said he did not make any further remarks to his mother when she gave him the ring, beyoni "thank you." He denied the statement attributed to him by Ada Dunbobbin.</p>
        <p>Arthur Dunham and Miss Eliz;bsth Monks, daughter of the defendant also gave evidoice. They stated that they were preent when the ring was handed to tlie defendant, and that the plaintiff said she would give it to him.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne thought His Honour could come to no other conclusion but that the ring was a Oft after the evidence of Dunham, who was an independent witness, and had been asked to given evidence by the plaintiff's solicitor.</p>
        <p>His gonnur summed up at considerable length. He said he had given the case very great attention and he felt extremely sorry that mother and son should have a quarrel over such a matter. He accepted in its main line, the evidence of the man Dunham, but he did not think it carried the case very far in the defendant's favour. Mr. Monks never appeared to have asked his mother for the jewellery and seemed to have trusted to his mother to do what was right and fair by her children with respect to the gifts. He only asked for the loan of it and he never believed when she was to bring it to him that it was as a gift. There was also no reason why the plaintiff shuld have come to the sudden determina- tion to give him the ring. It was rather extraordinary that if the defendant theuebt she had given it to him he should only say "Thank you" and no more. It was very easy for a misinterpretation to have been put on the plaintiff's words when she handed the ring to the de- fendant. She might have said" There Jack, there it is for yea," meaning that it was his temporarily in obedi- ence to the promise she had made to him. That might account for the discrepancy in the evidence. As there apparently was a quarrel existing between the de- fendant and his brother-in-law named Fleming he (his Honour) could quite understand the defendant refusing to give up the ring when he was asked. He must come to the conclusion that the plaintiff was entitled to suc- ceed, and he would give judgment for the plaintiff for the return of the ring with costs. He would not allow the claim for damages for detention.</p>
        <p>The ring was then handed over by Mr. Browne. His Honour expressed the hope that the unhappy dispute between mother and son would be put an end to. It was only reasonable that the eldest son should expect to have his father's ring in course of time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LOCAL HARVEST FESTIVALS. WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>LOCAL HARVEST FESTIVALS. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Continuation harvest festival services were held on Sunday, morning and evening, at the Parish Church and at St. Paul's, St. Barnabas', St. Ann's, St. Peter's, and Holy Trinity Churches. There were large congre- gations, particularly at the evening services. In several cases the decorations had been added to, and fresh flowers and fruit introduced. Services were con- tinued at the Parish Church,where the preacher was the Rev. C. C. Elcum, vicar of St. Agnes,Liverpool. In the afternoon a special service was held for the children connected with the various Sunday schools in the parish, an address being delivered by the Rev. A. A. Markham. At the evening service the church was filled almost to overflowing. The Rev. Hylton Stewart, vicar of New Brighton, occupied the pulpit, and an earnest appeal was made on behalf of the clergy fund. A hearty response was forthcoming, and the aggregate amount at the whole ot the services reached £43, this being the highest Rum takln for some years. An anthem was ably rendered by the choir, whose members under Mr. W. S. Woods' tuition have reached a high state of perfection. The preacher at St. Paul's, Church was the Rev. T. M. R. Young-Hughes, a former curate of the parish, and now Vicar of St. Bride's, Glasgow. He preached two excellent sermons, which were listened to with rapt attention. The choir were never7heard to greater ad- vantage than on Sunday evening, the music of the Magnificat, Nunc Dirnittis, and anthem being most ably and tastefully sung. Mr. Ernest Wilkes was at the organ. At St. Peter's Church, reference was made to the Queen having reigned 80 years, and the Pope's reply re Anglican Orders. The Nitinnal Anthem was sung at the commencement of the service.</p>
        <p>LYMM BAPTIST CHAPEL. The Lymm Higher- lane Baptists held their harvest thankcgiving services on Sunday, the preacher, being Mr. Hollingshead, of Manchester. The sacred °edifice was tastefully decorated for the occasion, and at the morning service the special anthem." Praise ye the Lord." was sung by the choir, Mr. Bates conducting. Special praise is due to Mr. Bates for the trouble he has taken with the chair to get it to its present state of efficiency. There was a good congreiption at each service. LATCHFORD WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>_ - On Sunday the annual thanksgiving servic9s for the in-gathering of the harvest were hold at Latchford Wes- leyan Church. The preacher morning and evening was the Rev. G. Harbot!le, one of the newly-appointed ministers of the cir.cuit,who delivered two eloquent ser- mons. At tbe morning service he Preached from the text "He that g,oeth forth and weeneth,bea.ring precious seed shall doubtless come again with rejoicing, bringinc, his sheaves with him " Psalm 126, 6v., while the words cm which his evening's discourse were based were " And behold there came a voice unto him and said 'What doth thou here,Elijah ?' " 1 Kings,l9c.l3v. There were good congregations. Pretty floral decorations inside the church were carried out by the Misses Henshall. Miss Hartley, Miss Whittaker. Mrs. Gornall, Mr. J. Corbett. Mr. B. Walker, and Mr. F. Blease. Fruit, flowers, &amp;c., were contributed by Mr. and Mrs. Benshall, Mr. and Mrs. Hartley, Mr. and Mrs. Gornall, Mr. and Mrs. Ellison, Miss Stanley. Miss Fletcher, Mrs. M. Browne, Mr.J. Cor- bett, Mr. J. Lowndes, Mr. D. Cliffs, and Mr.W.Pearson. A ni,.o selection of plants was also lent by Mrs. Broad- bent, of " The Flollies." During the services several anthems were well sung by the choir, and Mr. A. Ford preqded at the organ. Collections were taken on behalf nf the Circuit Fund.</p>
        <p>The revenue returns for the six months ended on Wednesday show an increase of £1,262,921 as compared with the corresponding period ot last year.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUN CIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Runcorn Urban District Council was held on Thursday morning in the Council chamber. Mr. J. W. Lightburn presided,and there were also present the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. J. H. Grice, J. Pritchard,J. H. Kirkham, F. Wiswall, J. Horsfield,W. Handley, W. Sweetman, D. Bisbrown, E. Imison G. Hewitt and Wilson Mr. F. A. Lake (law clerk; Mr. E. Marshall (sec.) and Mr. J. Wilding (surveyor.)</p>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. McDougall in his report stated that the number of deaths registered during the past month was 18, of which one was an Inquest case. Of the 17 deaths 4 were due to congenital debility, 2 each to bronchitis, disease of the liver, disease of the kidney, typhoid fever ; one each to scarlatina, pnuemonia, phithisis, infantile diarrhoea and heart disease. The 18 deaths showed an average death rtite for the twelve months of 11.6. and exclusive of inquest case of 11. There had heen 49 births registered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>HIGHWAYS AND BUILDING COMMITTEE,</head>
        <p>HIGHWAYS AND BUILDING COMMITTEE,</p>
        <p>Mr. Bisbrown moved the adoption of the Highways minutes.—The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi asked why the sub- committee appointed to aperoach the Gas Company con- cerning the incandescent light had not reported.—The Chairman: They have nothing to report. They could not meet the Gas Company because Mr. Frith, the manager was away on his holidays.—Dr. Gastaldi pro- posed as an amendment, that the sub-committee be em- powered to appeal to the company for a reduction in the price of gas in the town.—The Chairman : The matter doee not arise out of the minutes and he weuld rule the Rev. Dr. out of order.—The minutes were then adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>HEALTH COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>HEALTH COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee recommended that the tender of Mr. J. Green for painting, &amp;c., required to be done at the Matron's house at the Hospital be accented ; and that notices be printed and distributed warning persons against depositing wall paper in public places, and re- quiring;the same to be destroyed.—Mr. Elandley, in ro- posing the minutes of the above committee, said com- plaints had b en made about wall papers being put in public places. The practice might prove injurious to health and on this account they had issued notices. With regard to the general health of the town he was sorry the notification received last month numbered 23 against 11 in August. This seemed a serious addition to the previous month's returns. It was more serious that eight of them were notified as typhoid fever, but this number when reduced to proper limits showed that there were only three or four typhoid cases and the others were continued fever. There had been two deaths from typhoid. The commit- tee had taken steps to prevent the spread of the disease, and it was satisfactory to know that there had been no further extension,although it was rampant in Widnes.— Dr. Gastaldi seconded the proposition which was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The committee reported that a communication had been received from the Runcorn School Board inviting the Council and the Technical Instruction Committee to confer with them on the advisability of providing secon- dary education for the tosvn,and they recommended that Messrs. Wiswall,lmison. Handley, Wilson, Kirkham, and Rawlinson be deputed to confer with the School Board on the matter.—Mr. Wiswall proposed the adoption of the above minutes,anu said there was much obscurity as to secondary education hut the subject was one for conference before they said mach. The committee would be certainly careful in their proceedings, and would do nothing without fully informing the Council. —Mr. Wilson seconded.—The motion was carried.</p>
        <p>CHAIRMAN'S COMMUNICATIONS.</p>
        <p>These consisted of an order of the Local Government Board constituting a Port Sanitary Authority for the Port of Manchester for three years. (Manchester four members, Salford four members,Stretford one member); also sancYlun of the Leal Government Board to the bor- rowing of the sum of £6,432 repayable in ten years for works of private street improvement, and cancelling the sanction to the borrowing of a like amount for seven years, granted on the 10th ultimo.—The Chairman said the Local Government Board desired to limit the period of the borrowing of the money to 7 years, but as the re- sult of strong representations as to the condition of trade of the town they extended it to 10 years. (hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BRIDGE FARES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bisbrown asked if any reply had been received from the L. and N.W. Railway Company in reference to their memorial praying for the reduction in the charges made for pedestrians crossing the bridge.—The Chair- man : No reply has been yet received.</p>
        <p>_ _ DR. GASTALDI AND THE TRADES' COMIIITTEE.-" A FARCE."</p>
        <p>Thell.v. Dr. Gastaldi asked if the Trades' Committee had done anything.—The Chairman: We have no report to submit. —Dr. Gastaldi : Then I say they are doing nothing.—The Chairman: That is not a proper conclu- sion to arrive at.—Mr. Imison : Was not Dr. Gastaldi a member of the Committee ?—Dr. Gastaldi said be was not and was therefore obliged to ask for information. Questions were put to him as to what was being done seeing the Committee had been in existence six months. He was extremely anxious on the subject. Six months had elapsed, and they had heard nothing. The matter was altogether a "farce."— The Chairman said the information could easily be ob- tained in a proper manner. They were all equally in- terested in the welfare of the town. The Chairman of the sub-committee would give the necessary informs- tion.—Mr. Bisbrown. demurred to the remarks of the Rev. Doctor. Every effort had been made to bring trade into thp town, and they (the committee) were earnest in their efferts.—Mr. Wiswall said Dr. Gastaldi had charged the committee with being a farce —Dr. Gastaldi: It is --Mr. Wiswall, continuing, said the words were uncalled for. The committee had tried every inducement to bring trade into the town, and it was a delicate matter to in- terfere with the conducting of trade. It; required the committee to move with perfect discreticn. To the Doctor's mind the committee might be doing nothing, but he assured them, that it was doing a useful work, and it moved with great care and discretion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN LAY-BY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN LAY-BY.</p>
        <p>Dr. Gastaldi asked if the Ship Canal in the vicinity of the lay-bye had been dredged so as to allow ships of 24 feet draft, to approach it.—Mr. Wilding stated that the work was expected to be executed that afternoon.</p>
        <p>TEE PRICE OF RUNCORN GAS. Dr. Gastaldi moved that the sub-committee appointed to meet the directors of the Gas Company with reference to the incandescent light be empowered by the Council to ask for the reduction in the price of gas for the town. They paid in this the 19th- century an extraordinary price, namely 34. 4d.. whereas Widnes got the same quantity for is. 10d. Something should be done in the direction indicated.—The Chairman : A committee was formed some time ago to wait on the Gas Company,and Dr. Gastaldi was its chairman. On that occasion ar- raegements were made for an interview between the committee and the Gas Company. The chairman of the directors ot the company came from London to mect them. The committee did not go because they said they had no case. The Secretary of the Council was sent to apologise for their non-appear- ance. If energy had been thrown into the matter at the time something might have been done.—Dr. Gastaldi said the reason they did not meet the directors was be- cause they had not been previously called together to arrange what they should do. They simply received a letter asking them to meet the ras directors. Was that a proper :mode of business ? From what the had heard, the majority of the Council were not determined to ask for a reduction. He thought it was scandalous that they should have to pay 3s. 4d. per 1,000 feet.—Mr. Wiswall said he objected to the Council being turned into a de- bating society.—Dr. Gastaldl gave notice that at the next meeting of the Council he would bring the matter forward. Tnis was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>W ESTON.</head>
        <p>W ESTON.</p>
        <p>BAZAAR.—A two days' bazaar was held on Wednesday and Thursday in the schoolroom, Weston, to raise funds for the repairing; painting, and decorating of the Wes- leyan Chapel and Schools at Weston. There was a large attendance on the opening day. Mr. W. Oulton.J.P.,of Liverpool. after a brief speech on village Methodism. declared the bazaar open. The following were the stall- holders :—Congregational Stalls, Mrs. Hazlehurst,Mrs.S. Dodd, Mrs.D. Kirkham, Mrs. Norcott, Mrs. John Smith, Mrs. John Mort, Mrs. T. Littletnore ; Mts. James Ball, Mrs. 0. Johnson, Mrs. John Ball, Mrs. W. Brown, Mrs. James Mort, Mrs. John Spruce, Mrs. E. Onions. Re- freshment Stall, Mrs. John Griffith, Mrs. Joseph Bazley, Miss H. Fitton, Miss B. Cottrall, Miss M. Rilgrass, Miss S. Dodd, Miss Mort, Mrs. Nesbitt, Mrs. Price, Mrs, Walker. Dairy Stall, Mrs. Jo.eph Ball. Miss Dunbabin, Miss E. Griffith, Miss Jessie Griffith, Mr. William Dun- babin, Mr. John Mort. Rummage Stall, Mrs. T. Ford, Mrs. Levis, Mrs. Aldridge, Mr. John Smith, Mr. William Griffith, Mr. T. Ravenscroft. Toy Stall, Mrs. T. Loath, Miss Ford, Miss Savage, Mies C. Ball, Miss Polly Griffith. Gallery of Art and Curiosity Shop, Miss Emily Johnson, Miss Lewis,Miss Dutton,Miss Aldridge, Mr.Jantes Somer. ville. Bran-tub, Miss E Smith,Miss E Wreneh,Miss N. Lightfoot. Shooting Gallery, Messrs. E. Griffith and J. Dunbabin. Weighing Machine, the Mis.es Mort and Jones. Concerts were given by the Prize Glee Party and others, in the Infants' schoolroom'at intervals. Messrs. F. Fitton, T. Ward, S Dodd, and W. Kirkham carried out the arrangements for the decorations. On Thursday, Mr. J. R. Frith. of Runcorn, opened the second day's bazaar —The hazaar was opened for the second day, on Thursday,by Mr. J. R. Frith of Runcorn. The proceed- ings were opened by prayer, and the Rev. W. George in a brief speech introduced Mr.Frith to the people present. Mr. Frith said he must congratulate them on the success of their first bazaar. Dealing with education he said the government grant on the day school was very satis- factory, and showed the good work that was being done by the school. In order to how thip, H.M. Inspector h d announced that no examination would be required next year. (Hear. hear.)—Mr. J. Ball proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Fritk for opening the bazaar.—Rev, T. Brackenbury seconded and it was warmly a.cttord4— The Rev.W.George then announcod that he had recetv.ed • a subscription of £5 that afternoon as a contrinutton from Mr. Frith. (Loud applause.)—On IVadnesdAY the handsome sum of £B2 12s. id. w Oulton,as taken. Mr. : J.P., of Liverpool, contributed £5.</p>
