        <head>F RODS 'IAA'.</head>
        <p>F RODS 'IAA'.</p>
        <p>THANKSGIVING SERVICES.</p>
        <p>The annual harvest thanksgiving services in connec- nection with the Frodsuam Parish Church were held on Sunday, when the Vicar the Rev. J. Blogg, preached the sermons.</p>
        <p>PETTY SESSIONS. WEDNESDAY.—Before Messrs. Reynolds and A. Thomas. ALLEGED ADULTERATION OE MlLlE.—Thomas Andrews was summoned for selling milk adulterated with 3 per cent. of water on August 14th.—Mr. W. J. Ballard, in- spector,said he called at defendant's house on the date named, and asked for two glasses of milk. He paid for them and tole Mrs.Anelrews that be was going to analyse the milk. The county analyst's certificate showed that the sample contained three per cent. of added water. Mr. H. M. Linaker,who defended,said when the measure was put in the milk pan, and taken out again, it would carry with it a certain amount of cream. There was no water added SO tar as the defendant was concerned. Witnesses were called in support of this statement and the case was ultimately dismissed. ASSAULT.—WiIIiam Woodward, Cliff View Cottages, was summoned for assaulting Llamas Atkinson, on the sth September. Cemplainan':. said on the date named he was in the Cheshire Cheese.lnn. The defendant was there and knocked his own glass of beer over. He wanted witness to supply and pay for another glass for him. This complainant refused to do, so on him leaving, the defendant pushed him and he fell. His ribs were injured and he had been under medical treat- ment for the past fortnight. After a number of witnesses were galled for the defence, the magistrates decided to dismiss the case.—Mary Davies, Fredsham Bridge, was summoned for assaulting Mary Moore, on September 12th, and a cross summons was heard agatlast Davies. In the first case Davies said that Moore struck her, knocked her down, kicked her on the legs, pushed her against some rails, and her forehead WeB badly bruised.—ln the second case, Moore said, the quarrel was commenced by Davies striking her. Witness haat to defend herself.—Tne magistrates bound each of them over to keep the peace for six months in their OWLI recognizance et £5 each and to pay their. own costs. IVIFE DESERTIoN : A SAD CAM—A maintenance order of 7s. 6d. per week was granted to Rebecca fhoreleY). • against her husband Allred Thornley for deserting her and,their .three children.-11rs. Thoreley, who is totally blind said her husband deserted her NIX weeks ago,since then she had not received a copper from him. Her children were respectively aged 8 years, 2 years, and 4 mc,tichs.The defendant earned,lBs.a week when in work.</p>
