      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY.—Hefore Alderman Hirrison (in the chair) and Alderman Wright. ILLTREITMENT OF A HORSE.—John Edwards, 29, Cart- wright-street, was charged with cruelly working a horse on the 11th September.—Defendant admitted that the horse was a little sore.—lnspector Luckings, R.S.P.C.A., deposed that , on the date named be saw defendant at Arplev in charge of a bay-horse attached to a load of sand. It was flinching and had a sore back. He found two old wounds discharging matter, aud in a very bad state indeed.—P.C. Beecham corroborated and stated that he thought the horse was in pain.—A fine of 111 s including costs was Imposed.—William Cliffe, 'bus driver 351,Knutsford-road. was charged with workin,- a horse in an unfit state ou the 15th September; William RusselLiridge-st., 'bus proprietor, was charged with causing the horse to be worked.—Both pleaded not guilty.—P.C. Deahay stated that at 121 on Tuesday afternoon the 15th inst, he was in Horsemarke' -street and noticed Cliffe driving two horses attached to a 'bus. One horse was flinching as if in pain and he examined it. He found on the off wither a wound about the size of a ss. piece. He pointed it out to Cliffe vilso sad there was a plate under the collar and the horse was ill right with that on. He then took out the plate and witness found on is a quantity of blozd and matter from the wound, which was apparently an old one ler Am open. Witness t.sld defendant he would be reported.—Sergt. Rowbottorn said that the horse had been taken out soon after, and he SAW it himself. There was a wound as stated and the pate had matter and blood upon it. Cliffe said it could not hurt the horse because it was a patent to prevent such a thing. Witness afterwards saw Mr. Russell. who admitted sending the horse out.—lnspector Bilson, R.5.P.0,A., said that he saw the horse on the 16th inst., the day after it was reported, and examined the wound which was healed over. It was an old wound but if it re-opened the horse would be in an unfit state to work.—Mr. Russell said that when he sent the horse out it was in a fit state to work and he put the plate ' on as he had done before. He did not think there was any possibility of anything happening to the horse. The wound was on the off-side of the off-horse where it could be plainly seen. Pilsen told him (Russell), that he could work the horse with pads on but not with the plate.—An ostler em- ployed by Russeil corroborated as to Bilson saying this.— Inspector Bilson denied using the words.—The magis- trates said there was sufficient doubt in the case to justify them in dismissing the summons—Joseph Turner, 77, Man- chester-road, was charged with cruelly working a horse in an unfit state on the 17th September.—lnspector Luckings said that on the 17th September he saw the defendant in Bridge- street in charge of a mare with a cover,cl cart. It was flinch- ing and had a wound on the top of the neck, which the collar pressed intl. Another on the near shoulder and another near the tail. Defendant said he would take it home at once and did so. The horse WAS in much pain and quite unfit to work. —P.C. Delahay corroborated.—lnspector Bilson stated that defendant had visited him and wanted the case to be set- tled.— Defendant was fined lOs and costs, A NEIGHBOURS' Row.—Cecilia Mitchell, Welch's-yard, was charged with using threats to Catherine Riley, 4. Old-road, on the 21st September.—Complainant stated that defendant used horrible threats and called her mother in support of her statement.—Defendant denied the offence.end also cal:ed her mother to prove that there had been a tong-standing quarrel between Use parties —The Bench bound defendant over to keep the peace for six months in one surety of £5. Moxpev.—Before the Mayor (in the chair). Alderman Marrison, Alderman Francomb, Mr. F. Monks, Mr. J. W. Hayward and Mr. S. Wright THEFT BY A WOMAN. —Elizabeth Longshaw, 63 Howard- street, was charged with stealing from the shop of Catherine Gibbon, Mersey-street, on Sa,urday night, a roan's coat and vest, v -lue les 64. —Prisoner pleaded guilty —The Chief Constable stated that pris,ner came in the shop but pur- chased nothing and went out. After her departure the arti -1,-s were missed. Detective Barl-me and P.C. Lewis afterwardsjapprehendedherlwith the coat in her passession.— Prisoner was rent to gaol for a month with hard !about, DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—ror being guilty of this offence on divers dates the following were fined :—Thomas Clancy. 34 Poster-street. fined Sc. and costs : Patrick Burke, 9 Gas-street, fined the costs; this defendant had been teetotal for 12 months but. had indugled in port wine believing that he was still keeping the pledge. DESERTION —Arthur Stirk and Edward Cannon were charged with being deserter, from the 2nd Battalion of the East Yorkshire Regiment stationed at Curragh Camp.—P.O. Turner proved the case and defendants were remandel to fitrangeways to await escort.—Turner was recommended for the usual reward. - -</p>
        <p>DRUNK IN CKARGE.—For being drunk In charge of a horse and cart in Winwiek-street, on the 19th Sep ember, David Plinston. lack Aikin-str-et. was fined 51. and costs.—P.o Baxendale proved the case. DISORDERLINKIS.—DanieI 11lichens. 15, Powys-street, was charged with being disorderly in Powys-street on the 20th September. —He pleaded guilty and evidence as to his rough conduct was given by a Mrs. Smith.—Defendant was b3und over to keep the peace for 6 months and pay costs. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>• FIGHTIN6.—Bridget O'Brie c,), Green s run, and Annie Bess. Crossley-et.. were charged with fighting la Dial-st. on the 21st September.—The first defendant was discharged, and the case of Annie Roes, wtio made her 44th appearance was adjourned for a week as she promised to loave the town at once. _ _</p>
        <p>Turspir.—Before Ur. B. J. Guest (In the chair) and Dr. _ Smith. _ .... . _ .</p>
        <p>AN ARMY OFTExca.—John Davies. a middle-aged man, was charged with being a soldier in the army reserve, and having failed in December 1833 and March 1894 to apply in re- spect o! payment in the army reserve.—A Sergeant from the Barracks produced a docuinent proving that ci.fen lant, had failed to report himself on the dates named. —Defendant stated ;tilt he had written reporting himself and re- ceived no answer.—The magistrates inflicted a penalty of 41s. and costs. _ _</p>
        <p>_ SCHOOL 01.11ES.—Wi1l1am Thompson wts finel 55. for negle:ting to send his child regularly to scho3l.</p>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. —Be'oro Mr. T. J Gillespie ( n the chit's.), Colonel Wood, Messrs. B. Stone, C. B. F. Borroa, and J. W.</p>
        <p>It mmatt.</p>
        <p>JURY LISTS.—The lists of the names of those persons only qualified to serve as jurymen in the neighbouring townships were presented by the various overseers, of the respective townships and were accepted. FIRST COURT</p>
        <p>Before Mr. T. J. Gillespie_ (elsairrean), Mr. R. St me and</p>
        <p>Mr. .1. W. Emmett.</p>
        <p>_ A Bow ix FAIRCLOUGH-STREET.—The magistrates were engaged for.same considet able time in hearieg cues in which Edward Wilcocks, Fairclough-street, Bariestown was charged with assaulting; Marriet Bedworth. on the 11th inst. and also with a-saultlng Bachael Pendar at the sane time and place. A cross-summons charged Rachael Pendar with assaulting Wi'cocks. Mr. Stuart. solicitor. represented Pend tr. and Mr. Percy Davies. solicitor, Warrington. appeared on behalf ot Wilcocks. In st tting the case Mr. Stuart informed the Bench that Mrs. Pendar resided next door to Wilcocks. Mrs. Bedworth, her dauglater, lived with his client On the cats named Mrs. Pendar was in the kitchen nursing her d sughter's baby and Wilcocks came up to her in a menacing way and she pushed him away. R e then took hold of her arm and hit her in the face. The baby was knocked out of her arms and she fell to the ground. Harriet Bedworth then came to look after her baby and Wilcocks struck her on the side or the face,and kn icked her down, practically senseless. He gave her a black eye and sho afterwards went to the police and showed her bruises.—Rachael Pendar gave evidence in support of her solicitdr's statement. She gave Wilcock, no provocation to assault her ; she merely pushed her away.—Frank Pendar, a boy, spoke to seeing the assault and ea cling the baby in Ws arms.—Mr and Mrs. Bedworth also gave evidence of an assault by Wilcocks.—James Briscow at the outset refused to give evidence until his expenses were paid. On being promised them by Mr. Stuart. he said he saw both Wilcoeks and Bedworth on the floor right opposite his door. He saw Wilcock. strike Mrs. Bedworth blow on the fa^e. —John Hayes stated that he saw Wilewsks strike Mrs Bedworth.—Mr. Davies for the defence, contended that Mrs. Pendar was responsible for what Utak place, since she initiated it be striking Wilcocks without ativ provocation whatever. Ile though it was a case in ‘,/liich both piffles should be bound over to keep the peace.</p>
        <p>If Wileocks had not been struck he would not have get ex- cited.—Mri. Howcroft, 62. Fairelough-st., stated thit she saw Mrs peular first strike Wileocks when h'* was passing Mrs. Pendar's door. She did not see him riive her any provo- cation for striking him . Mrs. Pendar hid no a baby i her arms. Witness did not see the end 3f the aff-dr. Th. migis- trittes retired to consider their d4cision. and on their re- turn the Bench fined Wilcocks ss. and oits for aseaulting Mrs. Pendar ; ss. and costs for assaulting Mrs. Bedworth ; and dismissed the cross-summons acrailst Mrs. Pendar. SRCONO COURT,</p>
        <p>Before !Colonel Wood (in the chair), and Mr. C. B. ; Forron.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DIRORDERT,Y —Joseph Scott. Peter Bothwell. Stephen Weave. Thomas Pilling. Mary O'Brien. and Martin Mansion were all found guilty of drunkenness and disorderly conduct, and were fined, UNAUTZZLED Dons —James Lowe and Simeon Talbot were severally fined for having allowed their (Inks totwander at large In Oolbwne. minus of collars and muzzles _</p>
        <p>DRlrmcrsr RlDER.—James Cunliffe was charged with having been drunk whilst in charge of a horse on the 7th inst.—He pleaded truilty.—&amp; constable informed the magis- trates that he saw the defendant'on the eav named riding a horse in Wargrave-read. Newten. whilst drunk. He siw the defndant sway from one side of the horse to the e11e... and then fall from its back to the ground. His right foot remained in the stirrup, and he was drawei some distance by the horse It was a miracle almost that he was not killed, and had no one been the, to rescue him from his predica- ment, he would most certainly have been very much injured. —The Chairman said the conduct of the defendant had not only been very dangerous to himself, but also to the public and he would be fined ss. and Posts. BTCYCLI4T ON THE FOO,PLTH —Frederick Gandolpher, youth, was lined Is. and costa for having ridden a bicycle on a footpath at Winwlek on the Bth inst. Cows STRAYING ON THE MGRWAY.--JSMAS and Thomas Herfoot. farmers, were surnmoncd for allowing three CJWS to stray upon the the highway at Winwiek on the 14th Se Item- ber.—A constable spoke te seeing the cowr straving.oa the highway at Winwiek k^own as Hollins-lane about 7 30 in the morning.—Defendants did not deny the offenee.but corn- olained of persons brealring down the hedges of their fields which enabled the cattle to get out on the highway.—The magistrates said they were hound to convict, but had decided to inflic` only a nominal amount, namely 55., the costs of the summons.</p>
        <p>Bitscimvotre Boa's.—Two boys named James Southern and Edwere James.were charged with wilfully lare'king two telegraphic insulators at Newton on the 20th inst.—P.C. Turner deposedto seeing the boys deliberately throw stones at two telegraph poles, and break two insulators. The offenders went to a third pole. but before they c Wel throw At that one witness caught them and took their names—Mr. WearinW. engineer, attached to the Liverpool Post Office etaff, said he represented the Postmaster General. who was the prosecutor in this case. He had been instructed to say to their worships that since April a very large number of insulators hed been brnken, especially in the district of Newton, and the Post Office authorities had had to ask the police to give them as- sistance in endeavouring to check it. In this instance the authorities did not wish the magistrates to deal harshly with the lads ; all they wanted was a cenyiction in order that other boys who were inclined to offend would be deterred.—The Chairman: I suppose t'es break ine of these insnlaters inter- feres with the telegraphic current.—Witness: When a large number are bmken your worship it does interfere very materially.—The bench, taking into consideration the wish of the Posteraster General that the punishment slionld not be severe, ordered the boys to pay the costs, h,t cautioned them that if brought up again on a similar chargs they would be severely dealt ye th Ax UNWELCIM ft Lope.—Peter Sutton. of Burtenwond, was summoned by Bliza.beth Rothwell, of the same loealitv for haying made Use of threats towards ber oe the 19th Sep- tember. 9 here wee a counter charge of assault hv Sutton against Mrs. Rothwell. and two of her relative, Wm. and James Rothwell..-Mr Riley. who stoppered for the Rothwells first ref srred to the alleged threats hy Sutton and said that a few weeks prior to the 19th September Sutton had been a lodger in the house of the female at Isurtonwood On t'ae 18th he brought to a climax a long course of objec ionable conduct by coming home drunk using threats towards Mrs. Rothwell, and laming a favourite dog belonging to the family by kicking it. Mrs. Rothwell told him he must go the next day, sayiug she would not have him in her house any longer. He le't the house the next day but retureed in the evening, at a late hour and tried to get into the house first by the back door, and then be the front but failed. Whilst he was at the front Wm. and James Rethwell came to the house to ask Mrs. Rothwell to visit, a dying relative, and when Sutton saw them coming he got it into his head that they were coming to interfere with him. He thereupon opened a volley of abuse unon all three of them uttering the most serious threats against Mr 3.liothwell.who was now afraid of him, and had taken that action to get preteotion from him. Prosenttrix and her daughter Hlizeheth both bore out the statement of Mr. Rilev.—Th case for Sutton against the Rothwells was then taken Sutton allege 1 that he got lap el the morning of the 19th Sept,mber, and seeing th at Mrs. Rothwell was not getting any breekfast, he w_.nt out. She called him b ick and told him to take his clothes, but he re• plied that he would first of all get fresh lodgines She said she would send his clothes away if lie did not take them, but as he had nowhere to take them he left the house intending to eall for them at night time. When he did call the house was loekerl, and although he tried both doors he could no get in. He shouted out and rung the hell, and at last Mrs. Rothwell appeared at the window above. He askei her to open the door and give him his clothes. but she would not. He thoneht. he heard someone leave the house by the beck doer whilst he was waiting at the front, and shortly afterwards the two other Roth. wells came to the front door. He heard the elder Rothwell says " get hold of him." whereupon witness tried to get away, thinking they were goine to 'heat him In doing so he draeged one of them about 20 7,-es-ds along the road, hut eventually the other came up and they then both assaulted him. They got him down and thumped and kicked him brutally. Mrs. Rothwell also came up at the time, and likewise assaulted him.—He denie 1 in cross examination that he had used en open knife in the struggle, and had Injured the head of the elder Rothwell with it. He hadn't a knife in his possession at. she time.—James Houghtop spoke to seeing all the three Rothwells assaulting Sntton, who had previously made an ineffectual endeavnur to escape. He heard the daughter shout out "Get at him while you can." and saw them all kic me htm.—Taeses and William II ath- well, and Mrs Rothwell all denied the cherges of assault and declared that h did have an ()len class, knife in his hand on the occasion. - The magistrates, after hearing the whole of the evidence, said they considered the case against Sutton proved and bound him over to keep the peace for six months lin two sureties of £5 end also to pay the costs. They dis- missed all the three counter charges against the Rothwells.</p>
      </div>
