      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>DATIESTIURY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>DATIESTIURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.--Flefore Mr. J. C. Parr (chairman), Captain Rogers, Messrs. W. Long, C. li..Broadbent,. John White, and</p>
        <p>71. Neild.</p>
        <p>JIITtY LISTS.—The overseers for the several townshins the division submitted lists of persons qualified to serve on juries. There were no objeetions and the lists as submitted were annroved of by the nmestratas. DRUNKENNESB.—Tohn Filters', of Walton Inferior was charged with being drunk at Walton on the 6th September. He was fined 2.. Cd and costs.</p>
        <p>rHE nESIJLT OF STELLING APPLES.—Bdward Mills, Samuel Williams, and John Wright all of Barnton, were summoned at the Instance of Richard Horton, of Little Leivh with stealing a cptantiev oapnles or he value n' si xnence,the property of John Morton, on the 13th September. Mills was also chsrged with assaulting Richard Horton ; J. Wright with a similar offence: and Williams with assault- ing Jesse Unswerth. solicitor, prosecuted. and Mr. Pere" Davies defended. In stating the case. Mr. Unsworth informed the Bench that, Mr. John Horton was a farmer and resided at Weaver View Farm, Little Leigh. On Sunday, the 13th of this month, the three sons 'f Mr. Hortnn were in the back yard of the farm and they heard a noise. One of the seas went to the orchard, and reportel somethine to his brothers. They went together to the orchard and saw the three defendants in a field in the act of tekingapples from the trees Their pockets were hulking out with the fruit they had taken from the trees. They went to the threedefendants ' and wanted te know what they were doing and told them to go away. They replied that they weuld not go away uatil they had get what they wentsel. One of the complainant's sons asked them to nut down the apples they had taken, and then Mills, who was the ringleader of the rest made a rush at Richard Horton. one of the sons. and !nee him a violent blow on the fee.. Williams afterwards made a rush at Jesse Horton, another son, and struek at him. Jesse struck Nice again and pushed Williams down. The three defendants afterwerds commenced to walk away and the three bmthers walked after them. Richard Horton said he would go for the malice whereupon Mills struck him another violent blow in the mouth cutting his lip open, with the result that. he had to go to Dr Smith, of Weaverham and have it stitched up The pre ice officer was sent for hut when he arrive:l the three defendants had disappeared. Mr. John Horton had been annoy •d for some time past by chemical labourers from Barnton who came round on Sundays and seemed to think they (mull do what they liked with other peeple's property. He asked that the defendaete should be severely dealt with. Wright. had been warned off the ground on a previous octet- sion.—Richar i Horton. Jesse Horton, and Herbert Horton. sons of John Horton, gave evidence emperting Mr. Unsworth's statement. in cross-examina- tion they admitted that the defendants were in a field taking the apples. Wright did not assault anyone but threatened to th-ow a stone.—Dr. Weayerharn stated that Richard Horton came to his surgery suffering from a severe cut of the lip and a contused ironed of the nose. The blow on the lip must have been a very severe one. He had to st'tch it no.— For the defence. Mr. Davies said he thought the magistrates weuld agre's with him that there was no case of assault against Wright. The only evhieum which had been given sgainst him was that he had a stone in his hand and threatened to throw it at one of the complainant's sons. and he would ask the magistrates to make a distinction between him and the ether defendants. Wright was a most respect- able men and had been engaged for fifteen years as a shinning, clerk at Messrs. Brunner. Mond and Co 's Works. He would rusk the magistrates to consider that part of the case now.— The Chairman said they saw no reason to separate the case.-- Mr. Davies then argued on the legal standing of the case He rooted from Stone's work, and contended that as the defeedants did not take the fruit frem a garden. orchard. nursery. greenhouse, hotbouse ins conservatory but took them from branches overhanging a field they were n,t liable to be summoned for larceny.—Mr Unsworth said such an argement might have held gene if the trees baud everhung the highway, but as a matter of fent the trees overhung the complainant's own field. and the d 4 mdents were committing an act of trespass in being there.—The Chairman said he was afraid the megis- trates ceuld not assume what Mr. Davies pro,.osed.— Mr. Davies eroceeling to defend the ease, said he was obliged to admit that tha defendants were doing what they ought not to have done. and he admitted that they were trying to get apples from the tree in which they steed. But when the sons of the complainant saw them they had none in their possession. Al! three men were of good character and he asked the magistrates to deal leniently with them. The magistrates retired to consider their deeision, and after a short deliberation they reterned and fined each defendant us and costs for the apple-stealing. and El and costs each for the assail'', or in default. 14 days' imprisonment. STONE THROWING —WARRINGTON B•TS IN TR^l*BLE.—Jrlin Cntterill, Valentine Baker. Thomas Hatch, Thomas Dutton. Harry Harbourne. and Captain Bowen. all boys living at Warrington were ehareed with wilftilly nestructine the free passage of a highway by throwing stones on the 6th Sep- t' mher. Mr. Unsworth defended Bowen and pteee‘es</p>
        <p>Ti,e other boys also pleaded guilty. P.C. Prew stated Om; at 4.3 S on the date named he saw the defendant. in comoany with ethets throwing at a horse chesnut tree in Sandy-lane, Stockton Heath. A ntimb?.r of passengers were frightened to pass at the time.—Mr. Unsworth said Bowen was a respect- able boy and son of Inspector Bowen who had recently re- tired from the Borough Police Force. He asked ler the boys to be treated leniently.—The Chairman fined the boys six- pence each'including costs, and admonished them for their conduct. A SLEEPY DRIVER.—Robert Buchannan of Walton was charged with being asleep whilst in charge of a ho-se and lurry on Warrington-road. Walton Inferior on the 12th inst. P.C. Lee proved the case, end the defendant was fined ss. and c eats.</p>
        <p>RIINCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MONDAY.--Before Messer'. A. Themes and A. W. Lightbnrn. THE JURY LlSTS.—These lists for the several townehips, in the Runcorn Potty Sessional Division were submitted to and approved by the Benoh. OIeTRUCTION IN CHURCH-STREET —Thomas Stanton, 18 Brunswick-street,, was ordered to pay (he costs, 4s. 6d., for obstructing the footpath in Church-street en the 20th inst.— P.C. Hughes preyed the case. DRITNEENNF.SS —lllizabeth Done. 15 Cartwright-street, was summoned for beim.: drunk and disorderly in Halten-road on the 21st inst., was fined ss. and 9s. 6d. cotts.—rieorge Fryer wts fined ss. and costs for being drunk and disorelerly in Lock-street on .:atirrday night,. Wrrnour • eouan.—Edward Fershaw. Canal-street, was ordered to pay ss. ineludilg costs,for allowing his dog to be at large, without a collar. SAVAGE ASSAULTB.—Catberine Osborne. 32. Helten-roael, was summoned for assaulting 811.- n Wainwright. 35 English- row. on September 24th.—Defendtnt pleaded guittv.—The complainant said she and defendant met in Church street on the date named. Mrs. Osborne made reference to alleged statements made by her with regard to stealin7 i-wellerv, and said she would wait for her at Top Locks. The defendant then struck the complainant on the face, discoloured her eye. and cut her lip open. Witness added that her life was miserable, and had been since 189 She would put an end to herself or go into the workhouse, for she colid not bear the life she was living. The defendant Was bound over to keep the peace for six months in the sum of £5, aid ordered to pay the casts. TKE PROPOSED NEW TKEATRE FoR RUNCoitte.—The plans of tha above were submitted to the bench by Mr. H. N. Linaker, who explained that they had been approved by the Runcorn Urban Council and the police. Mr. [Caddick would come later to apply for the licence of the present theatre tobe transferred to the new one.—Mr. Thomas said he ceuld not see what the bench could do in the matter in its prssent stage. —Mr. Linaker said their wish was to submit the matter, so far as they went to see if they met with the approval of the beneh. Superintentent O'Kell remarked that there was no doubt the new theatre would be an improvement on the present one. The means of exit were very satisfactory, the fire appliances were superior, and the neighbourhood better.</p>
      </div>
