      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>LOCAL PARISH COUNCIL MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARISH COUNCIL MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD WITHOUT.-BHIP CANAL MATTERS. The Latchford Without Parish Council meeting was held on Monday evening at the Baptist Schoolroom, Wash-lane, when there were present :—Councillor John Lee (in the chair),Messrs. W. Richardson,W. G.Hopkins, Isaac Turner,and George Pining (clerk).—The Clerk re- ferred to the application of the Ship Canal Company to stop up a portion of Acker's-lane. and to the consent of the Council to the same. He also read the followicg letter from the company :—" We are obliged by your letter of the 24th inst., which reached us to-day (27th August), with copy resolution of the Pariah Council of Latchford Without. We should be glad if you would give public notice of the resolution,and have it confirmed within two months, as required by Section 13 of the Local Government Board Act, 1894.—Yours truly. GRUNDY, KERSEAW AND COMPANY."—Tbe Clerk: The confirmation of the minutes did that.—The Clerk read the following letter, which had been re- ceived from the Ship Canal Company :—" Dear sir,—l beg to acknowledge the receipt of your letter of the 10h inst., which I think must have been written under some misapprehension, as we have , made no reduction in the number of our men at the Latchford swing bridge. I shall be glad if you will give me ady specific case where there has been delay at this bridge, no that I may have the matter inquired into. I may say that strict instructions have been given for everything to be done to avoid delay to passenger and vehicular traffic at the swing bridges, and I am informed that at the bridge named there has been n* delay."— Mr. Pilling explained that it had been ascertained since that one of the men had bad a holiday, and that his place had not been filled up in the meantime. The delay would not occur again.—The Ship Canal Company hay- ine been written to complaining of the dangerous con- dition of the steps leading up to this bridge, the Clerk read the following reply from the company:—" I will be obliged if you will inform me why you consider that this company is responsible for the repair of the steps to which you refer. The steps were placed in their pre- sent position as a concession to the late Daresbury High- way Beard, but no obligation for their maintenance was entered into by us. As "Feu are probably aware, neither the Various Powers Ant of 1890 nor any of the agree- ments entered into sines the passing of that Act corttain any provision for a communication between the high and low level roads at this point. I think, there- fore, that if the Parish Council desire that the steps should be retained they should themselves undertake the responsibility for their ntaintenanco."—The Clerk said the work had been done by the Runcorn District Coun- cil, under whose modes he had brought the matter —The Clerk also read the following letter :—" On behalf of the Baptist Church, we respectfully draw your attention to the state of the lane a:ongside of the chapel, and also to ask yen to use your influence as a Council with the res- ponsible authorities so that it may he put into proper repair as early as possible.—Yours, &amp;0., on behalf of the Church, CEARLES ANDREW (pastor), W. T. WILEINSON (secretary).—The Clerk said the matter had been brought under the notice of the Council several times.— The Chairman suggested that Mr. Pilling shenld again. draw the attention of Meurs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., to the condition of the lane as the lane belonged to them. It was important it should be kept in repair. He moved that that be done.—This was seconded by Mr. Hopkins, and agreed to. GRAPPENZALL.—THE PROTECTION OF LL 'TELFORD LOCKS. Grappenhall Parish Council meeting was held on Mon- day evening in the National Schools, Mr. J. C. Parr, J.P., C.A., presided, and there were present Messrs. W. Long, T. Smith, R. Warburton, F. Ellison, J. J. Fair- hurst, T. Burrows Lister Kaye, S. M. Houghton, and S. J. Woodcock (cleA).—The Chairman in referring tea resolution passed by the Grappenhall Parish Council calling the attention of the authorities to the state of the river at Wilderspool, said he was present when the resolution was brought before the County Council, and it was then referred to the Public Health , Committee. It was discussed and thoroughly gone in- to, wh ch was in consequence of the Grappenhall Parish Council forwarding it to the County Council. A deal of good had occurred through it. (Hear, hear.) The mat- ter would he well considered.—Mr. Parr also read a letter bearing on the nuisance at Wilderspool.—The fol- lowing.correspondence was read by the clerk in refer- ence to the protecting of the Latchford Locks. The following letter was sent by the Coun- cil to Mr. J. Ashton, clerk to the Runcorn Rural District Council, dated July let :—" At a meet- ing of the Council hold on Monday last, I was intimated to write and inform you that up to date the Ship Canal Company has not done anything in the way of protect- ing the Latchford Locks, and ask your Council to write to tho Ship Canal Company telling them that they should be held responsible for any accidents that may occur if the matter is net attended to at once." The appended letter has been forwarded to Mr. Ashton, clerk to the Runcorn Rural District Council :—" Manchester Ship Canal Company, Engineer's office. 41, Spring-gardens. Manchester, July 9th, 1896. " Dear Sir,—L itchford Locks. I have yours of yesterday's date with enclosure from the Clerk to the Graopenhall Pariah Council. Ido not understand what that gentleman means by" pro- tecting the Latchford Locks," and will be glad if you will inform me. The footways across the canal are efficiently protected by hand-rails. and I am not aware of any provision in Act of Parliament or agreement under which this company can be called upon to pro- vide for the lighting of the locks or footway."—The fol- lowing letter was also read, which had been received I from Mr. J. Ashton, clerk to the Rural District Council of Runcorn, dated 22nd July :—" Latchford Locks, Adverting to my letter of the 11th inst. and the I enclosure therein referred to. I certainly cannot find anything in the Act of 1885 by which the company I can be required to light the locks or footway. What I do find with reference to these locks is sub-sections 8 of Section 83, which is as follows :—" The company shall provide and forever maintain a footway protected by suitable and sufficient handrails for the use of the public free of charge across the canal at or near the Latchford Locks from the road numbered on the deposited plans 105 in the township of Grappenhall to the road on the north side of the canal n Imbered 106 in the same town- ship."—Mr. Long explained that when a person get to the locks there was no protection at all, and on a foggy night the slightest deviation would send him right into the water.—The following resolution was passed and for- warded to the District Council :—" The opinion of the deputation of the Grappenhall Parish Council is that it is time for the Ship Canal Company to adequately pro- tent the locks from danger. They consider that their obligation to light them has increased thereby,and think they ought to afford better protection to the approaches. The Grappenhall Parish Council must again urge your Council to take this matter up and put pressure on the Ship Canal Company,otherwise they must appeal to the County Council, as they feel that after the loss of life that has already occurred it would be a failure of duty on their part to remain inactive, especially as the winter is approaching."</p>
        <p>Mr. Fred Barnard, the well-known black and white artist, has hen burned to death in his house at Wimble- don. It is believed that while smoking in bed he set the room on fire.</p>
      </div>
