        <p>RUGdY. WARRINGTON v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Warrington with a large number of their supporters visited Runcorn on Saturday to play the old Cheshire fifteen. Warrington's team was similar to the one that was defeated at Stockport the previous Saturday. Con- siderable interest was evinced in this fixture as the last six matches played between these clubs, ended in favour of (with the exception of a draw) the Cheshire men. The scores summed up for the last six matches are Run- corn 5 goals. 5 tries; Warrington one try. Runcorn have win five matches out of the six and drawn one, Warrington being nilled each time with the exception of the first match played on April 14th, 1892, when the score was Runcorn one goal, Warrington sae try. Large erowds ef spectators came from Warrington and Widnes to witness these old rivals once again in the field of hos- tilities. The following players (undet the control of Mr. Williams of Salford) composed the teams. Warrington: Heesoin, full back; Lawless, Radford, Barber, Carey, threequarter backs; Bate, Houghton, half backs; Taylor. Thornton, Burrows, Hill, Scholtze, Barratt, Highest, Swift, forwards. Runcorn : S. Houghton, full back; T. Warder, W. Gayter, J. Butterworth, T. Richardson, three- quarter hacks; J. Faulkner, W. Pendlebury, half backs; W. Faulkner, H. Farmer, S. Walker, W. Lightfoot, 0. Hughes, F. Gayter, F. Darling- ton, J. Shepherd, forwards.</p>
        <p>As will be seen from the above teams, J. Shepherd, late of Broughton Rangers, whose transfer was granted to Runcorn, on Tuesday,at the meeting of the Lancashire Section ef the Northern Union held at Manchester, took his position as a forward in the Runcorn ranks for the first time on Satutday. ems and Dakin were absentees from the Warrington team. A large crowd of spectators had gathered on the field.when Faulkner set the leather roll- ing. Radford returned well, and a scrimmage took place on the line. Warder made a splendid run but was grounded before going far. Warrington were penalised, and Faulkner made an effort to secure ground in the visitors' quarters. This had not the desired effect, as Carey made a daring save, and landed the ball back to mid-Beld. Here, Warrington were granted a free kick, and Taylor sent the ball into touch. The visitors showed their pressing abilities and took the leather to the hems- stars 25. Scrimmages became very frequent and Bate made a brilliant attempt to get through. Lawless from a nags from the former, made a very poor show, and was dropped upon before he knew where he was. The ball travelled to neutral territory, where a number of exchange kicks took place. Runcorn emerged ta the middle bar, and soon began to show Warrington their mode of attacking, Radford was a weak point in the visitors backs and lost his team some of the very best chances of the afternoon. Play remained at half way line fora considerable time, until the home- stars, slowly departed and took play to the Warrington quarters. Line outs and scrimmages were the order for some time. Heuehton (Warrington) was quickly upon the scene, and saved the ball from travelling further. Warringtnn aided by a free kick, took play to the home- stars 25. Runcorns three-quarter backs were loudly ap- plauded for their dribbling movement, by which play was transferred to the visitors quarters. Runcorn re- mained in this position, (close to Warrington's goal tines and seemed deterimed to score before departing. one Jf the hornesters forwards,took possession and would have undoubtedly scored had not the visitors captain pushed him into touch. Luck then seemed to turn against Runcorn and the hall was conveyed to the other end of the field. A dribble by Bate, Houghton and Barber was of no advantage as the latter lost his hold. Lawless with a fine run stopped Runcorns progress, and the ball remained in the hnmesters territory. Play was trans- ferred to Warrington side. and Lawless with a grand shot cleared the danger. Bate received from a scrimmage at midfield, made a brilliant run, and was about to drop over when he was held up by one if his opponents. A scrimmage resulted, Bate received again, Passed to Barber.the latter to LaWless,whe missed what appeared to be a certain try. Heesom came to the freat made his mark and forced a minor. Barber lost ground, and the homesters visited Warrington quarters again. A golden chance was lest by Radford failing to take Bar- ber's pass. Richardson was loudly applauded for a piece of work by which he took matters to neutral territory. A minute later Walker, Lightfoot and Hughes were seen dribbling the ball together in fine style towards the visi- tors' quarters. Carey gained ground, made a splendid cave by falling in front, and putting an end to the drib- ble. Half time arrived with a clean sheet—nething scored.</p>
        <p>On the restart the homesters forwards took the ball to the left corner and Richardson was seen to drop with the ball near the goal line. At this point a loud 'uproar front the spectators was heard for what they thought a try. Their joy didn't last long as a scrimmage took place. Afterwards, J. Faulkner handled the leather, vitt a geed shut through the posts,and a'tWnor was forced. Warrington by a combined passing movement took play to the Cheshire quarters, but only far a short time. Neutral ground was readied and Houghton (Runcorn) was knocked into touch. Swift by a swift run reached the homesters 25, where he passed to Barber, then to Radford who appeared too slow to realise himself, and the itomesters came with a rush upon what appeared to be the visitors weak spot. Runcorn now began to show their combination and mails several rushesolividing the passes between them. At length Butterworth from a pass ran three parts the length of the field and scored the first try. The Lancashire men's Chance now ap- peared te be gone. They seemed to fall away and failed in their tackling bouts, which they se well maintained in the first half. The homesters, who had by this time been well applauded for their brilliant try, forced the leather to the visitors territory and Walker intercepted Pass, and after running a few yards passed on to J. Faulkner who scored. Nothing came of the kick at goal. Warrington remained for a short time at miifield, until the cup-holders crossed the bar, and gct once again in their quartsrs. The visitors had to de nothing but defend, and this they only did for a short period. Richardson was again on the scoring path and Walker from a pass from the former scored the third try, Botterworth attempted to improve the already large score, but without effect. The ball remained in the visitors' territory and a corner was forced. Here Butter- worth took possession of the leather and scored. Muehes tried his foot at improving and was successful. War- rington had by this time completely resigned themselves to their fate, and almost fell away. Carey and Lawless were several times conspicuously at fault by letting their opponents get through Bp often. The Cheshire men maintained their combination which had proved so valu- able a while ago, and were continually in the Warring- ton 25. Barber emerged and took play to midfield, where he passed to Radford. This player was soon spotted and floored before he had the chance to throw the ball. The hentesters. continued pressing and were again in the visitors' territory. Just at the last minute, Barber got the ball and dribbled it before him to the end of the field. Hare he was about to drop over, hut was fouled (shoved over). The, referee evidently did not see this, and of course he didn t allow anything for it. The game ended, the cup-holders being victorious by 1 goal, 3 tries, to Warrington nil. WARRINGTON "A " V. R.LINCORN "A."—This impor- tant fixture in the Lancashire" A " team league came if at Warringtea en Saturday before about 3,000 per- sons. The homesters had rather a weak team their re- presentatives being :—Boscow, full back ; Warburton, forwards. ckßo , oSoacvoarnge, : Isherwood, O'Brien and Moores,threequarters ; Price and Feden, half backs; Freeth, Andrews, Robey,Royle,Templeton and Jolley —Jollec.full back ; Myere, Nickson, Gregory and West, three-quarters; Woodward and Poole.half backs ; Lang- ley, Johnsen, Pendlebury, Woods T. Reece, Batley, Shaw,and Antrebus,forwards. Runcorn started from the railway end against the wind and Foden returned into touch near the 25 flag. Scrimmages took place in succession about here, and Price and Foden did good work behind the pack. Warburton missed a pass from Foden and Runcorn secured a footing in the home half by a strong kick. Boscow was safe, however, and Foden again returned into touch. Further scrimmaging followed, and an injudicious kick by Foden gave the visitors a chance. West picked up but O'Brien ran him ' into touch at the corner. This put the hemesters well en the visitors' line, where .ome exciting play was wit- nessed. The Runcornians, however, defended strongly and forced play to the opposite side of the field. Here Warrington wer, granted a tree kick and Warburton I took the place from the 25 line close to touch. Much to the surprise and delight ot the homesters he placed a splendid goal. On the re-start Price was hurt in the knee and had to retire, his place being taken by Reyle. A round of passing was taken up by the Warrington backs only to be wasted by a mull from Moires. Desul- tory play followed in which Warringtou had the advant- age. Geod kicking again helped Runcorn and their backs looked dangerous with good passing, but O'Brien tackled splendidly. Half-time arrived with Warrington leading by a penalty vial (3 points) to nil. Foden restarted and Moores gained ground by a good kick. Some sharp, open play followed and Bettley was injured in the spine and had to be assisted off the field. O'Brien and Moores were next prominent with some use- ful exchanges and a mark by Foden was advantageous. Soon after Runcorn were again penalised and Warbur- ton made another attempt at goal, but failed. More neat passing among the Worrington backs gave Moores possession and he put in a good run. A determined rush again put the visitors on the aggressive and Myers was given a pass. He first thought of dropping a goal and then ran but it was too late and a geed chance was ' lost. By combined passing Runcorn almist got over but Warhurtcn made a timely save. Isherwood and O'Brien relieved and time was then called with Warrington winners of a hard fought game by a penalty goal to Runcorn nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>JUNIOR LEAGUE. I W-----rvetToN S M ---' A</p>
        <p>ARRINGTON ST. .ARY'S V 'MERTON LiORNETS.—Thot junior champions played their first league match on Saturday, their opponents being Atherton Hornets. Both teams suffered through absentees. The visit,,rs winning the toss Caldwell started for the home team, a good return being made, and for some time the play was even, the Atherton forwards playing a strong game. Soon afterwards the home team began to press, and dribbing by the Saints' forwards ended in Siddall scoring, Roughley making a good attempt at goal. From the restart, the Saints were awarded a free kick for off- side play by the Hornets, who were penned in their own quarters, Butler relieving with a good run and kick to centre. The Hornets were now awarded a free kirk, but Warburton getting possession made a geed run, but without result. Soon after the Saints' backs get possess- ion, and after some splendid passing Higginbottom sc)red, Reughley again failing. From the kick off, the Saints again returned to the attack, and after some passing R. Bennett scored, Roughley this time improv- ing. Half time, St. Mary's, one goal two tries; Atherton Hornets. nil. The play in the second half was of a scrambling nature, but towards the finish the Saints attacked strongly, and R. Bennett by a pass from Iligginbottem dropped a neat goal. The same player scored a try directly afterwards, T. Bennett improving. The home team attacked to the finish, and won in easy fashion by three goals (one dropped) two tries (20 points) to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
