      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>EARLESTOWN v. WHISTON. LANCASHIRE ALLIANCE.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN v. WHISTON. LANCASHIRE ALLIANCE.</p>
        <p>At Earlestown, en Saturday, in fine weather, and be- fore a fair number of spectators. Earlestown kicked off uphill, and at once raided the Whiston goal, but Reyn- olds cleared, and midfield play resulted. Whisten be- came aggressive, and a claim for hands in front of the Earlestews goal, gays them an opening, but the effort was cleared. Earlestown pressed considerably, but the defence of Reynolds and Brown was perfect. A clever bit of play by Wersley and Hughes caused Lyon to handle. Whiston again gat away, but Mather cleared. Play slowed down somewhat and the ball hovered about the centre for sometime. A corner to Earlestown was cleared, and a foul off Clarks brought the ball down again. Edmondson and Harrison returned, and a cor- ner was conceded, but was cleared. •M'Carthy got away and Casey was troubled, but a return to the Whiston end by Edmondson and Harrison resulted in the latter scoring after 30 minutes' play. Earlestown nressed severely, and the Whiston goal was often in danger, but the defence was good. Half-time—Earlestewn, 1 goal ; Whiston nil. )on after the kick off one of the Whisten players " Fairhurst " struck Hughes, an Earlestown player, far some reason or other apparently because hurfrhes had robbed him of the ball. The game was stopped and the referee cautioned both players. play afterwards proceeding. In a few minutes Hughes got down to the Whiston goal and Fairhurst again struck him, causing: Maui to flow. The spectators broke on to the ground and it appeared as if a general melee was imminent. The referee, however, ordered both players off the field, and it is quite likely they will be suspended. The game was afterwards carried on in a half-hearted fashion and although Earles- town pressed somewhat, they were unable to score, the game ending as at half-time, Earlestown 1, Whiston nil.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS C a RGE AGAINST MR. JOHN HAMPSON.</p>
        <p>AN OLD MAN BADLY INJURED.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning at the Warrington Borough Police Court, before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Marrison and Francomb, Messrs. F. Monks, T. Birchall. J. W. Hayward, and S. Wright, John Flampson, 170, Church-st., described as a commission agent and for merly the Conservative agent for Warrington, wag charged with attempting to inflict grievous bodily barn, upon Joseph Cowlshaw, Glazehrook-st., in his house aV 11.45 on Saturday night. Mr. Arthur Browne reprr sented the defendant. The Chief Constable stated that he would have to ask for a remand of a week as the prosecutor was in the le* firmarY and would not be able te leave the institution for at least three weeks. Toe circumstances of the cage were these: Flarnpson was a commission event and fermerly.a sergeant major in the Army. Joseph Cowl- shaw the prssecutor, was 71 years of ago, and the tic° men had been acquainted and on good terms with ong another for the last year or two. Oa Saturday nigh° about 11 o'clock they mot in the "Ring-e-Bells" and had drinks together. Cowlshaw invited Hampson to hiS house and they west there, to 10, Glazsbrook-street. There,they:fetind Cowlchaw's wife with more drink than was good for her. They all sat down to supper and dur ing the conversation prisoner mentioned that he was ag old solelier,and Mrs,. Cowishaw, who is younger than het husband, remarked that she" loved old soldiers." SOO fatniliarity appeared to have tak-n place between thees. This displeased the old min who remonstrated sayinC be had einouois of this loving business and they were to stolo it. It was alleged that he then struck his wife in the face and nrienner,excited by drink rushed at Cowli-la seized him hr the throat, and threw him to the gro This occurred in the kitchen and in the fall,Cowlshaw! leg was broken and he got his head humped. He said"o!" don't kill tn.." and prisoner said in his rage o Yeg, w• 11 you nia b-- • kill ou,"and he throttled Cowl_ shave with hia We then pulled a knife ouving said I'll cut your threat." Cowlshaw said *. Dan Ci dread the knife more than anytkino.,, prisoner struck at him an,rl canoed a wound in his throat which bled freely. Thu wife, who was too drunk apparently to understand the seriousness of the situation, was stand me by laughing. Prisoner said "Give me a cord and I'll hang him " Mrs. Covelshaw get him a piece of cori and Sampson tied it round the old man's neck as he lal en the floor. Cewl.haw put his fingers between his nee; and the rope. Prisoner got hold of it and pulled the old man from the hack kitchen to the front rocas, A. part of this was witnessed by a nest: door noisrhbruir, a Mrs. Moston. Cowlshaw called foir assistance and reaching a stick knocked against the scab with it. Several stei.hbours came up and forced an eb" trance.. P.C. Con.iliffe was called from his house 8100 to and rendered first aid to the wounded an, who 10 prisoner's presence accused him of havieg assaulted hibl, but ho said nothing about prisoner trying to strangle him. Prisoner got up and left the house and was goid away when the officer followed and apprehended A knife Was found in his possession but there werel; bloodstain, upon it. All were more or less under P: influence of drink and prisoner was a man who had a good deal of foreign service. He (the Chief Constable; was informed that he was scarcely a an of sound Mr. Browns said he had no objection to the remand. Prisoner was remanded for a week. Mr. Browse: Mr. Hanspson wishes me to say tber whatever he did was in self-defence.</p>
        <p>ACCIDNNT AT TEE Ott/TAAL STATION. Theras Titchard,so years of age, residing at Dv: Winwick-read, was admitted to the Warrington IQ, tirtnary on Menday suffering trom injuries to ribs, sustained whilst at work in the goods yard the Central Station. It seems that a wheel of ' sheep truck suddenly came off, and Titchard 101° caught between the side of the truck and a post. Mrs PONlenelre HAIR RESTORER. a grOot cliello•Ory• • Most Refruhing Flask, Marvellous Results, Innumeraltil Testimonials, Patronised by Royalty. Ti great demos" by Professienals. anakes tka Hair Grow. prevents -RAW nese, lavigerating, restores hair to its naturalcninof. gives healtky 'and youthful appearance. Mostastonha" ing'results ; 2s 6d Bottle. post free.—A ddi issMrs. POP sem% 25, Lime-st.. Liverpoel—Advt. LOYAL ORDER. OF ANCIENT MHEPHERDS,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON DISTRICT. a There was a large gathering of members stle visiting brethren at the quarterly meeting of tPd Olive Branch Lodge at their lodge room (Rose 0110 Crown Inn, Bridge-street) on Saturday. The raillhe object of the gathering wee to acknowledge t" services of their late secretary,Brother John lial,sc At the request of the lodge members the weetlif.i.f wee presided over by the district chief, Brottve William Eckereley. IIPeWAS supported by deputy chief, Brother Henry Warburton, and James Parr, district treasurer,Brother Peter Edgs district secretary, and Brothers Dunn, Haig, Bennion, Adams, Myles, Denton, Woolley, Si" If James Owen and others. The Chairman, on bell • of tke lodge, presented Brother John Haley handsome travelling bag. Brothers Dunn,Beno"),.o and Adams also spoke. Mhearty vote of thanks " the Chairman terminated:a pleasant meeting. -</p>
