      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>TEE FATALITY AT MESSRS. RYLA.NDS' WORKS.</head>
        <p>TEE FATALITY AT MESSRS. RYLA.NDS' WORKS.</p>
        <p>DANGERS OF LOOSE CLOTHING.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest in the Parade Room, Iriam-st., en Tuesday morning, relative tc the death of William Edgar, 17 years of age, living with his parents at Sparling-st.. Latchford,and employed at the works of Messrs. Rylands Bros., Limited, who met with injuries which resulted iv his death en Thursday. Mr. Arthur Browne, solicitor, appeared en behalf of Messrs. Rylands Bros., Limited. Before the evidence was called Mr. Browne informed the Coroner that this was the first occasion for 100 years that a fatal accident had occurred at Messrs. Rylands' werki,although they employed between seven and eight hundred men. The Coroner said that showed that Mr. Browne's clients were careful people,and it spoke well for them. William Edgar,24,Dickenson-street,father of deceased, said the latter lodged at 14 Sparling-street, Latchford, and worked in the'• rnnery "at Messrs. Rylands Bros., wireworks. He was 17 years of age. Witness heard on Thursday evening of the accident and saw his son at the Infirmary. He was not present at the death of deceased who was unconscious when witness saw him. He had worked at Rylands' for about three years and had never complained of the danger of his work. Thomas Conner,a work-mate of deceased, said he saw the latter fast in his machine when he turned. The machine was out of gear and as soon as witness moved the handledeceased dropped out. He did not know how deceased got in. Deceased was 'running wire," and witness was " winding wire." They used different machines. Deceased had managed to knock the machine out of gear when he was caught. .</p>
        <p>By ajuror : Deceased had only gone on that machine that morning, but he had worked on a similar machine bofore.</p>
        <p>John loughton said he was walking towards the mac- hine at the time of the accident and noticed deceased bent over the machine which was almost stopped. Re thought there was no occasion to stop the machine until he was caught. Deceased heel been put on the machine the day before, but had worked similar machines before. Nis opinion as to what happened was that deceased was looking round the shop and his jacket pocket must have swung into the machine. That was the conclusion he came to.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Browne: The handle and brake were 4ft. high. The jersey deceased was wearing was too big far him. Witness had worked among the machines nine years Ind had never seen any similar accident. Factory inspec- tors had passed the machines. Nurse Hill said the deceased was admitted to the In- firmary on Thursday and died on Friday at 11 30 a.m. He was suffering from fracture of the skull and several wounds about the face. He was never able to speak to anyone after admission.</p>
        <p>William Whittaker,foreman of the department.said no bad known the machines; 24 years and they were now in precisely the same condition. There had been no acci- dents with them. It was the furthest thing he could think of for deceased to be caught. He had nothing to do but watch the in:.chine. The Factory Inspector had seen the machinery and could make no suggestion ex- cept that the employes wear tight jackets. Witness said he himself could not see that anything more could he done to make the machinery safer._</p>
        <p>By the Coroner : rh• 41i-stance from where deceased was standing to whore he was caught was two feet. It was marvellous to witne4s how deceased got caught. After some minutes' consultation the jury returned a verdict of " Accidendal death." They agreed with the Factory Inspector as to tight clothing and recommended its adeption. Mr. Browne said the firm would put up a notice te that effect. The firm also deeply regretted that such an accident should occur and they were going te bear all costs ef deceased's funeral, without admitting any liability.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH OF Mn. A. BIACKHURST.</p>
        <p>Very general regret is felt in the town at the death of Mr. Alexander Blackhurst, only son of Alderman Blackhurst, jeweller etc., Sankey-street, Warrington. On Thursday of last week the de- ceased gentleman attended the shop as usual and was apparently in good health, so that the news of his sudden death eame as a most disagreeable surprise to the townspeople on Saturday morning. Mr. Blackhurst felt unwell on Thursday evening, and beford long it was found necessary to call in Dr. Fox. Despite the medical assistance obtained Mr. Blackhurst died early on Saturday morning from peritonitis. He was 43 years of age and of a particularly healthy appearance; a more unexpected occurrence than his death could hardly have been imagined. To Alderman Blackhurst, and the rest of the familv,the death of an only son has come as a great shock which is felt the mere acutely as it is only about twelve months since the eldest Miss Blackhurst died.—The funeral of ths deceased gentleman took place at Weaverham Parish Church on Monday.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON INFIRMARY AND DISPENSARY. Mr. E. M. Crosfield, hon. secretary of the Work- ingmen's Infirmary Conamittee asks us to acknow- ledge the receipt of £62 Bs. Id. from Mr. Luke Talbot, Chief Constable, as net proceeds of the cricket match in August last between the police and tradeemen.</p>
        <p>"DISFIGURED FOR LIFE "is the despairing cry o thousands afflicted with unsightly skin diseases. Do yoi realise what this disfiguration means to sensitive souls ' t means isolation, seclusion. It iv a bar to social an, business success. Do you wonder that despair seizes up. on these sufferers when doctors fail, standard remedies fail, and nostrums prove worse than useless ? Skin dis- eases are most obstinate to cure or even relieve. It is an easy matter to claim to cure them, but quite another thing to do so. CLITICURA remedies have earned the right to be called Skin Specifics, because for years they have met with remarkable success. There are cases they cannot cure, but they are few indeed. It is no long drawn-out, expensive experiment. One shilling invested in CIITICURA SOAP will prove m)re convincing than a page of advertisement. In short, CUTICURA WORKS WONDERS, and its cures of torturing, disfiguring, ho miliating skin diseases are simply marvellous.</p>
      </div>
