      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>(</p>
        <p>INTERESTING MARRIAGE; AT</p>
        <p>PADGA.TE.</p>
        <p>An interesting marriage took place at Christ Church, Padgate, on Thursday week, being that of Miss Elizabeth Appleton, third daughter of the late Rev. Richard Appleton,Bruche Hall,Padgate,and Mr. James Douglas Buckton, solicitor, of Warrington. As both parties are very well known in the town and district, there was an exceedingly large num- ber present to witness the interesting ceremony which was timed to commence at 12.30. At the appointed time the choir boys made their way to the entrance of the church, and shortly afterwards the bride arrived. The choristers then marched to the altar ,followed by the Rev. R. Appleton and the Rev. J. J. Langham, vicar of Padgate. The bride entered the sacred edifice leaning on the arm of her brother, Mr. Appleton, of Ludlow, carrying a lovely bouquet,and wearing a dress of white duchesse satin with train, trimmed with Brussels lace and orange blossom, in which she looked exceedingly handsome. She also wore a tulle veil,and amongst her ornaments were an expansive gold bracelet with watch and a diamond crescent,sapphire and pearl,presented by tbe bridegroom. The bride was given away by her eldest brother, Mr. Appleton, of Ludlow ; and Mr. Harold Buckton, brother of the bridegro,m, fulfilled the duties of beat man. The five bridesmaids in attendance were, Miss Pierce, Miss Ellen Pierce and Miss E. M. Appleton (nieces ot the bride),and the two Misses Bucktoni(sisters of the bridegroom). They each wore white canvas dresses, trimmed with white lace and silk, and white felt hats with ostrich plunges. Each bridesmaid received a gold bracelet, presents from the bridegroom, and carried a bouquet of yellow chrysanthemums with auburn leaves. The hymns sung during the service were " 0 perfect love, all human thuught transcending " and " 0 Father all creating," the congregation joining in the singing. The officiating clergyman was the Rev. R. Appleton, assisted by the Rev. J. J. Langliana, of Pad- gate. Mr. Blackwell presided at the organ. The wed- ding breakfast was afterwards partaken of at Bruche Hall, after which the newly married couple left for London, where part of the honeymoon will be spent. Their future residence will be Bewsey New Hall. The following is a list of the more interesting and public wedding presents : Bridegroom to the bride, expansive gold bracelet with watch and diamond crescent, sapphire and pearl ; bride to the bridegroom, gold watch and chain; from Fair- field school teaching staff, silver lamp and silver dessert spoons ; Church-street Boys' School and Ellesmere-street school staff, silver candle sticks ; maids at Bruche Hall, lamp ; maids at Hollis Hey,New Brig!ston, dinner gong ; old nurse at Bruche Hall, biscuit cannister and silver- mounted brush and comb in case; Mr. Buckton's land • lady,Warrington,white lines and laced tray cloth ; from gardener and family at Bruche Hall, silver egg boiler ; Miss Appleton's class, Padgate, framed photograph of class group, and inkstand and letterweight ; clerks at Messrs. Greenall and Buckton's Office,silver fish slice and fork ; sergeants in A Company of the Volunteers,bronze statues of Nelson and Wellington. CAPTAIN BUCKTON AND THE VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>At the Drill Hall on Thursday, Captain Buckton, in commemoration of his wedding, entertained the mem- bers of the " A" company of volunteers, of which he is in command, to a substantial knife and fork tea. These present numbered about eighty. After the tea, which was thoroughly enjoyed, a smoking concert was held, the chair being taken by Colour-sergeant Jolley. After the usual loyal toasts had bees given, songs were sung by Corporal Losh and Mr. Headland. The Chairman then proposed " The health of Captain Buckton and Mrs. Buckton "in an appropriate speech. Songs were then given by Lance-Corporal Weedall and Sergeant Taylor, the latter also alluding in a seat speech to the happy event of the day and eulogising the officers,staff, and non-commissioned officers of the batallion. This speech was suitably acknowledged by Sergeant-Major Sullivan. Songs were afterwards given by Lance-Cor- poral Morris and Private Clayton (a bandsman). A vote of thanks was, on the proposal of Bugle-Major Plinsten, seconded by Band-inast.r Armstrong, accorded to the Chairman for presiding and to Mr. and Mrs. Headland, the caretakers of the Drill Hall, vio had catered for the tea. A very enjoyable gathering was brought to a close about 10 p.m. with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CRICKET CLUB.</p>
