      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>'tetterg to the etlitor.</head>
        <p>'tetterg to the etlitor.</p>
        <p>THE MARRIAGE LAWS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln reply to ney letter on the marriage laws,your sorrespondents seem to have wandered from that sub- ject to the evils ef drunkenness, and the letter by " Snrsum Cerda " is neither corteous nor fair. He states that married people may by mutual consent separate. Does he want your readers to lose sight of the fact that they cannot do so without mutual consent. When people are unhappily married they do not mutually consider each other, and the most blamable one is not likely to cunsent to an equitable deed of separa- tion. Let us consider in other respects the influence of the binding nature of the marriage laws. Suppose a woman marries a man who is an excellent singer, who frequently goes into company, who is often tempted to stay out too late and neglect his wife and family ; and that after having endured this for a time, his wife tells him that his conduct is distreying her affection, the cir- cumstance of her being unable to leave him distroys her legitimate influence and causes her to live under undesirable conditions, and may prevent that man being saved from evil habits by the affection of his wife, and the circumstance of a man being unable legally to separate from his wife lessens his influence over her con- duct and may cause many marriages to be much less 'happy than they might otherwise have been. I cantot at present, or for some time, write any more on this sub- ject and must conclude. Our first sweet happy home of rest Is on a mother's peaceful breast ; In manhood's years our home should 1 e Where love is faithful pure and free. The marriage laws prevent people separating when they no longer regard each other's welfare.and by doing as they cause drunkenness, sensuality and selfishness. which are the curses of civilized mankind, for they des- troy sympathy, affection and benevolence, and it is only in the due exercise of these virtues that happiness can be ever found.—Yours truly, Warrington, Sept. 30, 1896.</p>
        <p>S. KENYON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WEST LANCASHIRE MASONRY.</head>
        <p>WEST LANCASHIRE MASONRY.</p>
        <p>GRAND LODGE AT WARRING roN. The annual meeting of the Grand Masonic Lodge of the Province of West Lancashire took place in the Parr Hall, Warrington,on Wednesday afternoon. There were in attendance about 700 brethren, representing the whole of the 114 lodges which are comprised in the large prov- ince. The chair was occupied by Bro. the Right Hon. the Earl of Lathom, G.C.8., Pro.G.M., Prov.G.M. ;Bros. R. Wylie, P.G.D.Eng., Dep. Prov.G.M. ; the Right Hon. Lord Skelmersdale P.G.W.Eng., Prey. S.G.W. ; Rev. T. Barton Spencer, P.G.C. Eng., P. Prov. G.C. •' W. Good- acre, P.G. Swd.B. Eng., Prey. G.Sec. ; W. E. Tomlin- son, M.P.. P.G.D. Eng., P.P.G.W., T. For- rester, P.G.S.B. Eng.; J. Chadwick, P.G.S.B. Eng. ; and the following provincial officers : Brothers T. Wilde Rice, S.G.W. ; W. H. Arnott, J.G.W. •, Rev. C. C. Bluett, G.C. ; Rev. T. H. Leeson.G.C. ; W. Bowden.G.R. ; W. J. Worden,D.G.R. ; W. Goodacre. G.Sec. ;W. E. Harwood, S.G.D. ; J. L. Wolfendee,S.G.D. ; Joseph Cunliffe,S.G.D. ;W. J. Fleet- ; R. R. Ellis,J G.D. ; W. Sweetman,J.G.D.; J. Milne, G.oupt. of W. ; Thomas Vernon G.D. of C. ; Whittaker Bond, D.G.D. of C. ;F. Pritchard, A.G.D. of C.; William Taylor. A.G.D. of C.; J. Rigby, A.G.D. of C. •, T. Eastham,D.G.S.B. ; Robert H. Webster,G.St.B. ; R. Wardle, G.St.B. ; A. E. Workman, G.O. ; S. Howard, A.G.Sec. ; H. Peers, G.P. ; J. Ashton. A.G.P. ; Edward Itose,P. Armstrong,S. Fraser. W. R. Brewster, and J. F. Willan,G. Stewards ; and W. H. Ball,G. Tyler. Brother the Right Hon. the Earl of Derby wrote regretting his in- ability to be present owing to his municipal duties in -Liverpool. A balance-sheet, submitted by Mr. H. Hall, provincial grand treasurar, was of a satisfactory character, and showed total receipts of £1,673 13s. 7d., Including £769 12s. ld. from last year, £44 fees of honour. £799 14s. 61. fees from ledges, and £3O Os. 6d. collected at the Provincial Grand Ledge at Bootle. The expenses —which included £l9O paid in grants by the Charity Committee, £ll5 to the benevolent institutions, and -£3O Os. Eld to the Bootle Borough Hospital—amounted to £948 6s. 7d., leaving a balance in hand of £725 7s. Brother Goodacre, P.G.S., in his annual report, stated that the roll of lodges had during the year increased by two, and the roll of members by nearly 300. bringing up the number of lodees to 114, and of members to 7.533. The new lodges consecrated were the Stanley of Preston (Ni. 2,600), and the Wyreside, Garstane (No. 2,605). During the year the province had been successful in electing two boys and one girl on the foundation of tha London Royal Institution, and one brother had been elected an annuitant. In addition the province had afforded substantial aid towards the election of a third boy, whose father was a member of the Warrington lodge. It would be difficult to produce better evidence of the sound and healthy condition of the lodges gener- -ally than to point to the steady support given year by 'year to the three local charities, which were doing so much to alleviate distress, and add comfort to those in need. The income of the educational institution had been £2,176. against £1,850 in the previous year, and it had assisted 206 children at a cost of £1,473. The Hamer Institution had received nearly £BO more than in the previous year; while the claims upon it had been slightly less. The income of the Alpass Institution had been £1,142, against £922 in the previous year ; and it had paid in annuities £689 against £520 last year. The Earl of Latham heartily congratulated the prov- ince on the way it had kept up its character, and in- creased its numbers during the year. The roll of lodges new was an enormous one, but in view of the number of brethren belonging to them he did net think the lodges were too numerous. He found that the average num- ber of members for each lodge was 67, and there was net other province in the country that could shew a record like that. (Applause.) For the office of provincial grand treasurer there were two candidates, namely, Bro. E. R. Latham, P.M. Devonshire Lodge (584) and Bro. T. Banner Newton, P.M. Perseverance Lodge (1.551. The voting resulted in favour of Bro. T. Banner, Newton, who received 224 votes against 201 The following were appointed audi- tors :—Broe. H. W. Johnson, J. J. Lambert.J. T. Callow, and H. Nall. The Provincial Grand Master then invested the follow- ing provincial offieere for the year : EWA. E. G. Lewis. G.S. W. ; R. Layla nd, J. G. W,'; the Rev. R. B. Billinee and the Rev.T. H. Leeson . (re-enpointed), G.C. ; George Duxfield, :G. R. ; H. R. Thornton. D.G.R. ; William Goodacre (re-ap- pointed), G.S. ;S. Locke, J. Browne, G. Flnlme, jun., S.G.D. ; A. M. L. Fabert, T. Blanc, and W. R. Brewster, ; S. L Fraser. G.S. of W. ; J. H. Smith, G D.C. ; E. Rose, D G.D.C. ; T. Yarwnod,Henry Isaac, and S. J. Flenochsberg, A.G D 0.; Fred Batty, G S.B ; Tom Flunter.D G 5.8.; J. Williams and J. F. Willan,G Std.B ; W. Cole, G 0. ; L. Eceles, A.G.S. ; J. Bower, G.P. ; J. Heves.A.G.P. •, Isaac Smith, M. W. Clavton,J. Scott, A. King, H. Round,and Jonathan Parry, G. Stewards ; and W. H. Ball, G. Tyler. The Provincial Grand Secretary announced that in commemoration of his majority as Grand Master of Eng- lish Freemasenry,his Royal Highness the Prince of Wales had sanctioned the conferment of pastprovincial honours upon certain brethren of distinction in eanh province. The following were accordingly invested :—Past Provin- eial Grand Wardens: Sir George Pilkington and A. Franceve. Past Provincial Grand Deacons : T. Grime, J. Carter,and S. Kershaw. Past Provincial Grand Direc- ters of Ceremonies: G. Barker era P. J. Whitehead. Past Provincial Grand Sword Bearers : T. Crompton,D.l Lloyd. .I. J. Beyle,and G. Godfrey. A collection was taken for the benefit of the Warring- ton Infirmary and Dispensary and realised about £3O.</p>
        <p>DISPUTE AS TO THE TRANSFER OF A COAL BUSINESS. At the Warrington County Court on Wednesday before His Honour Deputy-,Tue.ge Crawford, a coal- dealer named John Bolan claimed from Francis Daniels £l2 55., balance owing on the purchase of a horse and lurry, and also £1 11s. 6d. for breach of agreement.—Mr. Unsvrorth, solicitor, who repre- sented the plaintiff, stated that the plaintiff and defendant were formerly in partnership as coal dealers. They had a horse and lurry and went about delivering coal. Not being satisfied with each other it was agreed that the plaintiff should sell his share in the horse and lurry, scales, Sze., to the defendant for £l5. The agreement wes drawn out and agreed to by both parties. The money Paid by the defendant since March was only ..E2 15s.— • John Bolan, the plaintiff, said the defendant, agreed to pay him the money at 10s. a week and two cwts of coal in addition each week. Only £2 15s. had been paid —Councillor William Rogers said he wit- nessed the signing of the agreement between the two parties. He read it out to them and they then signed it.—The defendant contended that he paid £5 5. towards the account, and produced a book showing the payments he bad made. He denied that there was any agreement to supply the plain- tiff with coal.—His Honour after examining the book produced gave a verdict for the plaintiff for £l3 16s. 61. and costs. CUTLERY and Eleetro-Plated GOODS.—GARLAND. Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. THE P.S.A. AT THE YOUNG MEN'S CHRISTIAN ASSOCIATION. There was a good attendance at the P.S.A. meet- ing on Sunday, when Mr. J. C. Harrington presided. The musical portion was contributed by Mr. H. H. Pearson'whose violin solos, Handel's " Largo "and Ernsts " Elegie," were a genuine treat, the former being played with piano and organ accompaniment. Miss Wooley also sang a solo in a pleasing manner. Mr.A. Cornell (always an acceptable speaker) de- livered a telling a4dress,his subject being "The God of our fathers." Mr. Higgingson,Mr. Yeadon, Mr. Kent, and others assisted in the meeting.,</p>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN IND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown Police Station on Monday morning,,before Mr. Borron ; Mary Ann Ellis, of Haydock-street, Earlestown, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in llaydock-street, on Saturday night, and was fined 2s. 6d. and Costs. EARLESTOWN WELSH WESLEYANS.</p>
        <p>_ The above body held a very successful tea and entertainment in the Houghton-st. Uhanel,on Satur- day evening. Over 50 sat down to tea, which was kindly given by friends, the proceeds being devoted to a fund for purchasing a new organ. 1.0 G.T. (EBENEZER LODGE).</p>
        <p>The usual meeting took place on Tuesday evening Bro. John Taylor, C.T., presided. The usual business was gone through. Two members were admitted to membership. Bro. Francis, St. Helens, D.S.J.T., gave a very inti3resting address, after which the programme for the evening was " The Chief Templar in a fix." The lodge deputy, Bro. W. Taylor, read the digest of the No. 1 convention to be held at Leigh OR Saturday, the 10th October. SOCIAL EVENING AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday night a number of Earlestown tradesmen and gentlemen had asocial dinner at the Wellington Hotel. Various gentlemen "gave the edibles, and an excellent dinner was served in the club room, about 30 sitting down. The after pro- ceedings were presided over by Mr. C. Chorlton, the vice chair being occupied by Ciuncillor W. Beatty. The usual loyal and patriotic toasts were given and duly honoured, and the health of the donors was en- thusiastically drunk. Songs and recitation were given by Messrs. G. Wright, Councillor Chadderton (Eccles), C. H. Lets, and R. Howley, (Eccles &amp;c.) ;GOOD TEMPLARY. The weekly session of the Earlestown Lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-operative Hall on Monday evening. Bro.A. Frances, D.S.J.T., of St. Helens, presided. Several reports were read by the Secretary. The digest of business of No.l Conven- tion to be held at Leigh, was read, and representa- tives were instructed to attend. The programme for the evening was "A night with the Convention officers," who were present from Widnes, St.Helens. afid Earlestown. Songs, recitations, readings, and addresses were given by the visitors and members of the lodge. WEDDING AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Monday at St. John's Church, Earlestown, Mr. Alfred Thorpe, second son of Mr. J. W. Thorpe of the Wellington Hotel, Earlestown, was married to Miss Louie Appleton eldest daughter of Mr. George Appleton of the Railway Hotel, Earlestown. The bridesmaids were Misses Appleton, sisters of the bride, the groomsman being Mr. James Thorpe. The wedding breakfast was held at the house of the bride's father there being a large number of guests. The newly married couple left during the after- noon for London where the honeymoon will be spent. The ceremony was performed by the Rev. E. W. W. Kaye. EARLESTOWN MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the Earlestown Musical Society, was held in the Co-operative Hall, on Monday evening. There was only a poor atten- dance of members. Mr. George Clarke having been voted to the chair, Mr. J. H. Profit, secretary, pre- sented a statement of accounts, which showed a de- ficit of £2 17s, The balance sheet was adopted and Mr. J. Harrison, and Mr. Birchall,were appointed to audit the accounts. The work for the ensiling session was considered and it was decided to give a rendition of the Messiah,"the week before Christ- mas, and to take up the study of Gaul's sacred can- tata "The Holy City." The election of officers resulted as follows :—President Mr.T. Ormandk con- ductor, Mr. E. Leyland; accompanist, Miss L. Bailey; treasurer,Dr. Valentine; committee: Messrs. Goslin, Barr, Mitchell, Griffiths, Jolley, Clarke, C. W. Emmett, J. Southern, Tomlinson, Atkinson, W. Williams, and Higham. Mr. Jesse Harrison, was appointed secretary, pro tem. ST. PETER'S CHURCH, NEWTON. _ _</p>
        <p>Services?of thanksgiving for the harvest were held in the above church en Sunday. The appeal issued to the parishioners by the'Vicar and wardens was as follows :—" Yet once more this harvest time has the congregation to be invited to discharge the bal- ance of debt for which the Building Committee for the recent enlargement of St Peter's Church con- tinues to be responsible. Since the like appeal was issued 12 months ago. an offer has been made and accepted of a sum sufficient to cover the cost of re- building the nave of the churei, as a memorial of the late Mr. McCorquodale, conformably with the portion completed three years ago. Since then the debt has been materially diminished,and there only now remains a balance of £9O due, which is surely not too much for the parishioners to defray out of gratitude for the gift of a nave about to be added free of cost to themselves." The church was very prettily decoratiA with fruit, flowers, corn, &amp;C., Mrs. Gillespie, Mrs. Rom, Miss Watkins, and other members ot the congregation having carried out the work of decorations. The anthem sung by the choir at the morning service was "0 praise the Lord, all ye his angels," and in the evening "0 give thanks unto the Lord." The morning sermon was preached by the Rev. W. J. Melville (rector of Ash- ton)and the evening by the Rev. A. G. Child,(chap- lain of the Liverpool Reformatory institutions). The collections during the day amounted to £24 6s. sd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S CHTJRCH,_ EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHTJRCH,_ EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The annual harvest thanksgiving services were held in the above church on Sunday. The building had been tastefully decorated with a profusion of cereals, fruit, and flowers, the work of decoration having been carried out by a good staff of lady members of the congregation. At the morning ser- vice on Sunday, when the Rev. H. J.Wadeson,rector of Croft was the preacher, the choir sang the anthem" I will feed my flock,' and in the evening, when the Rev. W. Williams, curate of Winwick, occupied the pulpit, the choirsang the anthem "Ye shall go out with joy," Miss Conway taking the solo in an efficient manner. In the afternoon the Earlestown brass band was present, and after a short service, played a selection of sacred music, and in the evening they accompanied the various hymns, also playing "The Better Land" and 1" Hallelujah Chorus.' The church was crowded at each service, especially in the evening, but the col- lections during the day, only amounted to a little over .Cl 4 in aid of the curate's stipend fund. On Monday evening an organ recital was given by the organist, Mrs. Latimer, A.R.C.0., with a selection of vocal music by members of the choir. The follow- ing was the programme submitted :—Anthem, "0 loird, bow manifold," choir; organ solo, Prelude and Fugue in G; solo and quartette, "Lord God of Abraham" and " Cast thy burden " ; organ solo, Andante; anthem, "The Lord is loving,' choir; organ solo,Minuetto ; solo, "Gentle,loving Saviour"; organ stilo, "Sicilian air " ; duet," 0 lovely peace:. organ solo, "ffertoire."—On Tuesday evening there was a short service, the preacher being the Rev.J.C. Stredder, St. Phillip's, Blackburn, and on Wednes- day evening a similar service was bold, the pulpit being occupied by the Rev. W. R. Hartwright, Orford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>ADJOURNED LICENSING SESSIONS AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>ADJOURNED LICENSING SESSIONS AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>These adjonrned sessions were held in the Town Hall, Newton. on Ssturda.y. The magistrates on the bench were: Mr. T. J. Gillespie (chairman), Colonel Wood, Messrs. R. Stone, C. B. F. Borron, and J. W. Emmett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>APPLICATIONS FOR AL I HRATIONS.</head>
        <p>APPLICATIONS FOR AL I HRATIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hopwood, of Wigan, made an application for per- mission to add to the Prince of Wales Hotel at Gal. borne. No additions would be made to the hotel itself, but it was intended to erect a stable, coachhouse, and to put a wall round. The application was eranted.—Mr. Stuart said he applied on behalf of Mr Foster for alterations to the Honghton Arms,Earlestown. It was intended to add a kitchen,alss to make additions to the bar and alterations to the bar parlour. The alter- ations would not increase the drinking facilities but (mid be a convenience to customers.—The magistrates granted the application.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD GOOD TF,MPLARS. A meeting of the Bonehill Branch Lodge of the Independent Order of Good Tempters was held in the Temperance Hall, on Monday evening. Bro. Councillor Ellison, 0.T., presided. There was a good attendance of members and visitors. The minutes'of the previous session and other business having been transacted, the Chairman invited Sister Hampton to the platform. A most interesting and instructive essay was given by her, the subject chosen being " How to make life happy from a female stand- point." At the conclusion, Bro. F. A. Jenkins moved a hearty vote of thanks to Sister J. Hampton for her essay. Bro. H. F:etcher seconded, and the motion was well supported by Bros. F. Blease, J. Ditchfield A. Ford, A. Hampton, Messrs. Roberts and O'Brien, each expressing their highest appreciation of the valuable efforts of Sister Hampton. The Chairman put the motion to the meeting, and it was unani- mously carried. RANOBs.—See the Herald Improved Lifting Fire Ranges.—Sole Agent, e Bridge-street.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>EXAMINERI SATURDAY, OCTOBER 3, 1396</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINERI SATURDAY, OCTOBER 3, 1396</p>
        <p>soft for any rough work they might come across when they went out into the world. Some of them, he be- lieved,might be sent abroad to the colouies,and speaking as one aho had spent five years in Canada, he assured them that if any of them went out there, and were well placed—as he had no doubt they would be—by those in charge of the school, and were well behaved, zheerful active, and ready to put their hands to anything, there was every opportunity for them to get on in the world. Referring to the excellent playgrounds which were at- tached to the school, he trusted that they played as Lan- cashire boys should do, and especially at football, and urged them to always play fairly and for their own side, as they ought to do in other departments of life. Let their acts,tie said, always be square and above board. (Applause.) His lordship then distributed prizes to the successful lads, after which a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to him,on the motion of Mr. R. Stone,seconded by Mr. Barton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>LOC AL RAILWAY COMPANY HEAVILY FINED.</head>
        <p>A LOC AL RAILWAY COMPANY HEAVILY FINED.</p>
        <p>At Newton Petty Sessions on Saturday, an important case was heard in which the Liverpool, St. Helens and South Lancashire Railway Company were charged at the instance of the Golborne Urban District Council with a contravention of the Railway Clauses Consolidation Act, 1845, and the special act authorising them to construct a railway, part of which lies in Golborne, sections 57 and 58 of which provided that the company should substi- tute a foot-bridge for a level crossing and also provide a fresh road for one cut through at Ashton-road,Golborne. —Mr. Ellis, solicitor, Wigan, prosecuted on behalf of the Cielborne Council. Mr. Johnson (Messrs. Oppenheim and Malkin, St. Helens), represented the railway com- pany and pleaded guilty to the charges. Mr. Ellis said in the year 1885 the St. Helens and Wigan Junction Railway Company obtained an Act of Parliament authorising them to construct a rail w ay. Part of the railway ran through the distiict under the charge of the Golborne District Council and crossed cer- tain roads for which the Council were responsible. The Golborne Authority obtained provisions in the Act pro- tecting their interests, and there were two sub-sections which effected the present case, stipulating that the bridge carrying the highway known as Ashton-road over the railway shall be constructed with approaches having a gradient not more than one in forty, and should be properly metalled, paved, and flagged. The Railway Clauses Act was incorporated in that Act of Parliament, section 58 of which provided that if the road interfered with could be restored in as good a condition as when the ,same was first interfered with by the company, and if suoh cannot be restored the railway company shall cause a new road to be put as near to the former road as circumstances would allow. Unless the trustees or parties having the management of the road consent to an extension of time, the time allowed for the work to be done was as follows, for a turn-pike road, within six months, other roads- within twelve months. Ashten road was not a turn. pike road and therefore the work should have been com- pleted within twelve months after it was interfered with. ISection 47 of the act provided that if roads be not res- tored within the periods fixed, the company should for- feit telthe authorities,if a public road, £5 for every day after the expiration of that period. Ashton-road was an important road in Golborne. It was inter- fered with by the company in the month of February, 1893 when they then commenced to build a bridge which was to carry it over the railway. They I made a temporary road while doing so. The road ought to have been restored in twelve months from February 1.893. The Company built the bridge, and threw together the soil necessary to make the ap- proaches to it but they had since taken very little trouble—in fact none at all—to make a road over which people could pass with safety. It was the duty of the Golborne Council to secure for the inhabitants the com- pletion of the road as soon as possible, and he thought the magistrates would come to the conclusion that the Council had tot been unduly hasty in thsir proceedings to get the road completed. On the 23rd of November. 1895—that was two years after the road was interfered with, and 12 months after the time that it ought to have been completed—the Surveyor to the Golborne Council wrote to the solicitors of the railway company—Messrs. Oppenheim and Malkin—stating that he was instructed by the Highways Committee to draw their attention to the great delay in the completion of the Ashton-road approaches, and to state that at the next ordinary meet- ing a resolution would be probably passed ordering pro- ceedings to be taksn. At this point of the proceedings, Mr. T. J. Gillespie (chairman) remarked that he was a shareholder in the I railway company in question, and Mr. R. Stone also stated that he was a shareholder in the St. Helens and Wigan Junction Railway.—Mr.Ellis said he had not the slightest objeetion to them sitting as magistrates. He hoped the railway would be a safe investment. continuing, Mr. Ellis proceeded to read the correspon- dence which had taken place between the Council and the Company. On the 3rd December the company's solicitors wrote asking for an interview with the mem- bers of the Council. The result of the interview was that the company made an agreement that the approaches , should be completed by the 31st May of the present year I and the footbridge should be erected at once. As a matter of fact the footbridge was not there now, and the correspondence showed that the company's solici- tors had invented a number of ingenious excuses , for not completing the work. They were now at the , end of September and the state ot things was pretty much what it was at the time the company made the un- , dertaking to complete the work. The present condition of the Ashten-road was this. The material which was to form the approaches of the road to the bridge was there and a few cinders had been put on the surface. It was in holes, and the rain had washed most of the cinders away. There was a considerable amount of water on the bridge and where it joioed the road there was a drop of some inches. It was a dangerous place, and Mr. Melling,of Ashton,in crossing it with his horse and cart met with an accident and his horse was seriously hurt. The Council had tried every possible way to avoid com- ing into court to get the work completed. When the summons was taken out offers were made that the work should be proceeded with, but he (Mr. Ellis) was instructed to press for the penalty to be enforced from the 31st of May when the work should have been completed according to the undertaking given by the company. The amount received by the Council would be applied in the first instance in payment of the expenses incurred by the Council ; secondly, in indemnifying the Council against any claims which !eight be made in consequence of the state of the road, and lastly, the balance would be given back to the company towards the work which they had undertaken to do. Mr. Bennie, surveyor, to the Golborne Council, and Mr. Melling, auctioneer, Ashton, gave evidence in sup- port of the Council's case. The Clerk (Mr. Greenall) asked the company's solicitor whether he was aware that the company had made themselves liable to a penalty of £595 on the first charge alone. Mr. Johnson said he had not reckoned it up but he knew it was a large amount. Mr. Stone inquired whether an undertaking was given with reference to the footbridge. Mr. Ellis said he intended to proceed with that now. In the act of Parliament it stated that the company should erect a footbridge over the railway four feet wide for the convenience to foot passengers. The agreement which was entered into applied to the footbridge, and it was promised that it should be put in hand at once. Mr. Emmett: Has the bridge been put up. . Mr. Ellis : There is no bridge there at all. Continuing, Mr. Ellis stated that the Council received a letter from Mr. Hopwood, of the Board of Trade, on the 6th July in answer to a communication, stating that the company had informed the Board that the footbridge would be erected and they had ordered it to be put in hand. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Bennie gava evidence with respect to the foot- bridge which he stated had not vet bees erected. Pas- sengers had to step over the rails. Mr. Ellis pointed out that the company were liable to a penalty of £5 a day in respect to this. For the defence, Mr. Johnson said the railway com- pany were sorry for the delay which had taken place. The work ha* now been taken in band by the contractor and would be completed as soon as possible. He under- stood that the contractor was working at both ends of the bridge and the approaches would soon be finished. Mr. Stone said the magistrates had considered the case and made an order against the company for £2O for not having completed the approaches ; £lO for not having completed the bridge, and ordered £1 a day in each case until the work was complete&amp; oir. Ellie said he was afraid the magistrates were ex- ceeding their authority. 'I he act said that the penalty should be £5 a day. The Clerk: That is the maximum. On reference being made to the clause in question.the Clerk said the magistrates would have to make an order for £5 a day. The magistrates retired and on their return, they im- posed a penalty of £5 a day from the 31st of May tr 3 the 25th of September for the non-completion of the road ; £5 a day for the same period in connection with the foot-bridge, and a penalty of £5 a day in each instance until the work is completed, and the costs. The amount due up to Saturday last was £1,190.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>PRIZE DAY AT NEWTON FARIt</head>
        <p>PRIZE DAY AT NEWTON FARIt</p>
        <p>SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The Lord Mayor of Liverpool (Earl Derby), who, be- sides being a permanent patron of the Liverpool Ju.venile Reformatory Association, is by virtue of his position as .Lord Mayor president of the association, on Friday journeyed to Newton-le-Willows, and visited the Firm and Industrial School in that locality. The farm school forms one of the three institutions to which children are relegated at the discretion of the stipendiary or other lay magistrates before whom they are brought at the Liverpool Police Court; and its work is similar in prin- ciple to that performed by the kindred icstitutions the training-ship Akbar and the Girls' Reformatory at Mount V,irrion-green. On the arrival of the Lord Mayor, the lads, who number about 150, welcomed him with cheers. Their neat appearance and orderly behaviour were distinctly noticeable, and speak well for the discip- line and strict training which they receive at the hands of Mr. J. Digweed, the superintendent, and the other officers and masters who assist in the control of the farm school. The Lord Mayor made a careful inspection of the various buildings which are comprised in the farm, and which include workshops for almost all classes of industry. Several gentlemen and a considerable num- ber of ladies resident in the neighbourhood also assemb- led to witness the proceedings, which were enlivened with the playing by the band of the school of several creditably-rendered selections of music, and the singing of the hymn "From all that dwell below the skies" and a song entitled "Off the Line." Previous to the distribution of the prizes, Lord Derby, addressing the lads, said that on inspecting the building he had noticed with pleasure that they were provided with every reasonable comfort, and at the same time were not made too tender or too</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>EARLESTOWN, NEWTON AND DISTRICT</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN, NEWTON AND DISTRICT</p>
        <p>FANCIERS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The first annual show of poultry, pigeon, and cage birds held under the auspices of the above newly formed association, took place on Saturday in the Assembly, Rooms, Legh-st., Earlestown, and was of a highly suc- cessful character. It must be very gratifying to the Committee to know that their first venture has met with such success, the applications for entry being so numer- ous that they had to refuse something like 500, and this fact has led them to decide upon bolding another show at Christmas. The staging of the competition was well carried out, and the attendance of the general public was so large as to cause much inconvenience. Alto- gether there were 180 entries in the various classes and as a whole the exhibits can be described as being of a very good order. The judge for poultry was Mr. Brad- ley of Hindley, and for cage birds, Mr. Dagmore of Widnes, their decisions giving every satisfaction. The following are the winners in the classes named :- Pouurne.-Class 1.-Leghorn cock: 1, A Widd (Newton Common) ;2, A Widd ;3, A Widd; the same having three others highly commended. Leghorn hen: 1, A Widd; 2, A Widd; 3, A Widd, and two highly commended. Class 4.-Minorca hen: 1, J Randall; 2, T Bishop; 3, J Randall. Class 6. - Plymouth Rock bee: 1, J Lythgoe; 2, J Lythgoe; 3, J Lythgoe; H Heyes very highly commended. Class 7.-Cochin or Brahma cock : 1, J Lythgoe ; 2. J Lythgoe ; 3, J Lythgoe. Class 8.-Cochin or Brahma hen : 1, J Lythgoe; 2, S Boardman ;3, S Boardman and highly commended. Class 9.-Orpington or Langshan cock: 1, 2 and 3, J. Randall. Class 10- Orpington or Langshan hen : 1. 2 and 3, J Randall. Class 12.-French hen, any variety : 1, F Wood; 2, F Wood ;3, J. Randall and highly commended Class 13. -Game cock, any variety : 1, F Wood ; 2, G Payne ; 3, H Hibbert. Class 14 -Game hen, any variety: I,J Lythgoe ; 2, F Bloor ;3, J Lythgoe ; H Hibbert, highly commended. Class 15.-Bantam cock,any variety : 1, S Dickens; 2, H Hibbert: 3, J Randall ; G Payne, commended. Class 16.-Bantam hen, any variety: 1, F Wood; 2, H Hibbert ;3, G. Payne. Class 17.-Any other variety cock 1, T Bishop ; 2, J Lythgoe ;3, F Wood. Class 19.-Selling class cock or hen, not to ex- ceod 103 : 1. R.Smith ; 2,R Smith;!3,S Boardman; J Ran- dall.very highly commended;F Wood ,highly commended. Class 20.-Any variety, duck or drake: 1, S Boardman; 2, S Boardman ; 3. S Boardman ; J Randall,commended. PIGEONS-Class 21: Best working homer cock: I,J Rylance, Newton ;2. E Burrows ; 3, S Boardman; E Burrows very highly commended ; F Hutchings and T Lythgoe, very highly commended. Class 22-Best work- ing homer hen : 1, F Bloor ;2, S Boardman; 3, T Lyth- goe ; E Burrows and S Boardman, very highly com- mended ; F Hallett. T Lythgoe and T Hutchings,highly commended. Class 23.-Any other variety cock or hen:l, T Lythgoe ;2, F Sharp; 3, F Hallett; F Sharp and r Hallett, very highly commended. CAOI4 BIRDB.-Ckss 24 : Clear yellow Yorkshire: 1, R Johnson ; 2, T.Hewitt ; :3, G Caldwell ; J Hales, very highly commended ; R Johnson, highly commended; T Hewitt, commended. Class 25.-Clear Buff Yorkshire: 1, R Johnson; 2, R Johnson ; 3, R Johnson ; and very highly commended ; J Appleton, highly commended, J Plant, commended. Class 26.-Ticked or unevenly marked yellow or Buff Yorkshire: 1, R Johnson ;2. R Johnson ; 3. G Caldwell; R Johnson, very highly com- mended ; T Hewitt, commended. Class 28.-Clear ticked or unevenly marked buff Norwich: 1, G Caldwell ; 2, R Johnson ; 3. R Johnson; J Clary, very highly commended Class 31.-Golden Lizards, clear or broken cap: 1, 2 and 3, Wfßray ; G Caldwell very highly commended ; W Bray, highly commended ; W Bray, commended. Class 32.-Silver Lizards, clear c r broken cap : 1, G Caldwell; 2, W Bray ;3, W Bray; and very highly commen ied. Class 33.-Selling class,bird and cage not to exceed 103.: 1, T Hewitt ;2, R Johnson ; 3, R Johnson; W Bray, very highly commended ; G Caldwell, highly com- mended. Class 34.-Any other variety of Canary or Mule : 1, G Caldwell ;2, W Bray ;3, G Caldwell ; and very highly commended. Class 35.-Any variety British Bird, large or small : 1, G Burgess ; 2, G Burgess; 3. R. Johnson; J Rylance, very highly commended F Sharp, highly commended. 28 special prizes were also given to the competitors who gained the highest num- ber of marks in all classes : 1, A Widd ; 2, R Johnson ; 3. W Bray ; 4 and 5, J Lythgoe and J Randall; 6, S. Boardman : 7. G Caldwell ; 8, F Wood; 9, E Burrows; 10, T Lythgoe; 11, F Sharpe; 12, T Hewitt; 13, F Bloor ; 14, T Bishop; 15. H Hibbert ; 16, G Payne ; 17, J Rylance; 18, F nutebieen; 10, It Romett ; 20, R Smith; 21, G Burgess ; 22, S Dickens ; 23, J Clary ; 24, J Bales; 25, H Hayes ; 26; J Plant ; 27, J Appleton, 28, J Bray.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>L. &amp;N.W. RAIL\V-AX COMPANY AND ST. JOHN AMBULANCE WORK.</head>
        <p>THE L. &amp;N.W. RAIL\V-AX COMPANY AND ST. JOHN AMBULANCE WORK.</p>
        <p>, COMPETITION AT EARLESTOWN- For some years past classes of instruction in first aid under the St. John Ambulance Association have been held througout the system of the L. and N.W. Railway Co., and the directors recognising the importance of the employes being able to render first aid to the injured in cases of accident, have formulated a series of competi- tions, amongst the men, the idea being also to create an interest in the work where none hitherto existed, a shield being offered for annual competition. For con- venience in carrying out the competition the system has been divided into centres, Earlestown being included in the Wigan centre. A committee has been appointed to carry out the arrangements in each district, the commit- tee repreeenting the Wigan centre being : Mr. T. Or- mond (Earlestown) chairman, wagon department ; Mr. W. Burgess,carriage department,Preston; Mr. J. Tarver, goods department,Wigan ; Mr G. W. Greaves, perman- ent way department ; Mr. G: Dingley, loco department; Mr. J. W. Green, traffie department, Wigan, acting as hon. sec. The conditions of the competition are that the classes be divided into teams of four men each, and where there are two or more teams at a station such teams compete amongst themselves, the winners in each case competing at the centre, the successful teams there being eligible to take part in the first competition of the whole system, the team winning the shield to be called the champion team of the year. A suitable medal will also be presented to each member of the winning dis- trict team, a silver medal to We members of the cham- pion team, and medals to the runners up. The competition for the Wigan centre took place on Wednesday afternoon in the Viaduct Institute recreation grounds, when five teams entered as follows : No. 1 team (Earlestown Wagon Works), No. 2 St. Helens, No. 3 Springs Branch, No. 4 Sutton Oak, No. 5 Warrington. The examiner was Dr. Hunter, M.A., B.Sc., M.D., Wigan, who put each team through a searching exam- ination, firstly in private, in theory and bandaging, and afterwards in the open ground, in stretcher drill, the time occupied in the examination being about 2 hours 45 minutes. Amongst those present were Mr. J. W. Emmett, superintendent, Mr. T. Ormand, Dr. Knowles, Dr. Handyside, and the district Committee. At the close Mr. Emmett proposed a vote of thanks to Dr. Hunter, and Mr. Ormand seconded, and it was heartily accorded. Mr. Emmett thanked the competitors for their attend- ance and hoped the winning team would go on and be- come the champions. A report was received on Thurs- day afternoon from the Examiner in which he states that No. 1 team (Earlestown) are the winners, they having shows the greatest knowledge of the subject, neatness in bandaging and best stretcher drill. Be does not give the respective positions of the teams, but we expect to give this later on. The members of the win- ning team are H. Jolley (captain), J. Green, F. Water- man, and H. Teece, and have been tutored by Dr. Handyside and Dr. Knowles to whom every credit is due.</p>
        <p>MUSIC IN THE PARK. On Sunday afternoon the Volanteer Band contributed the following selection of music which is the last to be played this season. March," Alannah " Vine ; selection, ",Latest Sacred Sow's" Ira D. Sankey; cornet solo," A child's letter to heaven," Levy ; grand selection, ":Reminiscences of Donizetti,". Smith ; selection, "The Creation," Haydn ; recit, "In sulendour bright" ; chorus, "A new created world"; solo, " The cattle in herds," solo " Now heaven in fullest glory shone," ; Largo "In rosy mantle,"; Ist chorus, "Achieved is the glorious work,"; solo " On thee each living soul awaits,"; 2nd chorus, " Achieved is the glorious work, " ; choru., " The heavens are telling, " ; "God save the Queen." There was a good attendance despite the inclemency of the weather,</p>
        <p>For NAV it BROOCHES, solid silver name brooches 101 d any name (Government stamped. Present•s or Articles of yourself. you may visit DE BERT'S. 10. CHURCH-ST e EFCT LIVERPOOL, where you can get hundreds of articles such as WatchPs, Clocks, Jewellery, Electro-Plate, Cut'erv,Purses Pocket Kn,ves, etc.. at wholesale prices. See advertisement (Illustrated Chronograph Watch, tis. 111., etc.), on page 7 ot this Paper.— tdvt.</p>
        <p>An interesting licensing case was heard on Wednesday at Chester. The Corporation some time ago sold the Old Green Dragon Hotel to the Bank of Liverpool, hut apparently intended to keep the:licence in force. The magistrites refused, however, at their annual meeting, to grant a licence for a new hotel in place of the old one and yesterday they refused applications to renew the licence to the city accountant or to transfer it to a re- presentative of the bank. An appeal is threatened.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>MIRRIAGE OF MAJOR</head>
        <p>MIRRIAGE OF MAJOR</p>
        <p>WALKER.</p>
        <p>FETE AT GATEACRE.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday,the marriage was solemnised at Frampton, near Dorchester, of Miss Sophia Sheridan, youngest daughter of Mr. A. B. Sheridan, of Frampton Cour;, to Major W. H. Walker, of the Grange, Gateacre, son of the late Sir A. B. Walker. The village of Frampton was gaily decorated. and floral arches were erected at the park gates and at the entrance to the church. Heavy rain fell in the morning, but at the hour fixed for the ceremony the weather was beautifully fine. The wed- ding excited an enormous amount of interest in the county, and a distinguished company of guests attended the service, which was conducted by the Rev. G. Turn- bull, rector of the parish, and the Rev. J. J. Sandy., vicar of Gateacre. The bridgegroom, who was accom- panied by Sir Peter Walker and Mr. L. Harcourt,arrived at the church a few minutes before two. The bride,who was attended by eight bridesmaids, was escorted to the church by her father, who rave her away. The service, which was preceeded by the Bridal March from "Lohen- grin," was fully choral, and at the close Mendelssohn's Wedding March was played. Among those who attended the ceremony were Sir William Harcdurt and Lady Har- court, uncle and aunt of the bride. The bride was attired in a handsome gown of rich ivory duzhesse satin, with full Court train of the same material, and draped with lovely old Brussels point lace, the gift of her mother. The bodice was of veiled chiffon, draped with old Brussels point lace, the deep sorslet being of satiniand the sleeves of satin and chiffon. She were a beautiful old Brussels lace veil over a coronet of real orange blossoms, caught by two diamond stars, the gift of her mother. Der ornaments consisted of a strihg of pearls and a diamond broach, the gifts of the bridegroom. The eight bridesmaids were Miss L. Walker Miss Hylda Paget, Miss Hester Kennard, Miss Laura Chetwode, Miss Mabel Tombs, Miss Isabel Thynne, Miss Violet Bampfylde, and Miss Evelyn Court. Each was attired in a gown of rich white satin,a bodice of chiffon, deep Empire belts, crossed under the left arm, of mir- roired apple-green velvet, and fastened by old diamond buttons set in silver, fichus of chiffon edged with lovely old lace caught by Tiolets,long ruched transparent sleeves and large white picture felt hats,droopine over the right side of the face, trimmed with a handsome bird of paralise. These young ladies carried posies of violets, and wore as ornaments diamond geese, typical of Michaelmas Day. Mrs.Sheridan,the bride's mother,wore a very choice gown of grey and white silk trimmed with old lace and blue chiffon, fastened by sable and old dia- mond buttons. She carried a bouquet of glorie Dion see. Mrs.Rylance Court, the sister of the bridegroom, were a gown of grey chine silk, a corsage of green silk richly embroidered, and a large hat. The Hon. Mrs. Bampfylde's gown was of mauve and white mirrior silk, trimmed with pale blue satin and old paste buttons, and she carried a mauve sunshade to correspond, with gold handle encrusted with rare jewels. After the service, a reception was held at Frampten Court,when there was a large gathering of local gentry, tenantry, and others. In the course of the afternoon, Major and Mrs. Walker left for London on their way to Paris and Venice. _ _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>The bride's travelling gown was of grey faced cloth, the boler• richly embroidered in white over a bodice of an equisite shade of blue. She wore a large grey felt hat to matchea beautiful bird of paradise nestling on the brim. Included in the bride's trousseau were a ball gown of black net over satin richly embroidered in bur- nished steel; a pink silk lisse ball gown embroidered with straw and silk ; a white satin gown draped with chiffon and lace, turquoise velvet belt with La France roses and Neapolitan violets at the waist; a white figured chine silk dinner skirt with dainty bodice of white lisse, trimmed with ?dechPn lace and a two-coloured belt; a pale blue net ball gown, spangled with silver and trimmed with trails of button roses of palest pink, with belt of two shades of rose silk ; a theatre or dinner gown of black moire velonrs skirt and black chiffon bodice ; a Marie Antoinette fichu of black satin and cream guipure ; a black moire velours or era or earriage cloak lined with Russian sable : a long blue astrachan driving coat,the collar extending over the shoulders to a point at the waist,with cuffs to match; an opera cloak of violet velvet.the collar and front being of ermine ; beau- tiful millinery, muffs, &amp;c. The lingerie was of finest French lawn trimmed with real Valeneienne, the bride's monogram being embroidered on all the articles. The entire trousseau, together with the bridesmaids gowns and hats,was designed and built by Madame Josephine Hayhurst, of Old liturlington-street, London. The wedaing guests staying at Frampton Court in- cluded, in addition to the sight bridesmaids. Mrs. Court, Sir Peter Walker, the Hon. Coplestone and Mrs. Ramp- fvlde, Sir William and Lady Harcourt, Colonel and Mrs. St. John Mildmay. Lord and Lady Poltimore, Mr. Chris- tian Eliot, Mr. L. Harcourt. and Mrs. Charles Duff. Major Walker's gifts tie his bride were exceedingly numerous and costly, and included a diamond tiara and diamond brooch to snatch, diamond riviere, diamond sun, pearl and turquoise collar, diamond and turquoise comb, diamond heart, single row of pearle,rope of pearls, diamond watch and bow, diamond ornament forming three brooches, diamond butterfly, turquoise and dia- mond curb bracelet, two diamond bracelets together forming a necklace. five rines—aaunbire. rtaby. and two diamond ; diamond pendant, large pearl form- ing a dragon set with diamonds, large pearl forming a mermaid set with diamonds, black fan, hand-painted, the sticks set with diamonds; eld point lace fan, large white feather fan, black and white lace fan, several diamond brooches and ornaments, a beautiful cloak of best Russian sable, and a Humber bicycle. Appended is a list of some of the presents to the bride:— Mr and Mrs Julian Sturgis. silver tea caddy; Lady and Miss Chetwode. sleeve links ; Miss Hilda Paget, old ehins plaque; Miss M. Chandos Pole, pair alive, trays ; Mr W Caleroft, tortoiseshell and silver box; Mr T Thynne, two silver inlaid trays ; Miss Madeline Stanley, silver and glass scent bottle; Mr and Mrs T Grant, two heart-shaped pearl and enamel brooches; Miss Hester Kennard, diamond butterfly; Mr W Beaumont, old silver box; Lord and Lady Digby. white feather fan ; Mr H Compton, photo frame; Mrs Leopold Seymour. fan and silver salver ; Mrs G Abuthnot. old Em- pire candlestick ; lion Mrs Bailie, fan; Mr and Mrs F Ben- tinek. Honiton lace fan ; C•untess of Amhurst. large photo frame ;Miss Beott,old Battersea enamel box;Earl and Countess of Bathurst, en-taut-ens; Mr Bertte Clay, carriage clock ; Mr and Mrs Percy Laming, gold and torquoi se broolhes ; Mr Christian Eliot, red enamel heart and chain ; Eon C and Mrs Bampfyide, pearl and diamond horseshoe; Sir Peter Walker, diamond heart bracelet; Mr Ran Grant. pearl and enamel bangle; Mr Lenten Harcourt. Ruskin' Works and despatch boa; Mrs A Handbury, silver pen Hon Frances Wereeley, fen : Hen H and Mrs old tray; Mrs Charles Duff, diamond and tur- quoise bracelet; Mr A B Sheridan, diamond bracelet - Mrs tray: Bourke. Mrs Sheridan old Brussels lace; Countass of Antrim and Miss Berta Corry, old brass boat ; Lady I Duly Sybil M'Honnel, silver bowl; Mr Howard Sturgis, ; inkstand and candlesticks ; Miss Monica y table inlaid ed ieo ot t , Thynne, revolving bookcase; Sir Edward Stracey, silver grandfather's clock;. Mr and Mrs C Van Raalte, gold and sliamond scent bottle ; Hon Mrs Evans, china Vase; Mr Ward Cook, inlaid box : Lady Uirica Thynne. Longfellow's Works; Misses Hansen. silver stoehorn ; Lady D'Arey Osborne. Rites " Life of Sheridan;" Hon. J W Harris. Bmerso ,'s Works ; Miss Bramston, blue enamel pin; Miss Alhasen, book ; Lady Gwendoline Ramsden fan; Mr Ralph Bankes, pair of silver shells ; Mr R Eliot, silver purse ;.Lady Hermione Graham, revolving b eakcase and Lowell's wiems ; Captain Balfour, gold and glass scent bottle; Mr and Mrs Winthrop. old amulet pendant; Hon. Mary Sackville. silver cream jug ; Mr Haynes-Smith, opera glasses ; Mr and Mrs St Maur, silver cream jug; Miss Ethel .15;ope, old silver cup; Sr Hubert Miller, Pair of con salt cellars ; Mrs George Burroughs, gold hand looking-glass; Mr and Mrs D Thornton. silver-box ; Colonel and Mrs Williams, silvc: bowl; Mrs J Fellowes, oak chest; Mr Claud Beddineten, cream jug ; Mr and Mrs Frampton, ivory pen tray; Miss Callender. Flemish lace handkerchief ; Hon. Elaine Guest, perse ; Colonel and Mrs Mildmay, opal and diamond bracelet; Mr and Mrs Munro Walker, pearl and diamond brooch; Miss Lisette Walker, diamond brooch; Mr Alhu- sen. carriage clock; Miss May Burns. tortoiseshell and gold paper knife; Mr and Mrs J Hargreaves, pair of silver candlesticks ; Miss Featherstonhaugh, silver pin cushion ; Hon. Maurice Glya, Chippendale tea tray ; Miss Amy Williams, silver and glass bell ; Captain Goulbourn, pair of silver candlesticks ; Mr and Mrs H Frampton. silver embossed box; Mr C Wyide, silver shamrock box; Mr and Mrs Darcy Taylor. silver box ; Mad'lle Gamier, silver frame ; the Poet Laureate, " In Veronica's Garden ; " Mr and Mrs Horace Hutchinson. Dresden china box; Mr S Hughes Onstow, bezique box; Misses M and T Thynne, engraving of " St Ceci- lia ; " Mr R Worthington, silver jug ; Miss and Mas- ters Worthington, hunting crop; Miss Digby and Mr Digby, hunting crop; Miss Kers. old china box ; Miss Verona Harcourt, isandkerchief sachet; Mr J Oilliat, pepper and salt hunting horn castors ; Rev and Mrs Hunt. brass candlesticks; Mrs Tudor, clock ; Miss T Digby. sliver basket; Miss F Williams, set of apostle spoons ; Miss Mabel Tombs, glass and silver bell ; Captain Forster. silver smelling bottle ; Miss Well's Christiana Rosetti's poems; Mr Arthur Kennard, gold- fitted manicure box ; Mr and Mrs Milburne. gold box ; Hon Mrs Williams, brass vases ; Hon Thomas Egerton. silver pepper pot ; Lady Evelyn Sutton, Tennysere's Works ; Mrs R Court, silver kettle and lamp stand ; Mr Fox-Pitt, silver ring box ; Sir Robert and Lady Edgeeumbe, old Worcestertea caddy ; Rev sa and Mrs Turnbull, silver embossed tray; Major and Mrs. Sykes, lace fan; Lady Blanche Bailie, urn- bells; Lord and Lady Wunborne, gold-backed brushes and hand-glass ; Sir William and Lady Harcourt, diamond orna- ment; lord ant Lady Paltimore, engravings. The bridegroom was also the recipient of many pre- sents, the list comprising : Mr Hamilton Pelly, sovereign purse ; Colonel and Mrs Stanley Arnold, Worcester vases; Hon Assheton Curzon. silver-mounted umbrella ; Captain Beever, R.A., surer pepper boxes; Earl and Countess of Harrington, silver inkstand; Mr and Mrs H B teson, silver and ivory paper knife; Mr S J Phillips. silver salver and cream ug ; Mr J H Meredith, silver candlesticks; Mr Adrian Jones. The Soarer inkstand, silver ; Mr Walter 3 H Jones, tortoise-shell and gold box; Mr and Mrs Arthur Dugdale, pair pillars and vases ; Mr George Melly, pair of candlesticks ; Miss Ruth Okeover, silver frame; Mr and Mrs G H Kellock, oyster forks ; Mr David G M Campbell. The Soarer paper knife :