      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>GREAT CELEBRATIONS AT GA.TEACRE.</head>
        <p>GREAT CELEBRATIONS AT GA.TEACRE.</p>
        <p>VISIT OF WARRINGTONIANS.</p>
        <p>Rarely, if ever, has Gateacre Grange presented such an animated appearance as it did on Thursday when a fete and gala were held there in honour of the marriage of Major William Hall Walker with Miss Sophie Sheridan. The celebrations were at- tended by those, engaged by the firm at Birming- ham, Burton-on-Trent, Leicester, Liverpool, Man- chester, Prescot, St. Helens, 'Uttoxeter, Widnes, and last but not least—Warrington. For the convey- ance of the large party from Warrington three special trains were chartered, and at 10.40 the first train of excursionists steamed out of the Central Station. The two other trains followed shortly afterwards. Amongst the company was the band of the Warrington Volunteers conducted by Mr. Thomas Armstrong. On arrival at Gateacre the band led the way to the Park playing the while several lively tunes. From the grounds a good view of the surrounding country is obtained, and the mansion which is to be the residence of the newly-married couple presents a substantial rather than an imposing appearance. In one of the large windows a portion of the wedding presents was displayed inclu,ling those from Warrington. The illuminated address presented with the gifts from the Warrington eniplosiis was as follows :—"To Major William Hall Walker. Dear Sir, —On behalf of the Employs of Peter Walker and Son, Warrington and Burton, Limited, we desire to express to you their most cordial and hearty con- gratulations upon your approaching marriage. We also beg your acceptance of the accompanying gift which we tender to you as a token of their high respect and esteem, and as a memento of the aus- picious occasion. With sincerest wishes for the long life, happiness, and continued prosperity of yourself and bride." The names of the committee were afterwards appended. For the enjoyment of the visitors, who must have totalled nearly two thousand, numerous attractions had been provided and the onerous duty of arranging them had fallen upon the following gentlemen : General Committee: Messrs. P. Wright (ehaiiman), J. A. Greaves, Ernest Ellis, James Monro, W. Jam's, J. Fulton Smith, E. G. Slatter, A. E. Benson. Sports Com- mittee : Messrs. J. Fulton Smith, Jno. Baird, J. Crad- dock, A. Potter, P. Weds, T. C. Brooks, C. Holland, W. James, S. J. Rowe', F. Jenkins, R. H. Jones, J. Adams, J. Parish, A. Smith, Jno. Glover, Jas. Brown, Jno. Brown, Edward Brown, and E. Heater. Stewards : Messrs. A. T. Neilson, R. B. Neilson, W. F. Neilson, P. T. Stolterfoht, J. W. Irvine, W. J. Lockett, and G. H. Pilkinaton. Mr. Ernest Ellis discharged the duties of secretary.</p>
        <p>THE LUNCHEONS, A:Nn ToAsTe.</p>
        <p>The first paiTy-tcTiilie- hin-Ch in the large tent which had been provided sat down at 11.30, and did full justice to the viands provided by Messrs. Galt and Capper, caterers, of Liverpool. Grace was said by the Rev. J. J. Sandes, vicar of Gateacre. Amongs': those present were: Mr. James Monro, manager of the Burton Brewery, Mr. A. E. Benson, Mr. J. C. Weston, Mr. J. H. Whittaker also of Burton, Mr. Robert Molyneux, agent of Leicester, Mr. J. A. Greaves, (general manager of Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons Limited), Mr. J. Fulton Smith, Coun- cillor J. Baird, Councillor W. J. Shaw, Messrs. Craddock Potter, Pilkington and others of Warrington. At the 1 conclusion of the repast,Mr. James Monro in a few felici- tous remarks proposed the toast of the health of Her Majesty the Queen. The toast was drunk with enthu- siasm, and the audience afterwards sang " God Save the Queen," Mr. Armstrong playing the accompaniment on his cornet. Mr. Monro then proposed the toast of the I afternoon, via: "The health of Major and Mrs. Walker." Ihe toast was drunk, and the audience heartily joined in the singing" For he's a jolly good fellow." Similar toasts were drunk by the guests at the other sittings. Mr Greaves, ef Warrington, proposing the health of the Queen at the second sitting, referred to the pleasure which it gave them to live in this the greatest and long- est reign of any rnenarch. It was not necessary to enter into any particulars respecting that reign, but he hoped Her Majesty the Queen might continue to reign for many years to come. (Applause.) After drinking this toast, Mr. lireaves proposed the toast of Major and Mrs. Walker. He said .nearly all there had known Major Walker ever since he was a boyl and he was certain they had always found that he gave good sound judgment in matters which he took in hand. They would not expect him to dilate on the many excellencies and the character of Major Walker, but he would now ask them to drink his health, and Major Walker's response to the toast would be eiven by the aid of the phonogrrph. (Applause.) —Mr. J. Fulton Smith then delivered the message which Major Walker bad spoken into the phonograph. It ran as tollows :—" Mo tern invention has given me the op-- portunity of addressing a few words of welcome to those whom I have much pleasure in entertaining to-day. It would have afforded me great satisfaction to have been with you and amongst you on this occasion but as may be readily understood my presence is in somewhat even greater request .elsewhere. (Laughter.) If this instru- ment could enable me to see.as well as to speak,E should recognise many old faces; faces of those who have been with me since my business career commenced and in some instances for many years before. Men whose long and honourable service in the firm will ever stand as a noble example to others who are younger and who may be called upon to fill the same high position in time to come. I have every reason to believe that I have been fortunate in winning the affections of a, lady who will prove a wise, loving and a true friend—a lady who will, I am sure, ever associate herself in the inter- ests I have at heart in and about my old home. E.L.st ins take this opportunity of personally thanking you tor the I magnificent presents which you in your good nature have been so generous to bestow upon my wife and my- self. They will ever afford me a lasting token of the I approval which I feel proud t 3 possess and of all those whose good opinions I envy most. With many,, many thanks, and hoping that this day will be one of true happiness to all I give you my hearty thanks and best wishes." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>TUE AMXISEMENTS.</head>
        <p>TUE AMXISEMENTS.</p>
        <p>The visitors were now free to roam through the conser- vatories and vineries in the !rounds adjoining the Grange, and this privilege was taken advantage of to the full. The fine eollection of plants and the well-kept vines were greatly admired. Mr. T. Birtles, of Warrington, photo- graphed groups of visitors in front of the hall. Owing to the fitful showers which fell during the day dancing on the lan n was not engaged in to any great extent. With much forethouaht a pavilion had been provided for dancing which was kept up to the strains of the Warring- ton Volunteer Brass Band,and Mr. B. Rowlands' orchestra. Quite a different amusement was that provided by Wallis's patent revolving galloping horses but neverthe- less they were frequen'ly mounted with a host of riders. From 2.30 until T. 30 ,grand variety entertainments were provided at intervals in a pavilion. Mr. J. Kiernan, of the Tivoli Palace,Liverpool,was in charge of the arrange- ments and he is to be congratulated upon the excellence of the entertainments provided. They commenced with a grand triumphal wedding march dedicated to Major aad Mrs. Walker on the occasion of their wedding. Artistes of first class merit in their several departments were en- gaged, and the entertainments, whieh were free—as in fact everything was—were largely attended. Quite a number of audiences were also delighted with a ventri- loquial and sleight-of-hand entertainment which was to be witnessed at intervals in a small marquee. Professor St. Clair was the entertainer and he was exceedingly clever in his necromantic and ventriloquial eccentrici- ties. A phonograph was also included in tiv attractions of the day, and many took the advantage of the o'gpor- tunity to hear the reproduction of Major Walker's voice in the speech which he had delivered. At night there was a grand pyrotechmic display cayti.ecleiiki,y Messrs, James Pain and Sons of London,</p>
        <p>REVISING THE LISTS.</p>
