      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>GRAPPENII ALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENII ALL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Clement Elphinstone Lloyd, banister at law, attended in the large room at Grappenball, on Tuesday, for the purpose of revising the lists of voters in the Grappenhall polling district, which comprises the town' ships of Grappenhall,Latchford,and Latchford Without, The Liberals were representerl by Mr.Josoph Siasith,and the Conservatives by Mr. James H. Dean and Mr. B. J' Longtcn. Mr. S. J. Woodcock, assistant overseer for Grappenhall, and Mr. J. Smith, representing Mr. J. L- Tunstall (assistant overseer for Warrington), were ale° in attendance. These were the only persons present,and the proceedings were of the usual routine character. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>DARESBURY.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY.</p>
        <p>The revision of the lists of voters in the following townships in the Knutsford division was held on TuesdaY afternoon :—Acton Grange, Daresbury, Hatton, Kelt&amp; wick, Moore. Newton-by-Daresbury, Preston-'oth-Hill, Walton Inferior, Walton Superior,Antrobus,Saven Oaks, Whitley Inferiortand Whitley Superior. Mr. C. Elphin- stone Lloyd was the revising barrister. The LiberalS were represented by Mr. Joseph Smith.of Knutsford, and Mr. Dean on behalf of Mr. Lance Bentley, estate agent, appeared an behalf of the Conservatives.—Mr. Jcseph' Norcott, of Antrobus, made a claim fora vote for three cottages and land. He said be had been the owner since 1887. The Liberals objected.and in answer to isquirieS• by the Liberal agent he said it was formerly his wife'S property but she had given it to him. The agent pointed' out that the applicant's wife was already on division three.—ln answer to further inquiries,the applicant said he had been married eleven or twelve years. He pro' duced the deeds of the bouses,and the Revising Barrister' after inspecting them said the applicant's wife might have given him the houses but it was not a legal gift. She could not give her husband houses in the same way as a horse or chattels. There was noting to show that he was the legal owner of the property. The claiiu could not be allowed.—Mr. Seeker claimed a lodger vote in the township of Moore. In answer to the Revising B'arrister, the applicant said be had the sole use of a bedro3m which he had occupied for twelve months from the 15th July. Be 4 paid £26 a year. He had a right to use the sitting' room with the family. Ile lodged at Moore Hall which was assessed at i.sa.—The Assistant Oversecr said the house and gardens were assessed at £BO.—.y Mr. Jos. Smith : What are you P—Estate agent.—An ii do you pay this £26 a year for the use of a bedroom ?-I(cs.,--- Doesn't it include attendance ?-orily that for making the bed.—The vote was allowed.—A, the ennclusicn the court, Mr. Dean proposed a vote of thanks to the Revising Ba.rrister for the manner in which he had re- vised the lists of the division —Mr. smith s4id nu be.' half of the Knutsferd Division Liberal Association be had pleasure in seconding the resolution. He wished to thank the Revising Barrister for the great patience and •courtesy and fairness with which he had conducted the revision. He (Mr. Smith) flattered himself that the next register would be more complete than the present one. As agents and overseers they ought to endeavour to make the register as perfect a register as possible. The Revising Barrister said he was much obliged for the kind remarks which had been made, and he thanked the agents present for the great assistance he had received from both of them.</p>
        <p>A tneetin. of the Joint Committeo of the workmen in the iron trade in the Manchester district was held 00 Tuesday night, at which it was reported that the ballot had shown that /12,543 men were in favonr of a strike for an advance of wages and 272 against. Unless, therefore, the advance of 2 per week is eranted, a strike will be commenced on Saturday. Nearly 8,000 men are affected:</p>
        <p>At Leeds on Wednesday, a Halifax mill girl named Rose Emma Ambler was awarded £5O damages against Edwin Harrison of Eland, an ex-soldier and ex-police• man, for breach of promise of marriage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>MA RKETS.</head>
