        <p>TUE EXAMINER. SATURDAY, OCTOBER 3rd, 1896.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The fortnightly meeting of this Board was held on ruesday in the Boardroom at Dutton Workhouse. There were present—Mr. J. Knowles (chairman), the Rev. Canon Wood, the Rev. Dr. Gasdaldi,the Rev.J. Simpson, Messrs. A. R. Norman, T. Lea, A. S. Yarwood,W.Ashton (clerk), G. Ashton (deputy clerk), and the members of the Rural District Council. THE LADIES' COMMITTEE. The Master reported that this committee had found everything in perfect ordar,and had promised at a future date, to consider the moans of providing enjoyment for the inmates at Christmas time.—Mr. Heywood moved that the Ladies' Visiting Committee be increased from nine to twelve members. At the time the committee was appointed, there was a scarcity of ladies.—Dr. Gastaldi seconded the motion, and said it was a move in the right direction.—it was then resolved that Mrs. Har- greaves, of Frodsham, be successor to Mrs. Renton (resigned), The additional ladies chosen, were Mrs. Ralph Milner, Frodsham ; Mrs. Whitley, Budworth ; and Mrs. Posnett, Runcorn. THE RECENT FIRE. Mr. Stokes,Chairman of the House Committee,said the committee appointed to inquire into the matter of pro- viding provision, in case of fire at the Workhouse, had not yet met. He recommended that the Master be in- structed to obtain specifications for an iron staircase, as a means of escape in case of another outbreak. VACCINATION. The Local Government Board wrote approving of the appointment of Mr. Thomas Cooke, relieving officer for the Runcorn registration sub-district,with a fee of sd.in respect of each case of successful vaccination registered by him. • THE CONTRACT'S. . The following tenders were accepted :—Drugs,Messrs. Speakman,Runcora ; potatoes, G. Kinsay,Dutton ; cart- ing, W. Grounds, Aston ; bacon, J. Nicholls, Frodsham ; butchers' ineat,G.lio wcock, Warrington ; coal,J.Barrow, Halton, and Biram and Co., St. Helens; conveyances, llooth, Runcorn ; boots, J. Bazley, Runcorn ; groceries, E.Gregory,Runcorn ; drapery and clothing,Fletcher and Lee, Runcorn and Warrington. FINANCE COMMITTEE. Mr. Heywood submitted the report of this committee, which showed that the out-relief of the various districts for the pat fortnight was—Frod sham £2B and Budworth £22. The balance in hand was £2,407. He moved the adoption of the report.—Mr. Woodward se- conded.—The motion was carried. This concluded the business.</p>
